    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1620",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An employer and employee had an employment contract that contained an \"at will\" provision allowing either party to terminate the employment upon reasonable notice. One year later, the employer had the employee sign a covenant not to compete for a two-year period after termination. Two years later, the employee quit and went to work for a competitor company. The prior employer sued, asking for a preliminary injunction to enforce the covenant. The employee argued that the covenant was invalid because no consideration passed to her  she could be fired at any time and did not receive anything for signing. The employer argued that it provided her with continued employment when it did not have to do so. Does the employer have a legal right to enforce the covenant?\n\nOptions: \nA. Yes, because the employer's providing of continued employment for a substantial period after signing the covenant is adequate consideration.\nB. Yes, because modification of an existing contract does not require consideration.\nC. Yes, because the courts never question the adequacy of consideration in an agreement.\nD. No, because there was inadequate consideration for the covenant.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The core issue here is whether the covenant not to compete was supported by valid consideration. In many jurisdictions, continued employment can be sufficient consideration for a new agreement, even if the original contract was at-will. The employer's promise to keep the employee working for a period after signing the covenant, rather than exercising their at-will right to terminate immediately, can be seen as a benefit to the employee, thus constituting consideration.\n\nThe most likely correct answer is **A**. Continued employment, especially for a substantial period, is generally considered valid consideration for a new agreement like a covenant not to compete, even in an at-will employment context. Options B and C are incorrect legal principles. Option D is incorrect because continued employment is typically seen as adequate consideration in such situations.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
