{a}}_{{\\,\\text{{nine}}}}$ to be a three-digit number in base nine, $b \\neq 0.$\n\nSo the equation is $99a = 70b + 8c,$ and $a \\in \\{1, ..., 8\\}, b \\in \\{1, ..., 8\\}, c \\in \\{0, ..., 8\\}.$\nThis is exactly what I have been using.\n\nLet's check the case when $a=3.$\nThe modular relation: $3a \\equiv 6b \\pmod{8}.$\nIf $a=3,$ $9 \\equiv 6b \\pmod{8}.$ $1 \\equiv 6b \\pmod{8}.$\n$6b = 1 + 8k.$\nIf $k=0, 6b=1.$ No.\nIf $k=1, 6b=9.$ No.\nIf $k=-1, 6b=-7.$ No.\nThis confirms that $a=3$ is not possible.\n\nLet's assume that the question implies that $a,b,c$ are digits in base 10 for the first number, and $b,c,a$ are digits in base 9 for the second number. But this makes the problem much harder as the interpretation of $a,b,c$ changes. This is not a typical phrasing.\n\nUsually, when a notation like $\\underline{{a}}\\,\\underline{{b}}\\,\\underline{{c}}$ is used, the digits refer to the base of the number next to it.\nSo, if it's $\\underline{{a}}\\,\\underline{{b}}\\,\\underline{{c}},$ it's base 10. If it's $\\underline{{b}}\\,\\underline{{c}}\\,\\underline{{a}}.$ nine,$ it's base nine.\n\nLet's revisit the example solution 817, assuming it's the correct number.\n$a=8, b=1, c=7.$\n$a$ is the hundreds digit in base 10.\n$b$ is the tens digit in base 10.\n$c$ is the units digit in base 10.\nSo the number is $817.$\n\nIn base nine, the number is $\\underline{{b}}\\,\\underline{{c}}\\,\\underline{{a}}.$\nSo the digits in base nine are $b=1, c=7, a=8.$\nThis means the base nine representation is $178_9.$\nLet's convert $178_9$ to base 10:\n$1 \\cdot 9^2 + 7 \\cdot 9^1 + 8 \\cdot 9^0 = 1 \\cdot 81 + 7 \\cdot 9 + 8 \\cdot 1 = 81 + 63 + 8 = 152.$\nSo, $817_{10} \\neq 152_{10}.$\nThis confirms 817 is not the solution if my interpretation of the problem is correct.\n\nLet's assume the problem is from a competition or textbook. There must be a solution.\nWhat if the digits $a,b,c$ used in $\\underline{{a}}\\,\\underline{{b}}\\,\\underline{{c}}$ are the same $a,b,c$ as in