    "global index": "AIME_75",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Among the 900 residents of Aimeville, there are 195 who own a diamond ring, 367 who own a set of golf clubs, and 562 who own a garden spade. In addition, each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts. There are 437 residents who own exactly two of these things, and 234 residents who own exactly three of these things. Find the number of residents of Aimeville who own all four of these things.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let D be the set of residents who own a diamond ring, G be the set of residents who own a set of golf clubs, S be the set of residents who own a garden spade, and C be the set of residents who own a bag of candy hearts. We are given the following information:\nTotal residents = 900\n|D| = 195\n|G| = 367\n|S| = 562\n|C| = 900 (since each resident owns a bag of candy hearts, C is the set of all residents)\n\nLet R be the total number of residents, so R = 900.\nThe number of residents who own exactly two of the first three items (D, G, S) is 437.\nThe number of residents who own exactly three of the first three items (D, G, S) is 234.\n\nWe are looking for the number of residents who own all four things, which is $|D \\cap G \\cap S \\cap C|$. Since |C| = 900, which is the total number of residents, owning a bag of candy hearts does not distinguish any subset of residents from the total. Therefore, we are looking for the number of residents who own all three of the first items, i.e., $|D \\cap G \\cap S|$.\n\nLet's denote the number of residents who own exactly one of D, G, S as $N_1$, exactly two of D, G, S as $N_2$, and exactly three of D, G, S as $N_3$.\nWe are given that the number of residents who own exactly two of *these things* is 437. Since the question mentions \"among the 900 residents... In addition, each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts\", we should interpret \"these things\" in the context of the first three items. The phrasing \"exactly two of these things\" refers to combinations of Diamond Ring, Golf Clubs, and Garden Spade. Thus, we are given that the number of residents who own exactly two of D, G, S is 437.\nThis means $|(D \\cap G) \\setminus S| + |(D \\cap S) \\setminus G| + |(G \\cap S) \\setminus D| = 437$.\n\nWe are also given that the number of residents who own exactly three of *these things* is 234. This refers to owning all three of D, G, S. Thus, $|D \\cap G \\cap S| = 234$.\n\nNow consider the total number of residents who own at least one of D, G, S.\nLet |D only| be the number of residents who own only a diamond ring.\nLet |G only| be the number of residents who own only a set of golf clubs.\nLet |S only| be the number of residents who own only a garden spade.\nLet |D and G only| denote residents owning D and G but not S.\nLet |D and S only| denote residents owning D and S but not G.\nLet |G and S only| denote residents owning G and S but not D.\nLet |D and G and S| denote residents owning D, G, and S.\n\nWe are given:\n$|D \\cap G \\cap S| = 234$. This is the number of residents who own exactly three of D, G, S.\n\nWe are given that 437 residents own exactly two of *these things*. This implies:\n$|(D \\cap G) \\setminus S| + |(D \\cap S) \\setminus G| + |(G \\cap S) \\setminus D| = 437$.\n\nLet's consider the total number of residents in the union of D, G, and S.\nUsing the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion,\n$|D \\cup G \\cup S| = |D| + |G| + |S| - (|D \\cap G| + |D \\cap S| + |G \\cap S|) + |D \\cap G \\cap S|$.\n\nWe know the number of residents who own exactly one item, exactly two items, and exactly three items in terms of the items D, G, and S.\nLet $N_1$ be the number of residents owning exactly one of D, G, S.\nLet $N_2$ be the number of residents owning exactly two of D, G, S. We are given $N_2 = 437$.\nLet $N_3$ be the number of residents owning exactly three of D, G, S. We are given $N_3 = 234$.\n\nThe total number of residents is 900. Everyone owns a bag of candy hearts. The question asks for the number of residents who own all four of these things. Since everyone owns a bag of candy hearts, this is equivalent to finding the number of residents who own a diamond ring, a set of golf clubs, and a garden spade. This is $|D \\cap G \\cap S|$.\n\nWe are given that 234 residents own exactly three of these things. The \"these things\" refers to the diamond ring, set of golf clubs, and garden spade. So, the number of residents who own exactly the diamond ring, set of golf clubs, and garden spade is 234. Since everyone owns a bag of candy hearts, these 234 residents also own the bag of candy hearts. Therefore, the number of residents who own all four of these things is 234.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{{234}}$.",
