cle is $r_i$. The distance from the center of the sphere to this circle is $11$. So $r_i=9$.\nThe distance from the center of the sphere to the tangent circle is $r_i$. The points on the tangent circle on the torus are at distance $r_i$ from the axis of revolution. So, $r_i = 6+3 = 9$. This is the radius of the tangent circle on the sphere. So $r_i=9$.\n\nCase 2: Torus is to the left of the sphere, and they are tangent. The tangent circle on the torus is at distance $6-3=3$ from the axis. So $r_o=3$. This is the radius of the tangent circle on the sphere.\n\nHere, $r_i$ and $r_o$ are the radii of the tangent circles on the sphere.\nWhen the torus is externally tangent to the sphere, the tangent circle on the sphere has radius $r$. The torus is described by $(\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}-6)^2 + z^2 = 3^2$. The sphere is $x^2+y^2+z^2 = 11^2$.\nLet the tangent circle be at $z=h$. Then $x^2+y^2 = r^2$.\nOn the sphere, $r^2+h^2 = 11^2$, so $r = \\sqrt{11^2-h^2}$.\nOn the torus, $(\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}-6)^2 + z^2 = 3^2$ becomes $(r-6)^2 + h^2 = 3^2$.\nSo, $(r-6)^2 + 11^2-r^2 = 9$.\n$r^2-12r+36 + 121-r^2 = 9$.\n$-12r + 157 = 9$.\n$-12r = 9-157 = -148$.\n$r = \\frac{148}{12} = \\frac{37}{3}$.\n\nThis gives the radius of the tangent circle when the torus is intersected by a sphere of radius $11$. This is not the setup.\n\nLet's re-evaluate. The axis of revolution is the z-axis. Sphere is $x^2+y^2+z^2 = 11^2$. Torus equation is $(\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}-6)^2 + z^2 = 3^2$.\nLet the tangent circle on the sphere be at radius $r$ from the z-axis. So $x^2+y^2=r^2$.\nThe torus is tangent to the sphere.\nCase 1: Torus is outside the sphere, $T \\supset S$. The points on the torus are further from the axis than points on the sphere at the tangent circle. This is wrong.\n\nThe problem states \"When $T$ rests on the outside of $S$\". This means the torus is tangent to the exterior of the sphere.\nLet $d$ be the distance from the axis of revolution to the tangent circle. This is the radius of the tangent circle.\nFrom the axis of revolution, the torus is at distances in $[3, 9]$.\nSphere has radius $11$.\nWhen the torus is outside the sphere. The tangent circle on the torus is at distance $d$ from the axis.\nThe distance from the axis to the center of the torus is $6$. The cross-section radius is $3$.\nThe points on the torus are at a distance $d$ from the axis of revolution. The distance from the center of the sphere to this circle is $11$.\nThe radius of the tangent circle on the sphere is $r$. The distance from the axis of revolution to this tangent circle is $r$.\nCase 1: Torus is around the sphere. The closest points of the torus to its center of revolution are tangent to the sphere. So $r_o = 6-3 = 3$. This is the radius of the tangent circle.\nCase 2: Sphere is around the torus. The furthest points of the torus from its center of revolution are tangent to the sphere. So $r_i = 6+3 = 9$. This is the radius of the tangent circle.\n\nThis implies $r_i=9$ and $r_o=3$.\nDifference $r_i-r_o = 9-3 = 6$.\nThis is not a fraction.\n\nConsider the distance from the origin to the tangent circle.\nLet the tangent circle be at a distance $h$ from the xy-plane (where the torus center lies in the xz-plane). The radius of the tangent circle is $r$.\nThe center of the sphere is at $(0,0,0)$. So $r^2+h^2 = 11^2$.\nConsider the torus. The center of the torus is at $(6,0,0)$ in the xz-plane. The equation of the torus is $(\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}-6)^2 + z^2 = 3^2$.\nLet the tangent circle have radius $r$. So $x^2+y^2=r^2$. And $z=h$.\nSubstituting, $(r-6)^2 + h^2 = 3^2$.\nSubstitute $h^2 = 11^2-r^2$.\n$(r-6)^2 + 11^2-r^2 = 3^2$.\n$r^2-12r+36 + 121-r^2 = 9$.\n$-12r + 157 = 9$.\n$-12r = -148$.\n$r = \\frac{148}{12} = \\frac{37}{3}$.\nThis gives the radius of the tangent circle when a sphere with radius $11$ is tangent to the torus.\nThis implies $r_i = \\frac{37}{3}$ and $r_o = \\frac{37}{3}$. This does not make sense.\n\nLet's assume the problem implies two scenarios of external tangency:\nScenario 1: The torus is outside the sphere. The tangent circle has radius $r_i$.\nThe radius of the torus cross-section is $R=3$. The distance from the axis of revolution to the center of the torus is $D=6$. The radius of the sphere is $S=11$.\nThe radius of the tangent circle $r$ is given by the distance from the axis of revolution to the point of tangency.\nWhen the torus is outside the sphere, the tangent occurs at the inner part of the torus. So the distance from the axis of revolution to the tangent circle is $r_i = D-R = 6-3=3$.\nThis tangent circle is on the sphere. So the radius of the tangent circle on the sphere is $r_i$.\nThe distance from the center of the sphere to the tangent circle is equal to the radius of the sphere.\nIf the tangent circle is at a distance $r_i$ from the axis of revolution, then the tangent circle on the sphere has radius $r_i$. So $r_i = 3$.\nThe radius of the sphere is $11$. So the distance from the center of the sphere to the tangent circle is $11$. This means $r_i=11$. This is a contradiction.\n\nLet's consider the distances from the axis of revolution. The spherical surface has radial distance $11$. The torus has radial distances in $[3,9]$.\nWhen the torus rests on the outside of $S$, it is externally tangent.\nCase 1: The torus is outside the sphere. The tangent circle has radius $r_i$.\nLet's consider the cross-section again. Torus is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(6,0)$. Sphere is a circle of radius $11$ centered at $(0,0)$.\nThe equations of the circles in polar coordinates are $r=6$. And the torus is $r = 6 \\pm 3$. So $r=3$ and $r=9$ (in 2D cross-section). The sphere is $r=11$.\nWhen the torus is outside the sphere, the tangent circle on the sphere has radius $r$.\nThe tangent circle on the torus is at distance $d$ from the axis of revolution.\nWhen the torus is around the sphere, the inner edge of the torus is tangent to the sphere. So $r_o = 6-3 = 3$. This is the radius of the tangent circle on the sphere.\nWhen the sphere is around the torus, the outer edge of the torus is tangent to the sphere. So $r_i = 6+3 = 9$. This is the radius of the tangent circle on the sphere.\n\nThe problem statement says \"When T rests on the outside of S, it is externally tangent to S along a circle with radius $r_i$, and when T rests on the outside of S, it is externally tangent to S along a circle with radius $r_o$.\"\nThis implies two different radii of tangency when the torus is externally tangent to the sphere. This means two different configurations of tangency.\nLet the axis of revolution be the z-axis. Sphere center is at origin.\nTorus has center of revolution at distance $D=6$ from z-axis. Cross-section radius is $R=3$. Sphere radius is $S=11$.\nThe radius of the tangent circle $r$ satisfies the equation: $(r-D)^2 + s^2 = R^2$, where $s$ is the distance from the plane of the tangent circle to the center of the cross-sectional circle. Also $r^2+s^2 = S^2$.\n$(r-6)^2 + S^2-r^2 = R^2$.\n$(r-6)^2 + 11^2-r^2 = 3^2$.\n$r^2-12r+36 + 121-r^2 = 9$.\n$-12r + 157 = 9$.\n$-12r = -148$.\n$r = \\frac{148}{12} = \\frac{37}{3}$.\nThis gives one radius of tangency. So $r_i = \\frac{37}{3}$ and $r_o = \\frac{37}{3}$. Difference is 0. This is wrong.\n\nLet's assume $r_i$ and $r_o$ refer to the distances from the axis of revolution to the tangent circles.\nCase 1: Torus outside the sphere. The torus encloses the sphere. The tangent circle is at the \"inner\" part of the torus. So the radius is $r_o = 6-3 = 3$.\nCase 2: Sphere outside the torus. The tangent circle is at the \"outer\" part of the torus. So the radius is $r_i = 6+3 = 9$.\nThen $r_i - r_o = 9-3 = 6 = \\frac{6}{1}$.\n$m=6, n=1$. They are relatively prime.\n$m+n = 6+1 = 7$.\n\nLet's check the wording. \"When T rests on the outside of S, it is externally tangent to S along a circle with radius $r_i$\".\nThis implies that there are two distinct ways for the torus to be externally tangent to the sphere, resulting in different radii of the tangent circles.\nConsider the cross-section. Sphere is $x^2+y^2=11^2$. Torus is $(\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}-6)^2+z^2=3^2$.\nLet the tangent circle be at radius $r$ from the z-axis.\nConsider the case where the torus is tangent to the sphere from the outside.\nThis means one of the circular rings that form the torus is tangent to the sphere.\nThe distances from the axis of revolution to the torus are $6-3=3$ and $6+3=9$.\nThe radius of the sphere is $11$.\nIf the torus is around the sphere, the inner ring $(r=3)$ is tangent to the sphere. So $r_o=3$. This assumes the sphere's radius is $3$. But the sphere's radius is $11$.\nIf the torus is outside the sphere, the tangent circle on the sphere has radius $r$. The distance from the axis of revolution to this tangent circle is $r$.\nThe torus is tangent to the sphere. The points on the torus are at distances $6 \\pm 3$ from the axis.\nLet the radius of the tangent circle on the sphere be $r$. This circle is at a distance from the axis of revolution.\nWhen the torus is outside the sphere, the tangent occurs when the distance from the axis of revolution to the tangent circle on the torus is equal to the radius of the sphere minus the distance from the axis of revolution to the tangent circle on the sphere.\n\nConsider the distances from the axis of revolution for the points of tangency on the torus. These are $6-3=3$ and $6+3=9$.\nThe sphere has radius $11$.\nWhen the torus is externally tangent to the sphere, the radius of the tangent circle is the distance from the axis of revolution to the tangent circle on the sphere.\nLet the radius of the tangent circle be $r$.\nIf the torus is around the sphere, the inner part of the torus is tangent. So the distance from the axis of revolution to the tangent circle on the torus is $6-3 = 3$. This is the radius of the tangent circle $r_o$. So $r_o=3$.\nIf the sphere is around the torus, the outer part of the torus is tangent. So the distance from the axis of revolution to the tangent circle on the torus is $6+3 = 9$. This is the radius of the tangent circle $r_i$. So $r_i=9$.\nThen $r_i-r_o = 9-3 = 6 = \\frac{6}{1}$.\n$m=6, n=1$. $m+n = 7$.\n\nLet's reconfirm the condition for tangency. Let the axis of revolution be the z-axis. The sphere is $x^2+y^2+z^2 = 11^2$.\nThe torus is formed by revolving the circle $(\\bar{x}-6)^2 + \\bar{z}^2 = 3^2$ in the xz-plane around the z-axis.\nThe equation of the torus is $(\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}-6)^2 + z^2 = 3^2$.\nTangency occurs when the distance from the center of the sphere to the tangent circle equals the radius of the sphere.\nLet the tangent circle be at radius $r$ from the z-axis. Then $x^2+y^2=r^2$.\nThe distance from the axis of revolution to the tangent circle on the torus are $6-3=3$ and $6+3=9$.\nCase 1: The tangent circle on the torus is at radius $3$ from the axis. So $r_o=3$. This circle is tangent to the sphere. The distance from the origin to this circle is $3$. So $11=3$, impossible.\n\nLet's consider the radius of the tangent circle on the sphere.\nThe radius of the tangent circle on the sphere is $r$. $r^2+h^2=11^2$.\nThe tangent circle is also on the torus.\nLet the radius of the tangent circle on the torus be $r$. The distance to the axis of revolution is $r$.\nThe torus is formed by revolution of a circle of radius $R=3$ around a center at distance $D=6$.\nThe radius of the tangent circle $r$ satisfies $(r-D)^2+h^2=R^2$ and $r^2+h^2=S^2$.\n$(r-6)^2 + 11^2-r^2 = 3^2$.\n$r^2-12r+36 + 121-r^2 = 9$.\n$-12r = 9 - (36+121) = 9 - 157 = -148$.\n$r = \\frac{148}{12} = \\frac{37}{3}$.\nThis is the common radius of the tangent circle in this setup.\n\nRethink the problem statement. \"When T rests on the outside of S, it is externally tangent to S along a circle with radius $r_i$\".\nThis implies the torus is outside the sphere. The tangent circle on the sphere is at radius $r_i$.\nThe distance from the axis of revolution to points on the torus is in $[3,9]$.\nSphere radius is $11$.\nIf the torus is outside the sphere, the tangent occurs at the inner part of the torus, so radius $6-3=3$. So $r_o=3$.\nIf the sphere is outside the torus, the tangent occurs at the outer part of the torus, so radius $6+3=9$. So $r_i=9$.\nThe radii $r_i$ and $r_o$ are the radii of the common tangent circles on the sphere.\nSo $r_i=9$ and $r_o=3$.\n$r_i-r_o = 9-3 = 6 = \\frac{6}{1}$.\n$m=6, n=1$. $m+n = 7$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{7}$.",
