    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let $w_{ij}$ and $b_{ij}$ be indicator variables such that $w_{ij}=1$ if cell $(i,j)$ contains a white chip and $w_{ij}=0$ otherwise, and $b_{ij}=1$ if cell $(i,j)$ contains a black chip and $b_{ij}=0$ otherwise. The condition that each cell contains at most one chip means that for each $(i,j)$, $w_{ij}+b_{ij} \\le 1$.\nThe condition that all chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same color means that for any row $i$, either all chips are white or all chips are black. Similarly, for any column $j$, either all chips are white or all chips are black. This implies that if cell $(i,j)$ has a white chip, then all chips in row $i$ are white, and all chips in column $j$ are white. If cell $(i,j)$ has a black chip, then all chips in row $i$ are black, and all chips in column $j$ are black.\n\nLet $R_i$ be the color of row $i$, and $C_j$ be the color of column $j$. Then the condition implies:\nIf $w_{ij}=1$, then $R_i$ is white and $C_j$ is white.\nIf $b_{ij}=1$, then $R_i$ is black and $C_j$ is black.\nSo, if cell $(i,j)$ contains a chip, its color is determined by the color of its row and column. If $R_i = C_j$, then cell $(i,j)$ can contain a chip of that color. If $R_i \\ne C_j$, then cell $(i,j)$ cannot contain a chip.\nSuppose there are $i_w$ rows that are all white and $i_b$ rows that are all black, where $i_w+i_b=5$.\nSuppose there are $j_w$ columns that are all white and $j_b$ columns that are all black, where $j_w+j_b=5$.\n\nLet's analyze the color of the chips in the grid. If a row is white, all cells in that row must be white. If a column is white, all cells in that column must be white. If a row is black, all cells in that row must be black. If a column is black, all cells in that column must be black.\nIf a cell $(i,j)$ contains a white chip, then row $i$ must be white, and column $j$ must be white.\nIf a cell $(i,j)$ contains a black chip, then row $i$ must be black, and column $j$ must be black.\n\nConsider the case where all cells contain chips. Then each row and each column must have the same color. However, we have both white and black chips, and a $5 \\times 5$ grid cannot be filled with only one color.\nIf row $i$ is white, all cells $(i,j)$ for $j=1,\\dots,5$ are white. If column $j$ is white, all cells $(i,j)$ for $i=1,\\dots,5$ are white.\nIf row $i$ is black, all cells $(i,j)$ for $j=1,\\dots,5$ are black. If column $j$ is black, all cells $(i,j)$ for $i=1,\\dots,5$ are black.\n\nThe third condition states that any additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions. This implies we cannot add another chip while maintaining the conditions.\nLet $W$ be the set of rows that are all white, and $B$ be the set of rows that are all black. Let $W_c$ be the set of columns that are all white, and $B_c$ be the set of columns that are all black.\nIf cell $(i,j)$ has a white chip, then row $i \\in W$ and column $j \\in W_c$.\nIf cell $(i,j)$ has a black chip, then row $i \\in B$ and column $j \\in B_c$.\n\nThere are four configurations of row and column colorings that are possible.\n1. All rows are white, all columns are white. This means all cells are white. We can place at most 25 white chips. If we place 25 white chips, we cannot add any more chips. This would fill the entire grid with white chips.\n2. All rows are black, all columns are black. This means all cells are black. We can place at most 25 black chips. If we place 25 black chips, we cannot add any more chips. This would fill the entire grid with black chips.\n3. Some rows are white, some are black. Some columns are white, some are black.\nLet $k$ be the number of white rows, so $5-k$ is the number of black rows.\nLet $m$ be the number of white columns, so $5-m$ is the number of black columns.\nFor a cell $(i,j)$ to contain a white chip, row $i$ must be white and column $j$ must be white. So, there are $k \\times m$ possible locations for white chips.\nFor a cell $(i,j)$ to contain a black chip, row $i$ must be black and column $j$ must be black. So, there are $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$ possible locations for black chips.\nThe total number of chips is $k \\times m + (5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nThe third condition implies that we have placed the maximum possible number of chips under the given constraints. This means either $k \\times m + (5-k) \\times (5-m) < 25$ and we cannot add any more chips while respecting the conditions, or we have filled some cells and cannot add more.\n\nConsider the case where we have $k$ white rows and $m$ white columns.\nThe number of white cells is the number of intersections of white rows and white columns, which is $k \\times m$.\nThe number of black cells is the number of intersections of black rows and black columns, which is $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nA cell $(i,j)$ where row $i$ is white and column $j$ is black cannot contain a chip.\nA cell $(i,j)$ where row $i$ is black and column $j$ is white cannot contain a chip.\nThe third condition implies that all cells where a chip can be placed are occupied.\nSo, the number of white chips is $k \\times m$, and the number of black chips is $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nThe total number of chips is $k \\times m + (5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nWe have 25 white chips and 25 black chips available.\nWe need to find the number of ways to choose the sets of white rows, black rows, white columns, and black columns such that the total number of chips placed is at most 25 white and at most 25 black, and we cannot add any more chips. Since the condition states that any additional chip would violate the rules, it means we have filled all the possible locations for chips.\n\nLet $k$ be the number of white rows and $m$ be the number of white columns.\nThe number of white chips is $k \\times m$. The number of black chips is $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nThe total number of chips is $k \\times m + (5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nWe are given that we can have at most 25 white chips and at most 25 black chips.\nThis means $k \\times m \\le 25$ and $(5-k) \\times (5-m) \\le 25$.\nSince all possible locations for chips are filled, we need to find the number of pairs $(k,m)$ such that $0 \\le k,m \\le 5$ and the number of chips is maximized given the color constraints.\nWe need to consider the cases where we have filled all available cells for a certain color.\n\nConsider the final configuration. Suppose there are $k$ white rows and $m$ white columns.\nThe number of white chips is $k \\times m$.\nThe number of black chips is $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nTotal number of chips = $k \\times m + (5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nThe condition that we cannot place any additional chip means that all cells where a chip can be placed are occupied.\nWe have two cases:\n1. We use only white chips. Then all rows must be white, and all columns must be white. This means $k=5$ and $m=5$. Number of white chips = $5 \\times 5 = 25$. Number of black chips = 0. This fills the grid with white chips.\n2. We use only black chips. Then all rows must be black, and all columns must be black. This means $k=0$ and $m=0$. Number of white chips = 0. Number of black chips = $5 \\times 5 = 25$. This fills the grid with black chips.\n\n3. We use both white and black chips.\nThe number of white chips is $k \\times m$. The number of black chips is $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nWe have 25 white chips and 25 black chips.\nThis implies that we must fill all possible locations for white chips and all possible locations for black chips.\nSo, $k \\times m = 25$ and $(5-k) \\times (5-m) = 25$.\nFrom $k \\times m = 25$, the possible integer pairs $(k,m)$ with $0 \\le k,m \\le 5$ are $(5,5)$.\nIf $(k,m) = (5,5)$, then $5-k=0$ and $5-m=0$. Then $(5-k)(5-m) = 0 \\times 0 = 0$.\nNumber of white chips is 25. Number of black chips is 0. This is case 1.\n\nLet's reconsider the problem statement. We have 25 white and 25 black chips.\nThe conditions are:\n1. Each cell contains at most one chip.\n2. All chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same color.\n3. Any additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions.\n\nThis means that if we have a configuration of chips, all cells that are allowed to have a chip according to condition 2 must be filled, up to the limit of 25 white and 25 black chips.\n\nLet $R_W$ be the set of white rows, $R_B$ be the set of black rows. $|R_W|+|R_B|=5$.\nLet $C_W$ be the set of white columns, $C_B$ be the set of black columns. $|C_W|+|C_B|=5$.\nNumber of white chips = $|R_W| \\times |C_W|$.\nNumber of black chips = $|R_B| \\times |C_B|$.\nTotal number of chips = $|R_W| \\times |C_W| + |R_B| \\times |C_B|$.\n\nThe third condition means that we cannot add any chip. This means that all cells $(i,j)$ where row $i$ and column $j$ have the same color must be filled.\n\nCase 1: All rows are white, all columns are white.\n$|R_W|=5$, $|R_B|=0$. $|C_W|=5$, $|C_B|=0$.\nNumber of white chips = $5 \\times 5 = 25$.\nNumber of black chips = $0 \\times 0 = 0$.\nThis uses 25 white chips and 0 black chips. We cannot place any additional chip. This is a valid configuration.\n\nCase 2: All rows are black, all columns are black.\n$|R_W|=0$, $|R_B|=5$. $|C_W|=0$, $|C_B|=5$.\nNumber of white chips = $0 \\times 0 = 0$.\nNumber of black chips = $5 \\times 5 = 25$.\nThis uses 0 white chips and 25 black chips. We cannot place any additional chip. This is a valid configuration.\n\nCase 3: Let $|R_W|=k$, $|R_B|=5-k$, $|C_W|=m$, $|C_B|=5-m$.\nNumber of white chips = $k \\times m$.\nNumber of black chips = $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nWe have 25 white chips and 25 black chips.\nThis means $k \\times m \\le 25$ and $(5-k) \\times (5-m) \\le 25$.\nAnd all possible positions are filled.\nSo we need to find integer pairs $(k,m)$ with $0 \\le k,m \\le 5$ such that $k \\times m + (5-k) \\times (5-m)$ is the total number of chips.\n\nConsider the configuration where we have $k$ white rows and $m$ white columns.\nThe number of white chips is $k \\times m$.\nThe number of black chips is $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nWe have 25 white chips and 25 black chips.\nThe third condition means that we have filled all possible locations for chips.\nThis means we have $k \\times m$ pairs of intersecting white rows and white columns, and $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$ pairs of intersecting black rows and black columns.\n\nWe are looking for the number of ways to assign colors to rows and columns.\nThere are $\\binom{5}{k}$ ways to choose $k$ white rows.\nThere are $\\binom{5}{m}$ ways to choose $m$ white columns.\n\nThe total number of ways to have $k$ white rows and $m$ white columns is $\\binom{5}{k} \\binom{5}{m}$.\nThe number of white chips is $k \\times m$. The number of black chips is $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$.\nWe have 25 white chips and 25 black chips.\nSo we must have $k \\times m = 25$ and $(5-k) \\times (5-m) = 25$.\nIf $k \\times m = 25$, then $(k,m)$ can be $(5,5)$. In this case, $5-k=0, 5-m=0$, so $(5-k)(5-m)=0 \\ne 25$.\nThis means we cannot have separate regions for white and black chips that are both fully populated with 25 chips.\n\nLet's consider the structure of the occupied cells. They form a shape such that if we take any cell $(i,j)$, and row $i$ contains a white chip, then all cells $(i,l)$ for $l=1,\\dots,5$ are white. Similarly for columns.\nThis implies that the set of occupied cells must be the union of some number of all-white rows and some number of all-white columns, and the union of some number of all-black rows and some number of all-black columns.\n\nThe only way to satisfy the conditions with both white and black chips being present is if we can partition the grid into a $k \\times m$ rectangle of white chips and a $(5-k) \\times (5-m)$ rectangle of black chips, where all other cells are empty.\nThis implies that the set of white chips forms a subgrid and the set of black chips forms a subgrid.\n\nLet's consider the possibilities for the number of white rows $k$ and white columns $m$.\n\nPossibility 1: All cells ar