is divisible by $p^2$.\nSo $p=17$. We need to find the least positive integer $m$ such that $m^4+1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{17^2}$.\nWe know $n^4 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p}$. Thus $n^8 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$. The order of $n$ mod $p$ is 8. So $8 | (p-1)$. $p \\equiv 1 \\pmod 8$. The least prime is $p=17$.\nWe need to solve $m^4+1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{17^2}$.\nThere exist solutions to $x^4 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p^2}$ when $p \\equiv 1 \\pmod 8$.\nWe need to solve $m^2 \\equiv \\pm i \\pmod{17^2}$.\nThe values of $n$ modulo $17^2$ such that $n^4 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{17^2}$ are $\\pm 303, \\pm 516 \\pmod{289}$.\nSo we are seeking the least positive integer $m$ among $303, 516, ...$\nThe least positive integer $m$ such that $m^4+1$ is divisible by $17^2$ is $m=303$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{{303}}$.",
