    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the given condition be $n+2 | 3(n+3)(n^2+9)$.\nWe can rewrite $n+3$ as $(n+2)+1$ and $n^2+9$ as $(n^2-4)+13 = (n-2)(n+2)+13$.\nSubstituting these into the expression, we get:\n$n+2 | 3((n+2)+1)(((n-2)(n+2)+13))$\n$n+2 | 3((n+2)(n-2)(n+2) + 13(n+2) + (n-2)(n+2) + 13)$\n$n+2 | 3((n+2)(n-2)(n+2) + 13(n+2) + (n-2)(n+2) + 13)$\nSince $n+2$ divides the first three terms, we must have $n+2$ divides $3 \\times 13 = 39$.\nSo, $n+2$ must be a divisor of 39.\nThe divisors of 39 are $\\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 13, \\pm 39$.\nSince $n$ is a positive integer, $n \\ge 1$, so $n+2 \\ge 3$.\nTherefore, the possible values for $n+2$ are $3, 13, 39$.\nIf $n+2 = 3$, then $n = 1$.\nIf $n+2 = 13$, then $n = 11$.\nIf $n+2 = 39$, then $n = 37$.\nWe need to check if these values of $n$ satisfy the original condition.\n\nFor $n=1$: $n+2 = 3$, $3(n+3)(n^2+9) = 3(4)(10) = 120$. $3|120$. So $n=1$ is a solution.\nFor $n=11$: $n+2 = 13$, $3(n+3)(n^2+9) = 3(14)(121+9) = 3(14)(130) = 42 \\times 130$.\n$13 | 42 \\times 130$ since $13 | 130$. So $n=11$ is a solution.\nFor $n=37$: $n+2 = 39$, $3(n+3)(n^2+9) = 3(40)(37^2+9) = 120(1369+9) = 120(1378)$.\n$39 | 120(1378)$ since $39 = 3 \\times 13$.\n$3 | 120$ and $13|1378$ since $1378 = 1300 + 78 = 1300 + 13 \\times 6$. So $n=37$ is a solution.\n\nThe positive integers $n$ are $1, 11, 37$.\nThe sum of these integers is $1 + 11 + 37 = 49$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{49}$.",
