lds for $n=3$ and $n=4$.\nFor $n=3$, we check $23^{2^{3-2}} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{{2^{3+1}}}$, i.e., $23^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$. $529 = 33 \\times 16 + 1$. True.\nFor $n=4$, we check $23^{2^{4-2}} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{{2^{4+1}}}$, i.e., $23^4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{32}$. $(23^2)^2 = 529^2 \\equiv 17^2 = 289 = 9 \\times 32 + 1$. So $23^4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{32}$. True.\nFor $n=5$, we check $23^{2^{5-2}} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{{2^{5+1}}}$, i.e., $23^8 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{64}$.\nWe found $23^{-1} \\pmod{32} = 7$.\n$23 \\times 7 = 161$.\n$161 = 2 \\times 64 + 33$. So $23 \\times 7 \\not\\equiv 1 \\pmod{64}$.\n\nThe condition for $a_n = a_{n+1}$ is that $23^{2^{n-2}} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2^{n+1}}$ for $n \\ge 3$.\nThis holds when $n=3$ and $n=4$.\nWe need to check if there are any other solutions.\nThe statement that the order of $23 \\pmod{{2^k}}$ is $2^{k-1}$ for $k \\ge 3$ is correct.\nWe need $23^{2^{n-2}} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{{2^{n+1}}}$.\nThis implies that $2^{n-1}$ divides $2^{n-2}$, which is impossible.\n\nLet's trust the direct computation: $n=3$ and $n=4$.\nThe number of positive integers $n$ less than or equal to $1000$ is requested.\nThe solutions are $n=3$ and $n=4$.\nThere are exactly $2$ such integers.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{{2}}$.",
