    "global index": "AsDiv_843",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: Three cleaners, A, B, and C are cleaning the windows of a building. Person A cleans a window every 8 min, person B every 12 min and person C every 14 min. If they start cleaning three windows at the same time at 14:15,\n\nQuestion: at what time will start cleaning windows at the same time again?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find when they will start cleaning windows at the same time again, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their individual cleaning times.\nThe cleaning times are 8 minutes, 12 minutes, and 14 minutes.\nFirst, we find the prime factorization of each number:\n$8 = 2^3$\n$12 = 2^2 \\times 3$\n$14 = 2 \\times 7$\n\nTo find the LCM, we take the highest power of each prime factor present in the factorizations:\n$LCM(8, 12, 14) = 2^3 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 8 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 24 \\times 7 = 168$\n\nSo, they will start cleaning windows at the same time again after 168 minutes.\nNow we need to convert 168 minutes into hours and minutes.\n$168 \\text{ minutes} = 2 \\text{ hours and } 48 \\text{ minutes}$ (since $168 \\div 60 = 2$ with a remainder of $48$).\n\nThey started cleaning at 14:15. We add 2 hours and 48 minutes to this time:\n$14:15 + 2 \\text{ hours} = 16:15$\n$16:15 + 48 \\text{ minutes} = 17:03$\n\nTherefore, they will start cleaning windows at the same time again at 17:03.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{{17:03}}$",
