  {
    "global index": "AsDiv_2086",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: Fabric is sold in stores from bolts that are 45 or 60 inches wide.\n\nQuestion: What is the width of the widest strips of fabric you can cut from either bolt without wasting any of the fabric if each strip has the same width?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem asks for the widest strips of fabric that can be cut from bolts of 45 or 60 inches wide without wasting any fabric, such that all strips have the same width. This means we are looking for the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 45 and 60.\n\nStep 1: Find the prime factorization of 45.\n$45 = 3 \\times 15 = 3 \\times 3 \\times 5 = 3^2 \\times 5$\n\nStep 2: Find the prime factorization of 60.\n$60 = 2 \\times 30 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 15 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 5 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\times 5$\n\nStep 3: Identify the common prime factors and their lowest powers.\nThe common prime factors are 3 and 5.\nThe lowest power of 3 is $3^1$ (from $3^1$ in 60 and $3^2$ in 45).\nThe lowest power of 5 is $5^1$ (from $5^1$ in 45 and $5^1$ in 60).\n\nStep 4: Multiply the common prime factors with their lowest powers to find the GCD.\n$GCD(45, 60) = 3^1 \\times 5^1 = 3 \\times 5 = 15$\n\nTherefore, the widest strips of fabric you can cut from either bolt without wasting any of the fabric if each strip has the same width is 15 inches.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{{15}}$",
      "success": true,
