      "generated_answer": "Let $x$ be the amount invested at 12% simple interest and $y$ be the amount invested at 10% simple interest.\nThe annual interest received is \\$130, so we have the equation:\n$0.12x + 0.10y = 130$ (1)\n\nIf the amounts were interchanged, the person would have invested $y$ at 12% and $x$ at 10%. The interest received would be \\$4 more, which is $130 + 4 = 134$. So, we have the equation:\n$0.12y + 0.10x = 134$ (2)\n\nNow we have a system of two linear equations:\n1) $0.12x + 0.10y = 130$\n2) $0.10x + 0.12y = 134$\n\nMultiply equation (1) by 100 to remove decimals:\n$12x + 10y = 13000$ (3)\nMultiply equation (2) by 100 to remove decimals:\n$10x + 12y = 13400$ (4)\n\nAdd equation (3) and equation (4):\n$(12x + 10y) + (10x + 12y) = 13000 + 13400$\n$22x + 22y = 26400$\nDivide by 22:\n$x + y = 1200$ (5)\n\nSubtract equation (4) from equation (3):\n$(12x + 10y) - (10x + 12y) = 13000 - 13400$\n$2x - 2y = -400$\nDivide by 2:\n$x - y = -200$ (6)\n\nNow we have a simpler system of equations:\n5) $x + y = 1200$\n6) $x - y = -200$\n\nAdd equation (5) and equation (6):\n$(x + y) + (x - y) = 1200 + (-200)$\n$2x = 1000$\n$x = 500$\n\nSubstitute the value of $x$ into equation (5):\n$500 + y = 1200$\n$y = 1200 - 500$\n$y = 700$\n\nSo, the amount invested at 12% is \\$500, and the amount invested at 10% is \\$700.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{500 \\text{ at 12% and } 700 \\text{ at 10%}}$.",
