    "global index": "GeoBench_998",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements accurately describes the differences between a cylindrical and a conic map?\n\nOptions: \nA. A cylindrical shows true direction and examines the Earth from one point, usually a polar projection, while a conic map breaks up the globe into continents and separates the oceans.\nB. A cylindrical map shows true direction but loses distance, while a conic map shows true direction but examines the Earth from one point, usually a pole or polar projection.\nC. A cylindrical map shows true direction but loses distance, while a conic map tries to keep distance but loses directional qualities.\nD. A cylindrical map shows distance but loses directional qualities, while a conic map tries to keep true direction but loses distance.\nE. A conic map is the commonplace name for a cylindrical map.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks about the differences between cylindrical and conic map projections. Cylindrical projections, like the Mercator, are known for preserving direction (meridians and parallels are straight and perpendicular) but distort distances and areas, especially near the poles. Conic projections are useful for mid-latitude areas, where they can preserve area and shape over a limited latitudinal range. They are derived by projecting the globe onto a cone.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\n\nA. This option is incorrect. Cylindrical maps don't typically show true direction from a single point, and the description of conic maps is inaccurate. Polar projections are a type of cylindrical or azimuthal projection, not specific to conic development.\n\nB. This option incorrectly describes conic maps. Conic maps do not examine the Earth from a single point in the way described.\n\nC. This option accurately captures a key difference. Cylindrical maps, like Mercator, are conformal and show true direction but severely distort distance. Conic maps can be designed to minimize distance and area distortion within their standard parallels, but this often comes at the cost of directional accuracy over large areas.\n\nD. This is incorrect. Cylindrical maps distort distance, not necessarily show true distance. Conic maps do not necessarily aim to keep true direction but rather a balance of properties.\n\nE. This is incorrect. Conic and cylindrical are distinct types of map projections with different geometric bases.\n\nTherefore, option C provides the most accurate general distinction between cylindrical and conic map projections.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 217,
        "output_tokens": 317,
        "total_tokens": 534
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
