    "global index": "GeoBench_1174",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements best explains the bid-rent theory?\n\nOptions: \nA. Land value is highest in the central business district, and land value decreases with distance from the CBD.\nB. Land value is highest in the suburbs, resulting in larger houses.\nC. More space is available in the urban core because most growth is upward.\nD. Land value is higher in the suburbs due to a greater demand for land there.\nE. Land value is constant throughout the metropolitan area due to the high demand for residential space in general.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The bid-rent theory describes how the value of land changes based on its proximity to a central business district (CBD). It posits that the highest bids for land will be closest to the CBD, where accessibility and competition for space are greatest. As one moves further away from the CBD, land values tend to decrease, leading to, among other things, larger plot sizes and potentially different types of development.\n\nTherefore, option A correctly states the core principle of the bid-rent theory. Option B is incorrect because land value typically decreases with distance from the CBD, not increases in the suburbs. Option C is partially true relating to urban growth but not the primary explanation of bid-rent. Options D and E misinterpret the relationship between bid-rent, distance from the CBD, and land value distribution.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{A}.",
