    "global index": "MATH_251",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem and provide the final answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Six cars pull up to a red light, one at a time. At the light, there are three lanes, one left-turn lane, one straight-going lane, and one right-turn lane. How many ways can the cars stack up so that all three lanes are occupied?\n\nNote that if the first car turns left and the second goes straight, this is considered different from the first car going straight and the second car turning left. In other words, the cars are distinguishable, but pull up to the intersection in a fixed order.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "There are 6 cars that pull up one at a time. Each car can choose one of the three lanes: left-turn, straight-going, or right-turn. Since the cars are distinguishable and pull up in a fixed order, we need to determine the number of ways each of the 6 cars can choose a lane such that all three lanes are occupied.\n\nThe first car has 3 lane choices.\nThe second car has 3 lane choices.\nThe third car has 3 lane choices.\nThe fourth car has 3 lane choices.\nThe fifth car has 3 lane choices.\nThe sixth car has 3 lane choices.\n\nIf there were no condition that all three lanes must be occupied, there would be $3^6$ total ways the cars could be arranged. However, we need to ensure all three lanes are occupied. This means we must exclude the cases where only one lane is occupied or only two lanes are occupied.\n\nThe number of ways to occupy exactly one lane is $3 \\times 1^6$, as there are 3 choices for the single lane, and all 6 cars must go into that lane. So, $3 \\times 1 = 3$ ways.\nThe number of ways to occupy exactly two lanes can be calculated using the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion. First, choose 2 lanes out of 3, which is $\\binom{3}{2} = 3$ ways. For each pair of lanes, there are $2^6$ ways for the cars to choose those two lanes. However, this includes cases where all cars go into only one of those two lanes. So, we subtract the cases where only one lane is occupied, which is 2 ways for each pair of lanes. Thus, the number of ways to occupy exactly two lanes is $\\binom{3}{2} \\times (2^6 - 2) = 3 \\times (64 - 2) = 3 \\times 62 = 186$ ways.\n\nUsing the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion, the number of ways all three lanes are occupied is $3^6 - \\binom{3}{1}2^6 + \\binom{3}{2}1^6$.\nHere, $3^6$ is the total number of ways without any restrictions.\n$\\binom{3}{1}2^6$ is the number of ways where at least one lane is not used (i.e., at most 2 lanes are used). We choose 1 lane not to be used and all cars can choose from the remaining 2 lanes.\n$\\binom{3}{2}1^6$ is the number of ways where at least two lanes are not used (i.e., at most 1 lane is used). We choose 2 lanes not to be used and all cars must choose the remaining 1 lane.\n\nSo, the number of ways all three lanes are occupied is $3^6 - 3 \\times 2^6 + 3 \\times 1^6$.\n$3^6 = 729$\n$3 \\times 2^6 = 3 \\times 64 = 192$\n$3 \\times 1^6 = 3 \\times 1 = 3$\n\nNumber of ways = $729 - 192 + 3 = 537 + 3 = 540$.\n\nAlternatively, we can think of this as assigning one of the three lanes to each of the six distinguishable cars such that all three lanes are assigned to at least one car.\nThis is equivalent to finding the number of surjective functions from a set of 6 cars to a set of 3 lanes.\nThis can be calculated using Stirling numbers of the second kind: $3! \\times S(6, 3)$, where $S(6, 3)$ is the Stirling number of the second kind, which represents the number of ways to partition a set of 6 elements into 3 non-empty subsets.\n$S(6, 3) = \\frac{1}{3!} \\sum_{k=0}^{3} (-1)^{3-k} \\binom{3}{k} k^6$\n$S(6, 3) = \\frac{1}{6} \\left( (-1)^{3-0} \\binom{3}{0} 0^6 + (-1)^{3-1} \\binom{3}{1} 1^6 + (-1)^{3-2} \\binom{3}{2} 2^6 + (-1)^{3-3} \\binom{3}{3} 3^6 \\right)$\n$S(6, 3) = \\frac{1}{6} \\left( - 1 \\times 1 \\times 0 + 1 \\times 3 \\times 1 - 1 \\times 3 \\times 64 + 1 \\times 1 \\times 729 \\right)$\n$S(6, 3) = \\frac{1}{6} \\left( 0 + 3 - 192 + 729 \\right)$\n$S(6, 3) = \\frac{1}{6} \\left( 732 - 192 \\right) = \\frac{540}{6} = 90$\n\nThe number of ways to assign 6 distinguishable items into 3 distinguishable boxes such that no box is empty is $3! \\times S(6, 3) = 6 \\times 90 = 540$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{{540}}$.",
