    "global index": "MATH_479",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem and provide the final answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There are finitely many primes $p$ for which the congruence $$8x\\equiv 1\\pmod{{p}}$$has no solutions $x$. Determine the sum of all such $p$.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The congruence $8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$ has no solutions if and only if $\\gcd(8, p) \\neq 1$. Since $p$ is a prime, this means $p$ must be a factor of 8. The prime factors of 8 are 2. Therefore, the only prime for which the congruence has no solutions is $p=2$.\n\nThe congruence $8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$ has no solutions if the greatest common divisor of 8 and $p$ is not 1. Since $p$ is a prime number, this happens only if $p$ is a divisor of 8. The prime divisors of 8 are just 2. So, $p=2$ is the only prime for which the congruence has no solutions.\n\nThe set of primes $p$ for which the congruence $8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$ has no solutions is $\\{2\\}$. The sum of all such $p$ is therefore 2.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{{2}}$.",
      "success": true,
