    "global index": "MATH_495",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem and provide the final answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the domain of the function $f(x) = \\frac{{2-x}}{{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))}}$, where $\\log$ is the base $10$ logarithm function? Express your answer in interval notation.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "For the function $f(x) = \\frac{{2-x}}{{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))}}$, we need to ensure that the arguments of the logarithms are positive and that the denominator is non-zero.\nFirst, for $\\log(x-2)$ to be defined, we must have $x-2 > 0$, which means $x > 2$.\nNext, for $\\log(2-\\log(x-2))$ to be defined, we must have $2-\\log(x-2) > 0$. This implies $\\log(x-2) < 2$. Since the base of the logarithm is 10, we have $x-2 < 10^2$, which means $x-2 < 100$, so $x < 102$.\nFinally, the denominator cannot be zero, so $\\log(2-\\log(x-2)) \\neq 0$. This means $2-\\log(x-2) \\neq 10^0$, which implies $2-\\log(x-2) \\neq 1$. Thus, $\\log(x-2) \\neq 1$. This means $x-2 \\neq 10^1$, so $x-2 \\neq 10$, and $x \\neq 12$.\nCombining all conditions, we have $2 < x < 102$ and $x \\neq 12$. In interval notation, this is $(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)$.\n\nThe domain of the function is the set of all real numbers $x$ such that $x > 2$, $2 - \\log(x-2) > 0$, and $\\log(2 - \\log(x-2)) \\neq 0$.\nThe condition $x - 2 > 0$ implies $x > 2$.\nThe condition $2 - \\log(x-2) > 0$ implies $\\log(x-2) < 2$. Since the base of the logarithm is 10, this means $x-2 < 10^2 = 100$, so $x < 102$.\nThe condition $\\log(2 - \\log(x-2)) \\neq 0$ implies $2 - \\log(x-2) \\neq 10^0 = 1$. So, $\\log(x-2) \\neq 1$. This means $x-2 \\neq 10^1 = 10$, so $x \\neq 12$.\nCombining these inequalities, we have $2 < x < 102$ and $x \\neq 12$.\nIn interval notation, the domain is $(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)}$.",
