    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The target of digestion of a nucleosome dimer to nucleosome monomers by DNase is\n\nOptions: \nA. histones H2A, H2B, H3, and H4\nB. linker DNA\nC. histone H2A and H2B only\nD. the nucleosome linker\nE. histone H3 only\nF. the nucleosome head\nG. the nucleosome core\nH. all histones and the nucleosome core\nI. the nucleosome tail\nJ. the H1 histone\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks about the target of DNase digestion of a nucleosome dimer to monomers. DNase is an enzyme that digests DNA. A nucleosome is composed of DNA wrapped around a core of histone proteins. A nucleosome dimer consists of two such nucleosomes linked by linker DNA. DNase digestion would therefore target the DNA component. Option B, \"linker DNA,\" represents the DNA segment that connects nucleosomes in a chromatin fiber. Digestion here would separate nucleosomes.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
