    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose thecodonsfor amino acids consisted of only two bases rather than three. Would there be a sufficient number ofcodonsfor all twenty amino acids? Show how you obtain your answer.\n\nOptions: \nA. There will be more than 20 codons for 20 amino acids\nB. 4 codons will be enough to code for all 20 amino acids, with some sharing multiple codons\nC. 16 codons will be insufficient to code for all 20 amino acids\nD. Each amino acid will have multiple codons, with a total of 40 codons available\nE. There will be exactly one codon for each amino acid, with no redundancy\nF. 25 codons will be available, providing extra combinations for some amino acids\nG. Each amino acid will have its own unique codon\nH. 16 codons will be enough to code for all 20 amino acids\nI. There will be 64 codons, providing ample combinations for all amino acids and stop signals\nJ. 20 codons will be just enough to code for each amino acid uniquely\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks if a two-base codon system would be sufficient for all 20 amino acids. With two bases and four possible bases in nucleic acids (Adenine, Guanine, Cytosine, Thymine/Uracil), the number of possible two-base combinations is calculated as $4^2$. This gives us $4 \\times 4 = 16$ possible codons. Since there are 20 amino acids, 16 codons are not enough to uniquely code for each of them.\n\nLet's evaluate the options:\nA. \"There will be more than 20 codons for 20 amino acids\" - This is incorrect because we calculated only 16 codons.\nB. \"4 codons will be enough to code for all 20 amino acids, with some sharing multiple codons\" - This is incorrect. 4 codons are far too few.\nC. \"16 codons will be insufficient to code for all 20 amino acids\" - This is correct because we need 20 unique codons, and only 16 are available.\nD. \"Each amino acid will have multiple codons, with a total of 40 codons available\" - This is incorrect. The total number of available codons is 16.\nE. \"There will be exactly one codon for each amino acid, with no redundancy\" - This is incorrect because 16 codons are not enough for 20 amino acids, meaning there would have to be redundancy or some amino acids not coded for.\nF. \"25 codons will be available, providing extra combinations for some amino acids\" - This is incorrect. The number of available codons is 16.\nG. \"Each amino acid will have its own unique codon\" - This is incorrect because only 16 unique codons are available for 20 amino acids.\nH. \"16 codons will be enough to code for all 20 amino acids\" - This is incorrect because 16 is less than 20.\nI. \"There will be 64 codons, providing ample combinations for all amino acids and stop signals\" - This refers to a three-base codon system ($4^3 = 64$), not a two-base system.\nJ. \"20 codons will be just enough to code for each amino acid uniquely\" - This is incorrect. With a two-base system, only 16 codons are possible.\n\nThe calculation shows that there are $4^2 = 16$ possible codons for a two-base system. Since there are 20 standard amino acids, 16 codons are insufficient to code for all of them uniquely. Therefore, some amino acids would not have a codon, or there would need to be a reduction in the number of amino acids.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
