    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks how muscle force can be graded given the all-or-none law of muscle fiber contraction. This means that individual motor units are either fully recruited or not at all. To generate varying levels of force, the nervous system must employ mechanisms beyond simply activating a single fiber.\n\nOption A is partially correct but incomplete as it doesn't account for all gradation. Option B is a consequence, not a mechanism. Option C is related but not the primary mechanism. Option D, F, and J are contributing factors but not the direct mechanisms for grading force in response to varying loads. Option E is not the primary way force is graded. Option G relates to different muscles having different properties but doesn't explain grading within the *same* muscle. Option H describes recruitment, which is crucial, but doesn't address how individual fibers can vary their tension contribution.\n\nOption I accurately describes the two primary mechanisms for grading muscle force: recruitment (activating more motor units) and varying the tension produced by individual fibers (through summation of stimuli, i.e., varying frequency of nerve impulses, and by the extent of fiber shortening influenced by length).\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
