    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the limiting high-temperature molar heat capacity at constant volume (C_V) of a gas-phase diatomic molecule?\n\nOptions: \nA. 4.5R\nB. 2.2R\nC. 1R\nD. 4R\nE. 2R\nF. 2.5R\nG. 5R\nH. 3R\nI. 3.5R\nJ. 1.5R\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks for the limiting high-temperature molar heat capacity at constant volume ($C_V$) of a gas-phase diatomic molecule. At high temperatures, all degrees of freedom are expected to be excited. For a diatomic molecule, these include translational (3), rotational (2), and vibrational (2) degrees of freedom. Therefore, the total number of excited degrees of freedom is 3 + 2 + 2 = 7. According to the equipartition theorem, each excited degree of freedom contributes $\\frac{1}{2}R$ to the molar heat capacity. Thus, the high-temperature molar heat capacity at constant volume is $7 \\times \\frac{1}{2}R = 3.5R$.\n\nThe correct answer is G.",
