      "generated_answer": "To determine the molecular geometry of SOCl2, we first need to consider the electron group geometry. Sulfur (S) is the central atom. It is bonded to two chlorine atoms (Cl) and one oxygen atom (O). Sulfur also has one lone pair of electrons. Therefore, there are a total of four electron groups around the central sulfur atom. According to VSEPR theory, four electron groups arrange themselves in a tetrahedral electron group geometry.\n\nHowever, molecular geometry considers only the positions of the atoms. With one lone pair and three bonding pairs (S-Cl, S-Cl, S=O), the electron groups will arrange themselves to minimize repulsion, leading to a molecular geometry that is not perfectly tetrahedral. The three bonding groups and one lone pair will result in a trigonal pyramidal shape, similar to ammonia (NH3).\n\nThe options provided are:\nA. tetrahedral\nB. Bent\nC. trigonal pyramidal\nD. trigonal planar\nE. T-shaped\nF. Octahedral\nG. Seesaw\nH. Trigonal bipyramidal\nI. Square planar\nJ. Linear\n\nBased on our analysis, the molecular geometry is trigonal pyramidal.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
