      "generated_answer": "The simplest alkene is ethene, which has the formula C2H4. Ethene has a double bond between the two carbon atoms, consisting of one sigma bond and one pi bond. The carbon atoms are sp2 hybridized, leading to a trigonal planar geometry around each carbon. Therefore, ethene has a trigonal planar configuration.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. cis-trans isomers: The simplest alkene, ethene, does not have cis-trans isomers because there are two identical groups (hydrogens) attached to each carbon of the double bond.\nB. at least four sigma bonds: Ethene has three sigma bonds (one between the carbons and one between each carbon and two hydrogens).\nC. no sigma bonds: Ethene has one sigma bond between the carbons.\nD. a trigonal planar configuration: The geometry around each sp2 hybridized carbon in an alkene is trigonal planar.\nE. a tetrahedral configuration: Tetrahedral configuration is associated with sp3 hybridized atoms, which are found in alkanes.\nF. at least three pi bonds: Ethene has only one pi bond.\nG. at least two pi bonds: Ethene has only one pi bond.\nH. aromatic properties: Aromatic compounds are cyclic, planar structures that follow Hückel's rule and have delocalized pi electrons, which is not the case for the simplest alkene.\nI. at least six sigma bonds: Ethene has only three sigma bonds.\n\nThe correct answer is D.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
