  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_4067",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: It is possible to produce very high magnetic fields over small volumes by special techniques. What would be the resonance frequency of an electron spin in an organic radical in a field of $1.0 \\mathrm{{kT}}$ ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 3.2 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nB. 1.8 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nC. 4.0 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nD. 3.0 $10^{{12}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nE. 5.5 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nF.  2.8 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nG. 1.5 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nH. 3.6 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nI. 2.2 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\nJ. 2.5 $10^{{13}} \\mathrm{{~Hz}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The resonance frequency of an electron spin in a magnetic field is given by the Larmor frequency formula: $\\nu = \\frac{g \\mu_B B}{h}$, where $g$ is the Landé g-factor (approximately 2 for an electron), $\\mu_B$ is the Bohr magneton, $B$ is the magnetic field strength, and $h$ is Planck's constant.\nUsing the values $g=2$, $\\mu_B = 9.274 \\times 10^{-24} \\mathrm{JT^{-1}}$, $B = 1.0 \\mathrm{kT} = 1000 \\mathrm{T}$, and $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{Js}$:\n$\\nu = \\frac{2 \\times (9.274 \\times 10^{-24} \\mathrm{JT^{-1}}) \\times (1000 \\mathrm{T})}{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{Js}} \\approx \\frac{18.548 \\times 10^{-21}}{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}} \\mathrm{s^{-1}} \\approx 2.80 \\times 10^{13} \\mathrm{Hz}$.\n\nComparing this calculated value to the given options, option F is the closest.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
