    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_4064",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the orbital angular momentum quantum number, l, of the electron that is most easily removed when ground-state aluminum is ionized?\n\nOptions: \nA. 5\nB. 2\nC. -1\nD. 4\nE. 6\nF. 3\nG. 7\nH. 1\nI. 0\nJ. 8\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the orbital angular momentum quantum number, $l$, of the electron that is most easily removed from ground-state aluminum, we need to consider its electron configuration. The electron configuration of aluminum (atomic number 13) is $1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 3s^2 3p^1$. The outermost electrons, which are the ones most easily removed during ionization, are in the $3p$ subshell. The orbital angular momentum quantum number, $l$, for a $p$ subshell is 1.\n\nThe outermost electron of aluminum is in the $3p$ subshell. The value of the orbital angular momentum quantum number, $l$, for $p$ orbitals is always 1. Since this is the outermost electron, it will require the least amount of energy to remove it, making it the most easily removed electron during ionization.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
