    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3983",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The duration of a $90^{{\\circ}}$ or $180^{{\\circ}}$ pulse depends on the strength of the $\\mathscr{{B}}_1$ field. If a $180^{{\\circ}}$ pulse requires $12.5 \\mu \\mathrm{{s}}$, what is the strength of the $\\mathscr{{B}}_1$ field? \n\nOptions: \nA. 3.8 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nB. 4.2 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nC. 4.7 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nD. 6.2 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nE. 7.1 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nF. 7.8 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nG. 9.3 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nH.  5.9 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nI. 5.0 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\nJ. 8.4 $10^{{-4}} \\mathrm{{~T}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The duration of a pulse, $\\tau$, is inversely proportional to the strength of the $\\mathscr{B}_1$ field, $B_1$, and directly proportional to the flip angle, $\\theta$, which is usually expressed in radians. The relationship is given by $\\theta = \\gamma B_1 \\tau$, where $\\gamma$ is the gyromagnetic ratio. For a $180^{\\circ}$ pulse, $\\theta = \\pi$ radians. We are given $\\tau = 12.5 \\mu \\mathrm{s} = 12.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{s}$. For protons, the gyromagnetic ratio is approximately $\\gamma_{p} \\approx 42.58 \\mathrm{MHz/T} \\approx 42.58 \\times 10^{6} \\mathrm{rad/(s \\cdot T)}$.\n\nTherefore, $B_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{\\gamma \\tau} = \\frac{\\pi}{42.58 \\times 10^{6} \\mathrm{rad/(s \\cdot T)} \\times 12.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{s}}$.\n\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{42.58 \\times 12.5} \\times 10^{-6+6} \\mathrm{T} = \\frac{\\pi}{532.25} \\mathrm{T} \\approx 0.00590 \\mathrm{T} = 5.9 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{T} = 5.9 \\times 10^{2} \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{mT} = 5.9 \\mathrm{mT}$.\n\nLet's recheck the units and calculation.\n$\\theta = \\pi$ radians\n$\\tau = 12.5 \\times 10^{-6}$ s\n$\\gamma = 42.58 \\times 10^{6}$ rad/(s·T)\n\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{\\gamma \\tau} = \\frac{\\pi \\text{ rad}}{(42.58 \\times 10^{6} \\text{ rad/(s·T)}) \\times (12.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{ s})}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{42.58 \\times 12.5} \\times \\frac{1}{\\text{T}}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{532.25} \\text{ T}$\n$B_1 \\approx 0.00590 \\text{ T}$\n$B_1 \\approx 5.9 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{ T}$\n\nThere seems to be a misinterpretation of the desired units or a typo in my calculation/understanding of the common units for $\\mathscr{B}_1$ field strength in MRI context. Let me reconsider the options and typical values.\n\nThe question asks for the strength of the $\\mathscr{B}_1$ field in $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$.\nLet's re-calculate:\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{42.58 \\times 10^{6} \\times 12.5 \\times 10^{-6}} \\mathrm{T} = \\frac{\\pi}{532.25} \\mathrm{T} \\approx 0.00590 \\mathrm{T}$\n$0.00590 \\mathrm{T} = 5.9 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{T}$\n\nLet's assume there might be a typo in the options provided, or I need to adjust the gyromagnetic ratio for a different nucleus or use a slightly different value. However, assuming proton NMR and the standard relationship, the calculated value is approximately $5.9 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{T}$.\n\nLet's express this in the form of the options, which are of the order $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$.\n$5.9 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{T} = 59 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$.\nThis is significantly larger than any of the options.\n\nLet's re-examine the problem statement and the formula.\nThe relationship is $\\theta = \\gamma B_1 \\tau$.\nWe are given $\\theta = 180^{\\circ} = \\pi$ radians.\nWe are given $\\tau = 12.5 \\mu s = 12.5 \\times 10^{-6}$ s.\nThe gyromagnetic ratio for proton is $\\gamma \\approx 2.675 \\times 10^8 \\mathrm{rad \\cdot s^{-1} \\cdot T^{-1}}$. My previous value was in MHz/T. Let's use the correct value.\n$\\gamma_{p} \\approx 2.675 \\times 10^8 \\mathrm{rad/(s \\cdot T)}$\n\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\theta}{\\gamma \\tau} = \\frac{\\pi \\text{ rad}}{(2.675 \\times 10^8 \\text{ rad/(s·T)}) \\times (12.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{ s})}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{2.675 \\times 10^8 \\times 12.5 \\times 10^{-6}} \\mathrm{T}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{2.675 \\times 12.5 \\times 10^{8-6}} \\mathrm{T}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{33.4375 \\times 10^2} \\mathrm{T}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{3343.75} \\mathrm{T}$\n$B_1 \\approx 0.0009359 \\mathrm{T}$\n$B_1 \\approx 9.359 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{T}$\n\nNow, let's compare this with the options:\nA. 3.8 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nB. 4.2 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nC. 4.7 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nD. 6.2 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nE. 7.1 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nF. 7.8 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nG. 9.3 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nH. 5.9 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nI. 5.0 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\nJ. 8.4 $10^{-4} \\mathrm{~T}$\n\nOur calculated value is approximately $9.359 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{T}$, which is very close to option G.\n\nThe duration of a $\\theta$ pulse is given by $\\tau = \\frac{\\theta}{\\gamma B_1}$.\nFor a $180^{\\circ}$ pulse, $\\theta = \\pi$ radians.\nSo, $\\tau = \\frac{\\pi}{\\gamma B_1}$.\nRearranging, $B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{\\gamma \\tau}$.\nUsing the gyromagnetic ratio for proton, $\\gamma \\approx 2.675 \\times 10^8 \\mathrm{rad \\cdot s^{-1} \\cdot T^{-1}}$.\nGiven $\\tau = 12.5 \\mu s = 12.5 \\times 10^{-6} s$.\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{(2.675 \\times 10^8 \\mathrm{rad \\cdot s^{-1} \\cdot T^{-1}}) \\times (12.5 \\times 10^{-6} s)}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{2.675 \\times 12.5 \\times 10^{8-6}} \\mathrm{T}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{33.4375 \\times 10^2} \\mathrm{T}$\n$B_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{3343.75} \\mathrm{T} \\approx 0.0009359 \\mathrm{T} \\approx 9.36 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{T}$.\n\nThe strength of the $\\mathscr{B}_1$ field is inversely proportional to the pulse duration for a given flip angle. Based on the derived formula and the gyromagnetic ratio for protons, the calculated value matches option G.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
