    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3349",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is incorrectly named?\n\nOptions: \nA. MgO   magnesium oxide\nB. AgNO3   silver nitrate\nC. CuCl2   copper(II) chloride\nD. H2SO4   sulfuric acid\nE. K2Cr2O7   potassium dichromate\nF. Na2SO4   sodium sulfate\nG. Fe(NO3)3   iron(III) nitrate\nH. CaCl2   calcium chloride\nI. AlBr3   aluminum tribromide\nJ. Zn(NO3)2   zinc nitrate\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks to identify the incorrectly named compound. Let's analyze each option:\n\nA. MgO is magnesium oxide. This is correct.\nB. AgNO3 is silver nitrate. This is correct.\nC. CuCl2 is copper(II) chloride. Copper can have multiple oxidation states, so the Roman numeral is necessary and correct.\nD. H2SO4 is sulfuric acid. This is a common and correct name for the acid.\nE. K2Cr2O7 is potassium dichromate. This is correct.\nF. Na2SO4 is sodium sulfate. This is correct.\nG. Fe(NO3)3 is iron(III) nitrate. Iron can have multiple oxidation states, so the Roman numeral is necessary and correct.\nH. CaCl2 is calcium chloride. Calcium is in Group 2 and forms a +2 ion, so no Roman numeral is needed. This is correct.\nI. AlBr3 is aluminum tribromide. Aluminum is in Group 13 and almost exclusively forms a +3 ion. While indicating 'tri' is sometimes done for molecular compounds, for ionic compounds like AlBr3, the correct nomenclature is to use the cation name followed by the anion name. Thus, it should be aluminum bromide.\n\nJ. Zn(NO3)2 is zinc nitrate. Zinc typically forms a +2 ion, and it's a transition metal, but it's a common exception where a Roman numeral is usually omitted as it's almost always +2. Therefore, zinc nitrate is generally accepted.\n\nBased on the systematic nomenclature rules, AlBr3 should be named aluminum bromide. The prefix \"tri-\" is typically reserved for covalent compounds.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
