  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_4320",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A buffer is made from equal concentrations of a weak acid and its conjugate base. Doubling the volume of the buffer solution by adding water has what effect on its pH?\n\nOptions: \nA. It causes the pH to fluctuate unpredictably.\nB. It causes the pH to equalize with the water's pH.\nC. It causes the pH to become extremely basic.\nD. It neutralizes the pH completely.\nE. It significantly decreases the pH.\nF. It has little effect.\nG. It significantly increases the pH.\nH. It doubles the pH value.\nI. It changes the pH asymptotically to the pKa of the acid.\nJ. It causes the pH to become extremely acidic.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation describes the pH of a buffer solution: pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]). In this case, the concentrations of the weak acid ([HA]) and its conjugate base ([A-]) are equal. When the volume of the buffer is doubled by adding water, both [A-] and [HA] are halved. However, the ratio [A-]/[HA] remains unchanged. Therefore, the pH does not change.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{F}.",
