    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3729",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What do the differences in properties between lithium acetyl acetone (m.p. very high, insoluble in chloroform) and beryllium acetyl acetone (m.p. 108°,b.p. 270°, soluble in chloroform) suggest about their structures?\n\nOptions: \nA. Both are non-ionic compounds\nB. Lithium acetylacetonate is non-ionic and beryllium acetylacetonate is ionic\nC. Both are ionic compounds\nD. Lithium acetylacetonate is soluble in chloroform due to its polar nature, while beryllium acetylacetonate is not\nE. Beryllium acetylacetonate is polymeric due to hydrogen bonding, whereas lithium acetylacetonate is not\nF. Both exhibit similar degrees of ionic character and solubility properties\nG. Beryllium acetylacetonate is a volatile liquid at room temperature, while lithium acetylacetonate is a high melting solid\nH. Lithium acetylacetonate is ionic or salt-like and beryllium acetylacetonate is non-ionic or covalent\nI. Lithium acetylacetonate is a polymeric compound, while beryllium acetylacetonate is monomeric\nJ. Both are polymeric in nature with extensive hydrogen bonding\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The observed properties of lithium acetyl acetone (very high melting point, insoluble in chloroform) and beryllium acetyl acetone (lower melting point, soluble in chloroform) suggest a significant difference in their bonding and structure. High melting points and insolubility in organic solvents are characteristic of ionic or highly polar lattice structures. Conversely, lower melting points, solubility in organic solvents, and volatility suggest covalent bonding and discrete molecular structures. Beryllium, being a smaller and more electronegative metal than lithium, and forming a more covalent bond with the acetylacetonate ligand, leads to a molecular, often polymeric, structure. Lithium, being a larger alkali metal, forms a more ionic bond with the acetylacetonate ligand, resulting in a high melting point solid with a salt-like structure.\n\nThe most likely correct answer is **H**.\n\nExplanation: The differences in physical properties strongly indicate a difference in bonding. High melting point and insolubility in chloroform for lithium acetyl acetone suggest an ionic or salt-like structure. Low melting point, volatility, and solubility in chloroform for beryllium acetyl acetone suggest a non-ionic or covalent molecular structure. This aligns with the general trend of alkali metal compounds being more ionic compared to alkaline earth metal compounds with similar ligands when considering electronegativity differences.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
