 differential equations. The Poincare inequality is crucial for coercivity in Lax-Milgram, making this option highly plausible.\nOption J suggests \"All of the above\", which is unlikely given the specificity of PDE theory.\n\nThe most accurate and complete proof for the existence and uniqueness of a weak solution to the Laplace equation with Dirichlet boundary conditions in $L^2(\\Omega)$ relies on the Lax-Milgram theorem. The coercivity of the associated bilinear form, which is essential for Lax-Milgram, is established using the Poincare inequality. While Sobolev embedding theorems can provide information about the regularity of the solution, they are not the primary tool for proving existence and uniqueness of the weak solution itself. The Riesz representation theorem is more generally about the dual space of a Hilbert space and its relation to the Hilbert space itself. Therefore, option I, which combines the Sobolev embedding theorem with the Lax-Milgram theorem, is the closest among the given options to the standard method, with the implicit understanding that Poincare inequality is needed for Lax-Milgram. However, if we consider the *proof*, Lax-Milgram is the central theorem, and Poincare inequality is a crucial ingredient for its application in this context. None of the options perfectly align with \"Poincare inequality and Lax-Milgram theorem\".\n\nRe-evaluating the options, option I is the most reasonable among the choices that directly involve theorems used in PDE theory. Lax-Milgram is the main theorem, and Sobolev embedding theorems can be seen as related to the function spaces considered. However, a direct reference to Poincare inequality alongside Lax-Milgram would be ideal. Given the options, we need to select the most appropriate.\n\nLet's consider the wording more carefully. The question asks how the existence and uniqueness \"can be proved by\".\n\nStandard proof involves:\n1. Defining the weak formulation of the PDE.\n2. Defining a bilinear form 