    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The set of all nth roots of unity under multiplication of complex numbers form a/an\n\nOptions: \nA. non-abelian group\nB. infinite group\nC. commutative semigroups with identity\nD. non-commutative group\nE. abelian group\nF. non-commutative semi group with identity\nG. semi group with identity\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The set of all nth roots of unity under multiplication of complex numbers forms a group. This is because:\n1.  **Closure:** The product of two nth roots of unity is also an nth root of unity.\n2.  **Associativity:** Multiplication of complex numbers is associative.\n3.  **Identity:** The complex number 1 is the identity element (1^n = 1).\n4.  **Inverse:** For every nth root of unity, its reciprocal (which is also an nth root of unity) is its inverse.\n\nFurthermore, multiplication of complex numbers is commutative. Therefore, the set is a commutative (abelian) group. It is not infinite, as there are exactly n distinct nth roots of unity.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{E}.",
