  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6533",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An assembly line machine is supposed to turn out ball bearings with a diameter of 1.25 centimeters. Each morning the first 30 bearings produced are pulled and measured. If their mean diameter is under 1.23 centimeters or over 1.27 centimeters, the machinery is stopped and an engineer is called to make adjustments before production is resumed. The quality control procedure may be viewed as a hypothesis test with the null hypothesis H0: μ = 1.25 and the alternative hypothesis Ha: μ ≠ 1.25. The engineer is asked to make adjustments when the null hypothesis is rejected. In test terminology, what would a Type II error result in?\n\nOptions: \nA. An incorrect assumption about the machinery's calibration\nB. An unnecessary stoppage of the production process\nC. Continued production of wrong size ball bearings\nD. An unnecessary adjustment of the machinery\nE. A warranted halt in production to adjust the machinery\nF. A false alarm about the size of ball bearings\nG. Continued production of proper size ball bearings\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A Type II error occurs when the null hypothesis is not rejected when it is false. In this context, H0: μ = 1.25 is the null hypothesis that the mean diameter of ball bearings is 1.25 cm. The alternative hypothesis is Ha: μ ≠ 1.25, meaning the mean diameter is not 1.25 cm.\n\nIf a Type II error occurs, it means the null hypothesis (μ = 1.25) is actually false, but the test fails to reject it. This implies that the true mean diameter of the ball bearings is indeed different from 1.25 cm, but the quality control procedure (based on the first 30 bearings) does not detect this. Therefore, production continues with ball bearings that are not of the proper size.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. An incorrect assumption about the machinery's calibration: This is a consequence of the error, not the error itself.\nB. An unnecessary stoppage of the production process: This describes a Type I error (rejecting H0 when it's true).\nC. Continued production of wrong size ball bearings: This accurately describes the outcome of a Type II error in this scenario. The machine is producing bearings with a mean diameter different from 1.25 cm, but the test fails to detect it, so production continues.\nD. An unnecessary adjustment of the machinery: This also describes a Type I error.\nE. A warranted halt in production to adjust the machinery: This describes correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis (a correct decision).\nF. A false alarm about the size of ball bearings: This can be interpreted as a Type I error (stopping production when it's not needed).\nG. Continued production of proper size ball bearings: This describes correctly failing to reject a true null hypothesis (a correct decision).\n\nThe most likely correct answer is that a Type II error would result in continued production of wrong size ball bearings.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 277,
        "output_tokens": 420,
        "total_tokens": 697
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
