  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7510",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is the primary difference between an experiment and an observational study?\n\nOptions: \nA. In an experiment, the researcher has no control over variables; in an observational study, the researcher can manipulate variables.\nB. Experiments are only conducted on human subjects; observational studies can be conducted on nonhuman subjects.\nC. Observational studies are conducted in a lab setting, while experiments can be conducted in any environment.\nD. Experiments must be double-blind; observational studies do not need to be.\nE. In an experiment, the researcher observes without interfering; in an observational study, the researcher manipulates the environment.\nF. Experiments can only be conducted once; observational studies can be repeated.\nG. Experiments must use randomized treatment and control groups; observational studies also use treatment and control groups, but they do not need to be randomized.\nH. Experiments involve quantitative data; observational studies involve qualitative data.\nI. In an experiment, the researcher manipulates some variable to observe its effect on a response variable; in an observational study, he or she simply observes and records the observations.\nJ. Experiments require a hypothesis; observational studies do not require a hypothesis.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The primary difference between an experiment and an observational study lies in the researcher's involvement in manipulating variables. In an experiment, the researcher actively intervenes and changes one or more variables to observe the effect. In an observational study, the researcher passively observes and measures variables without any manipulation.\n\nThe correct answer is **I**. In an experiment, the researcher manipulates some variable to observe its effect on a response variable; in an observational study, he or she simply observes and records the observations. This option accurately captures the core distinction between these two research methodologies. Other options contain inaccuracies or are not the primary differentiating factor.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
