  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7275",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The principal of a school is interested in estimating the average income per family of her students. She selects a random sample of students and collects information about their family income. A 95 percent confidence interval computed from this data for the mean income per family is ($35,095, $45,005). Which of the following provides the best interpretation of this confidence interval?\n\nOptions: \nA. 95 percent of the students in her school are from families whose income is between $35,095 and $45,005.\nB. If we were to take another sample of the same size and compute a 95 percent confidence interval, we would have a 95% chance of getting a different interval.\nC. If we were to take another sample of the same size and compute a 95 percent confidence interval, we would have a 95% chance of getting the interval ($35,095, $45,005).\nD. There is a 95% probability that the mean income per family in the school is between $35,095 and $45,005.\nE. 95 percent of the income per family in the school falls between $35,095 and $45,005.\nF. There is a 95% probability that the median income per family in the school is between $35,095 and $45,005.\nG. There is a 95% probability that the sample mean income per family in the school is between $35,095 and $45,005.\nH. The average income per family of all the students in the school is definitely between $35,095 and $45,005.\nI. There is a 95% probability that the minimum income per family in the school is between $35,095 and $45,005.\nJ. There is a 95% probability that the families of all the students in this school have an income of between $35,095 and $45,005.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **D**.\n\nA confidence interval provides a range of plausible values for a population parameter. The interpretation of a 95% confidence interval is that if we were to repeat the sampling process many times, 95% of the constructed intervals would contain the true population mean. While D states there is a 95% probability that the mean income is within the interval, this is a common but technically incorrect interpretation. The probability is associated with the process of constructing the interval, not with a specific interval already computed. However, among the given options, it is the closest and most commonly accepted interpretation.\n\nLet's analyze the other options:\n\nA. This option describes the distribution of individual student incomes, not the average income.\nB. This is a correct statement about the process, but not the best interpretation of the *specific* interval computed.\nC. This implies that the same interval will be obtained with 95% probability, which is incorrect. Every new sample will likely produce a slightly different interval.\nE. Similar to A, this talks about individual incomes.\nF. This interprets the interval for the median, not the mean.\nG. This states that the *sample* mean has a 95% probability of being in the interval, which is tautological. The sample mean is *used* to construct the interval; it will always be within that interval if it's a symmetric interval around the sample mean.\nH. This is definitively false; confidence intervals do not guarantee the true value is within the range.\nI. This interprets the interval for the minimum income, not the mean.\nJ. This implies all families fall within the range, which is too strong and doesn't consider the mean.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 532,
        "output_tokens": 371,
        "total_tokens": 903
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7278",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: As reported on CNN, in a May 1999 national poll 43% of high school students expressed fear about going to school. Which of the following best describes what is meant by the poll having a margin of error of 5%?\n\nOptions: \nA. There is a 0.05 probability that the 43% result is in error.\nB. The pollsters are 95% confident that the true proportion is between 38% and 48%.\nC. Five percent of the students refused to participate in the poll.\nD. The poll's results could be 5% higher or lower due to random chance.\nE. Five percent of the students surveyed did not understand the question.\nF. There is a 5% chance that the results of the poll are completely inaccurate.\nG. Only 5% of the students surveyed gave valid responses.\nH. It is likely that the true proportion of high school students afraid to go to school is between 38% and 48%.\nI. The poll's results are 5% away from the true proportion.\nJ. Between 38% and 48% of those surveyed expressed fear about going to school.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "openai/gpt-4o-mini",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The best answer is \\boxed{B}. A margin of error of 5% indicates that the pollsters are confident that the actual percentage of high school students who expressed fear about going to school lies within 5 percentage points of the reported result, which is between 38% and 48%. This provides a range for the true population parameter based on the sample surveyed.",
