    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6954",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use Stokes' Theorem to evaluate $\\int_C \\mathbf{{F}} \\cdot d \\mathbf{{r}}$, where $\\mathbf{{F}}(x, y, z)=x y \\mathbf{{i}}+y z \\mathbf{{j}}+z x \\mathbf{{k}}$, and $C$ is the triangle with vertices $(1,0,0),(0,1,0)$, and $(0,0,1)$, oriented counterclockwise as viewed from above.\n\n\nOptions: \nA.  $-\\frac{{1}}{{2}}$\nB. $\\frac{{1}}{{2}}$\nC. $0$\nD. $\\frac{{3}}{{4}}$\nE. $\\frac{{1}}{{4}}$\nF. $-1$\nG. $\\frac{{1}}{{3}}$\nH. $\\frac{{2}}{{3}}$\nI. $-\\frac{{1}}{{4}}$\nJ. $-\\frac{{1}}{{3}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Stokes' Theorem states that $\\int_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot d\\mathbf{r} = \\iint_S (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}) \\cdot d\\mathbf{S}$, where $S$ is any surface with boundary $C$.  First, calculate the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$: $\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z} \\\\ xy & yz & zx \\end{vmatrix} = (z - y)\\mathbf{i} + (y - z)\\mathbf{j} + (x - x)\\mathbf{k} = (z-y)\\mathbf{i} + (y-z)\\mathbf{j}$.\nThe surface $S$ is the triangle with vertices $(1,0,0),(0,1,0)$, and $(0,0,1)$. The equation of the plane containing this triangle is $x+y+z=1$. We can parameterize the surface as $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\langle u, v, 1-u-v \\rangle$ for $u,v$ in the triangle defined by $u \\ge 0, v \\ge 0, u+v \\le 1$. Then $\\mathbf{r}_u = \\langle 1, 0, -1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{r}_v = \\langle 0, 1, -1 \\rangle$. The normal vector is $\\mathbf{r}_u \\times \\mathbf{r}_v = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k} = \\langle 1, 1, 1 \\rangle$.\nThe orientation is counterclockwise as viewed from above. Since the normal vector $\\langle 1,1,1 \\rangle$ points upwards, this orientation is consistent.\nNow we evaluate the flux integral:\n$(\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}_u \\times \\mathbf{r}_v) = ((1-u-v) - v) + (v - (1-u-v)) = 1 - 2v - u + v + u + v - 1 = 0$.\nThe integral $\\iint_S (\\nabla \\times \\mathbf{F}) \\cdot d\\mathbf{S} = \\iint_D 0 \\, dA = 0$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 249,
        "output_tokens": 605,
        "total_tokens": 854
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
