    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6918",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use Stoke's Theorem to evaluate $\\int_C \\vec{{F}} \\cdot d \\vec{{r}}$ where $\\vec{{F}} = z^2 \\vec{{i}} + y^2 \\vec{{j}} + x \\vec{{k}}$ and $C$ is the triangle with vertices (1,0,0), (0,1,0) and (0,0,1) with counter-clockwise rotation.\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.166\nB. -1.000\nC. -0.166\nD. 0.333\nE. 0.500\nF. 0.666\nG. -0.333\nH. -0.500\nI. -0.666\nJ. 1.000\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to Stokes' theorem, $\\int_C \\vec{{F}} \\cdot d \\vec{{r}} = \\iint_S (\\nabla \\times \\vec{{F}}) \\cdot d\\vec{{S}}$, where S is any surface bounded by C.\nFirst, we compute the curl of $\\vec{{F}}$: $\\nabla \\times \\vec{{F}} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z} \\\\ z^2 & y^2 & x \\end{vmatrix} = (0 - 0)\\mathbf{i} - (1 - 2z)\\mathbf{j} + (0 - 0)\\mathbf{k} = (1 - 2z)\\mathbf{j}$.\nThe surface S is the part of the plane $x+y+z=1$ in the first octant. We can parameterize this plane as $\\vec{{r}}(x,y) = x\\mathbf{i} + y\\mathbf{j} + (1-x-y)\\mathbf{k}$.\nThen, $\\frac{\\partial \\vec{{r}}}{\\partial x} = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{k}$ and $\\frac{\\partial \\vec{{r}}}{\\partial y} = \\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{k}$.\nThe normal vector is $\\vec{{n}} = \\frac{\\partial \\vec{{r}}}{\\partial x} \\times \\frac{\\partial \\vec{{r}}}{\\partial y} = (\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{k}) \\times (\\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{k}) = \\mathbf{i} \\times \\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{i} \\times \\mathbf{k} - \\mathbf{k} \\times \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k} \\times \\mathbf{k} = \\mathbf{k} + \\mathbf{i} - (-\\mathbf{i}) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}$.\nThe unit normal vector is $\\vec{{N}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k})$. Since the curve C is counter-clockwise, the normal vector should point in the positive z direction, which is consistent with our calculated normal vector. So, $d\\vec{{S}} = \\vec{{n}} dA = (\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}) dA$.\nNow we need to evaluate $(\\nabla \\times \\vec{{F}}) \\cdot \\vec{{n}}$.\n$(\\nabla \\times \\vec{{F}}) \\cdot \\vec{{n}} = ((1 - 2z)\\mathbf{j}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}) = 1 - 2z$.\nSince $z = 1 - x - y$, we have $1 - 2(1 - x - y) = 1 - 2 + 2x + 2y = 2x + 2y - 1$.\nWe integrate this over the region in the xy-plane defined by $x \\ge 0$, $y \\ge 0$, and $x+y \\le 1$.\n$\\iint_S (\\nabla \\times \\vec{{F}}) \\cdot d\\vec{{S}} = \\iint_D (2x + 2y - 1) dA$, where D is the triangle with vertices (0,0), (1,0), and (0,1) in the xy-plane.\nWe can set up the integral as follows:\n$\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{1-x} (2x + 2y - 1) dy dx = \\int_0^1 [2xy + y^2 - y]_0^{1-x} dx$\n$= \\int_0^1 (2x(1-x) + (1-x)^2 - (1-x)) dx = \\int_0^1 (2x - 2x^2 + 1 - 2x + x^2 - 1 + x) dx$\n$= \\int_0^1 (x - x^2) dx = [\\frac{x^2}{2} - \\frac{x^3}{3}]_0^1 = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{3-2}{6} = \\frac{1}{6}$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 244,
        "output_tokens": 1012,
        "total_tokens": 1256
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
