    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6713",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $X_1, X_2, \\ldots$ be a sequence of independent indetically distributed random variables drawn according to the probability mass function $p(x) = N(0,1)$. Let $q(x)=N(1,1)$ be another probability mass function. Use natural logarithm to evaluate $\\lim -\\frac{{1}}{{n}}\\log{{q(X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n)}}$ as $n \\to \\infty$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 2.1\nB. 1.4\nC. 0.5\nD. 3.5\nE. 2.8\nF. 1.8\nG. 0.7\nH. 3.1\nI. 2.5\nJ. 1.1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem asks for the limit of $-\\frac{1}{n}\\log q(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n)$ as $n \\to \\infty$, where $X_i$ are i.i.d. from $N(0,1)$ and $q(x) = N(1,1)$. Since the $X_i$ are independent, $q(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n) = \\prod_{i=1}^n q(X_i)$. Therefore, $\\log q(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\log q(X_i)$. By the Law of Large Numbers, $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\log q(X_i) = E[\\log q(X_1)]$.\n\nThe probability density function for $N(0,1)$ is $p(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{x^2}{2}}$.\nThe probability density function for $q(x) = N(1,1)$ is $q(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{(x-1)^2}{2}}$.\nWe need to compute $E[\\log q(X_1)]$, where $X_1 \\sim N(0,1)$.\n$E[\\log q(X_1)] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\log \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{(x-1)^2}{2}} \\right) p(x) dx$\n$= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\left( \\log \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} - \\frac{(x-1)^2}{2} \\right) \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{x^2}{2}} dx$\n$= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\left( -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{x^2 - 2x + 1}{2} \\right) \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{x^2}{2}} dx$\n$= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} p(x) dx - \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (x^2 - 2x + 1) p(x) dx + \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (x^2 - 2x + 1) p(x) dx$\n$= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{1}{2} E[X_1^2 - 2X_1 + 1]$\nSince $X_1 \\sim N(0,1)$, $E[X_1] = 0$ and $E[X_1^2] = Var(X_1) + (E[X_1])^2 = 1 + 0^2 = 1$.\nSo, $E[X_1^2 - 2X_1 + 1] = E[X_1^2] - 2E[X_1] + E[1] = 1 - 2(0) + 1 = 2$.\nTherefore, $E[\\log q(X_1)] = -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{1}{2}(2) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - 1$.\nThe limit we are looking for is $-E[\\log q(X_1)]$.\n$-E[\\log q(X_1)] = -(-\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - 1) = \\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) + 1$.\nUsing $\\pi \\approx 3.14159$, $\\log(2\\pi) \\approx \\log(6.28318) \\approx 1.837877$.\nSo, $\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) + 1 \\approx \\frac{1}{2}(1.837877) + 1 \\approx 0.9189385 + 1 \\approx 1.9189385$.\n\nLet's recheck the question. It asks for $\\lim -\\frac{{1}}{{n}}\\log{{q(X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n)}}$.\nThis is $-E[\\log q(X_1)]$.\nThe expression for the limit is $E[\\log p(X_1)] - E[\\log q(X_1)]$. This is the Kullback-Leibler divergence $D_{KL}(p||q)$.\n$D_{KL}(p||q) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} p(x) \\log \\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} dx = E[\\log p(X_1)] - E[\\log q(X_1)]$.\n\n$E[\\log p(X_1)] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\log \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{x^2}{2}} \\right) \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{x^2}{2}} dx$\n$= \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\left( -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{x^2}{2} \\right) p(x) dx$\n$= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) E[1] - \\frac{1}{2} E[X_1^2]$\n$= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{1}{2}(1) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\n$E[\\log q(X_1)] = -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - 1$.\n\nSo, the limit is $D_{KL}(p||q) = E[\\log p(X_1)] - E[\\log q(X_1)]$\n$= (-\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{1}{2}) - (-\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - 1)$\n$= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) + 1$\n$= 1 - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.5$.\n\nThe question is to evaluate $\\lim -\\frac{{1}}{{n}}\\log{{q(X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n)}}$.\nThis is equal to $-E[\\log q(X_1)]$.\n$-E[\\log q(X_1)] = -(-\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - 1) = \\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) + 1$.\n$\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) + 1 \\approx 0.9189 + 1 = 1.9189$.\n\nLet's re-read the question carefully: \"Use natural logarithm to evaluate $\\lim -\\frac{{1}}{{n}}\\log{{q(X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n)}}$ as $n \\to \\infty$.\"\nWe have $X_i \\sim N(0,1)$, $p(x) = N(0,1)$, $q(x) = N(1,1)$.\n$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{1}{n} \\log q(X_1, \\ldots, X_n) = E[\\log q(X_1)]$ where $X_1 \\sim N(0,1)$.\nWe want to compute $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} -\\frac{1}{n} \\log q(X_1, \\ldots, X_n) = -E[\\log q(X_1)]$.\nWe calculated $E[\\log q(X_1)] = -\\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) - 1$.\nSo, $-E[\\log q(X_1)] = \\frac{1}{2}\\log(2\\pi) 