      "generated_answer": "Let $X_n$ be independent and exponentially distributed with parameter 1. We are interested in the probability $P(\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) = 1)$.\nWe can use the Borel-Cantelli lemma.\nFor any $\\epsilon > 0$, consider the event $A_\\epsilon = \\{X_n/\\log(n) > 1 + \\epsilon \\text{ for infinitely many } n\\}$. If we can show that $P(A_\\epsilon) = 0$ for all $\\epsilon > 0$, then $\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) \\le 1$ almost surely.\nAlso consider the event $B_\\epsilon = \\{X_n/\\log(n) < 1 - \\epsilon \\text{ for infinitely many } n\\}$. If we can show that $P(B_\\epsilon) = 0$ for all $\\epsilon > 0$, then $\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) \\ge 1$ almost surely.\n\nLet's analyze the tail probability of $X_n/\\log(n)$.\n$P(X_n/\\log(n) > 1 + \\epsilon) = P(X_n > (1+\\epsilon)\\log(n))$. Since $X_n \\sim \\text{Exp}(1)$, $P(X_n > x) = e^{-x}$.\nSo, $P(X_n > (1+\\epsilon)\\log(n)) = e^{-(1+\\epsilon)\\log(n)} = n^{-(1+\\epsilon)}$.\nThe sum $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty P(X_n/\\log(n) > 1 + \\epsilon) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n^{-(1+\\epsilon)}$. This is a convergent p-series since $1+\\epsilon > 1$.\nBy the first Borel-Cantelli lemma, $P(A_\\epsilon) = P(\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) > 1 + \\epsilon) = 0$ for all $\\epsilon > 0$.\nThis implies that $\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) \\le 1$ almost surely.\n\nNow consider the event $X_n/\\log(n) < 1 - \\epsilon$.\n$P(X_n/\\log(n) < 1 - \\epsilon) = P(X_n < (1-\\epsilon)\\log(n))$. Since $X_n \\sim \\text{Exp}(1)$, $P(X_n < x) = 1 - e^{-x}$.\nSo, $P(X_n < (1-\\epsilon)\\log(n)) = 1 - e^{-(1-\\epsilon)\\log(n)} = 1 - n^{-(1-\\epsilon)}$.\nFor large $n$, this probability is close to 1. The sum of these probabilities will diverge.\nThe second Borel-Cantelli lemma states that if $\\sum P(E_n) = \\infty$ and the events are independent, then $P(\\limsup E_n) = 1$.\nHere, the events $A_n = \\{X_n/\\log(n) < 1 - \\epsilon\\}$ are independent.\nAs $n \\to \\infty$, $P(X_n/\\log(n) < 1 - \\epsilon) = 1 - n^{-(1-\\epsilon)}$.\nThe sum $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty P(X_n/\\log(n) < 1 - \\epsilon) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (1 - n^{-(1-\\epsilon)})$.\nThis sum diverges since $1 - n^{-(1-\\epsilon)} \\to 1$ as $n \\to \\infty$.\nTherefore, $P(\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) < 1 - \\epsilon) = 1$ for any $\\epsilon > 0$.\nThis implies that $\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) \\ge 1$ almost surely.\n\nCombining the two results, we have $\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) \\le 1$ and $\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) \\ge 1$.\nTherefore, $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) = 1$ almost surely.\nThe probability $P(\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) = 1)$ is the probability that the limit exists and is equal to 1, which is 1.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{1.0}$.",
