    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7664",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the sum of $\\sum_{{n=1}}^{{\\infty}} \\frac{{2}}{{n^2 + 4n + 3}}$\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.6333\nB. 0.9333\nC. 2.0333\nD. 0.7333\nE. 0.8333\nF. 1.4333\nG. 2.1433\nH. 0.4333\nI. 0.5633\nJ. 1.2333\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the sum of the given series, we can use the method of partial fraction decomposition.\nFirst, factor the denominator: $n^2 + 4n + 3 = (n+1)(n+3)$.\nNow, decompose the term $\\frac{2}{n^2 + 4n + 3}$:\n$\\frac{2}{(n+1)(n+3)} = \\frac{A}{n+1} + \\frac{B}{n+3}$\nMultiplying both sides by $(n+1)(n+3)$, we get:\n$2 = A(n+3) + B(n+1)$\nIf $n = -1$, then $2 = A(-1+3) + B(-1+1) \\implies 2 = 2A \\implies A = 1$.\nIf $n = -3$, then $2 = A(-3+3) + B(-3+1) \\implies 2 = -2B \\implies B = -1$.\nSo, $\\frac{2}{n^2 + 4n + 3} = \\frac{1}{n+1} - \\frac{1}{n+3}$.\n\nNow, we can write the sum as a telescoping series:\n$\\sum _{{n=1}}^{{\\infty}} \\left( \\frac{1}{n+1} - \\frac{1}{n+3} \\right)$\nLet $S_N$ be the N-th partial sum:\n$S_N = \\sum _{{n=1}}^{N} \\left( \\frac{1}{n+1} - \\frac{1}{n+3} \\right)$\n$S_N = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{4} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{5} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{6} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{7} \\right) + \\dots + \\left( \\frac{1}{N} - \\frac{1}{N+2} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{N+1} - \\frac{1}{N+3} \\right)$\nThe terms $-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ cancel out.\nThe terms $-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ cancel out.\nThis pattern continues. The terms that do not cancel are the first two positive terms and the last two negative terms.\n$S_N = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{N+2} - \\frac{1}{N+3}$\n\nNow, we take the limit as $N \\to \\infty$:\n$\\lim _{{N \\to \\infty}} S_N = \\lim _{{N \\to \\infty}} \\left( \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{N+2} - \\frac{1}{N+3} \\right)$\nAs $N \\to \\infty$, $\\frac{1}{N+2} \\to 0$ and $\\frac{1}{N+3} \\to 0$.\nSo, the sum is $\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{3}{6} + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}$.\n\nNow, we convert the fraction $\\frac{5}{6}$ to a decimal:\n$\\frac{5}{6} \\approx 0.83333\\dots$\n\nComparing this value with the given options:\nA. 1.6333\nB. 0.9333\nC. 2.0333\nD. 0.7333\nE. 0.8333\nF. 1.4333\nG. 2.1433\nH. 0.4333\nI. 0.5633\nJ. 1.2333\n\nThe closest option is E. 0.8333.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$.",
