pprox \\frac{1}{r \\epsilon}$.\n\nConsider the behavior near $\\pi/2$.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx \\approx \\int_0^{\\pi/2} (\\pi/2)^r \\sin(x) dx \\approx (\\pi/2)^r \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\sin(x) dx = (\\pi/2)^r [-\\cos(x)]_0^{\\pi/2} = (\\pi/2)^r (0 - (-1)) = (\\pi/2)^r$.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx \\approx (\\pi/2)^r \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\cos(x) dx = (\\pi/2)^r [\\sin(x)]_0^{\\pi/2} = (\\pi/2)^r (1 - 0) = (\\pi/2)^r$.\nThe ratio would be $1/r$, which goes to 0.\n\nLet's use the result $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r f(x) dx \\sim \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1} f(\\pi/2)$ if $f(\\pi/2) \\ne 0$.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx \\sim \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1} \\sin(\\pi/2) = \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1}$.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx$. Since $\\cos(\\pi/2) = 0$, we need to consider the next term.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx = \\frac{1}{r+1} \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^{r+1} \\sin(x) dx \\sim \\frac{1}{r+1} \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+2}}{r+2}$. This is incorrect.\n\nConsider the ratio of the integrands near $\\pi/2$.\n$\\lim _{{r \\rightarrow \\infty}} \\frac{x^r \\sin(x)}{r x^r \\cos(x)} = \\lim _{{r \\rightarrow \\infty}} \\frac{\\sin(x)}{r \\cos(x)}$. Near $x=\\pi/2$, this approaches infinity.\n\nLet's consider $\\lim_{r \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin x \\, dx}{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos x \\, dx}$.\nThis limit is $\\frac{\\sin(\\pi/2)}{\\cos(\\pi/2)}$ when considering ratio of terms at $\\pi/2$.\nLet's analyze more carefully the asymptotic behavior.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin x dx \\sim (\\pi/2)^r \\sin(\\pi/2) \\int_0^\\epsilon (\\pi/2-u)^r du \\sim (\\pi/2)^r \\frac{\\pi/2}{r}$.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos x dx \\sim (\\pi/2)^r \\int_0^\\epsilon (\\pi/2-u)^r u du \\sim (\\pi/2)^r (\\pi/2/r)^2$.\n\nThe limit is $\\lim_{r \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{(\\pi/2)^r \\pi/2 / r}{r (\\pi/2)^r (\\pi/2/r)^2} = \\lim_{r \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{\\pi/2 / r}{r (\\pi/2)^2 / r^2} = \\lim_{r \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{\\pi/2 r}{r^2 (\\pi/2)^2} = \\lim_{r \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{r}{\\pi/2 r^2} = 0$. This is also not among options.\n\nConsider the ratio of integrands at $x=\\pi/2$. The question is asking for the limit of (Integral 1) / (r * Integral 2).\nThe ratio of $\\sin(x)/\\cos(x)$ as $x \\rightarrow \\pi/2$ approaches infinity.\nHowever, the $r$ in the denominator softens this.\n\nFinal thought process leads to considering the ratio of the values at the dominant point.\nThe dominant behavior is governed by $x^r$. For large $r$, the integrals are concentrated near $x=\\pi/2$.\nThe ratio of $\\sin(x)$ to $\\cos(x)$ as $x \\to \\pi/2$ is $\\frac{1}{0^+}$.\nLet $x = \\pi/2 - \\epsilon$.\n$\\lim_{\\epsilon \\to 0^+} \\frac{(\\pi/2-\\epsilon)^r \\sin(\\pi/2-\\epsilon)}{r (\\pi/2-\\epsilon)^r \\cos(\\pi/2-\\epsilon)} = \\lim_{\\epsilon \\to 0^+} \\frac{\\cos(\\epsilon)}{r \\sin(\\epsilon)}$.\n$\\sin(\\epsilon) \\approx \\epsilon$, $\\cos(\\epsilon) \\approx 1$.\nSo, $\\lim_{\\epsilon \\to 0^+} \\frac{1}{r \\epsilon}$. This depends on how fast $\\epsilon$ goes to 0 compared to $r$.\n\nThe value $2/\\pi \\approx 0.63662$.\nLet's consider the limit of the ratio of integrals of $x^r \\sin(x)$ and $x^r \\cos(x)$ without the $r$ in the denominator. This ratio is $\\lim_{r\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin x dx}{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos x dx}$.\nUsing integration by parts: $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos x dx = \\frac{1}{r+1} \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^{r+1} \\sin x dx$.\nSo the ratio is $\\lim_{r \\to \\infty} \\frac{(r+1) \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin x dx}{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^{r+1} \\sin x dx}$.\nThis is equal to $\\lim_{r \\to \\infty} \\frac{r+1}{\\pi/2} = \\infty$.\n\nThe problem is likely related to comparing $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx$ and $r \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx$.\nThe asymptotic behavior is $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx \\sim \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1}$ and $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx \\sim \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1} \\frac{1}{r}$. This is incorrect.\n\nA relevant result is that for large $r$, $\\int_0^a x^r f(x) dx \\sim \\frac{a^{r+1}}{r+1} f(a)$ if $f(a) \\ne 0$, and $\\frac{a^{r+2}}{r+1} \\frac{f'(a)}{2}$ if $f(a)=0$ and $f'(a) \\ne 0$.\nFor $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx$, $f(x) = \\cos(x)$, $f(\\pi/2) = 0$, $f'(x) = -\\sin(x)$, $f'(\\pi/2) = -1$.\nSo $\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx \\sim \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+2}}{r+1} \\frac{-1}{2}$. This integral should be positive.\n\nLet's stick to $\\frac{I_r}{r J_r} = \\frac{r+1}{r} \\frac{I_r}{I_{r+1}}$.\n$I_r \\sim (\\pi/2)^r \\sin(\\pi/2) \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\pi}{-r (\\ln x)''|_{x=\\pi/2} } }$ is not right.\n\nConsider the ratio of the terms $(\\pi/2)^r \\sin(\\pi/2)$ and $r (\\pi/2)^r \\cos(\\pi/2)$, if this was the whole story.\nThe limit is likely related to $1/(\\pi/2)$.\n\nConsider the value of $\\frac{\\sin(\\pi/2)}{\\cos(\\pi/2)}$. This is undefined.\nHowever, if we consider the ratio of the integrals, $\\lim_{r \\to \\infty} \\frac{\\int x^r \\sin x dx}{\\int x^r \\cos x dx} = \\infty$.\nThen $\\frac{\\infty}{r} \\to 0$.\n\nLet's reconsider the asymptotic expansion of the integrals.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx \\sim \\frac{(\\pi/2)^{r+1}}{r+1}$.\n$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx \\sim \\frac{(\\pi/2)^r}{r} \\sin(\\pi/2) (\\pi/2) \\times (\\text{something})$.\nThe limit is $2/\\pi \\approx 0.63662$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
