    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given series is a telescoping series. Let $a_n = \\cos(1/n^2)$. Then the series can be written as $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} (a_n - a_{n+1})$.\nThe partial sum is $S_N = \\sum_{n=1}^{N} (a_n - a_{n+1}) = (a_1 - a_2) + (a_2 - a_3) + \\dots + (a_N - a_{N+1}) = a_1 - a_{N+1}$.\nAs $N \\to \\infty$, $1/(N+1)^2 \\to 0$. Since the cosine function is continuous, $\\cos(1/(N+1)^2) \\to \\cos(0) = 1$.\nSo, the sum of the series is $\\lim_{N\\to\\infty} S_N = a_1 - \\lim_{N\\to\\infty} a_{N+1} = \\cos(1/1^2) - \\cos(0) = \\cos(1) - 1$.\nWe need to calculate the value of $\\cos(1) - 1$.\nUsing a calculator, $\\cos(1 \\text{ radian}) \\approx 0.540302$.\nTherefore, $\\cos(1) - 1 \\approx 0.540302 - 1 = -0.459698$.\nLooking at the options, -0.459 is the closest value. So, the answer is J.\n\nThe series is a telescoping sum. The partial sum $S_N = \\sum_{n=1}^{N} (\\cos(1/n^2) - \\cos(1/(n+1)^2)) = \\cos(1/1^2) - \\cos(1/(N+1)^2)$.\nAs $N \\to \\infty$, $1/(N+1)^2 \\to 0$, so $\\cos(1/(N+1)^2) \\to \\cos(0) = 1$.\nThus, the sum of the series is $\\lim_{N\\to\\infty} S_N = \\cos(1) - 1$.\nUsing a calculator, $\\cos(1 \\text{ radian}) \\approx 0.5403$.\nTherefore, the sum is approximately $0.5403 - 1 = -0.4597$.\nThe closest option is J. -0.459.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
