    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The set $E$ is defined by two conditions: $\\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\cos(x+y)$ is irrational. The set of points $(x, y)$ in $I = [0, 1] \\times [0, 1]$ where $\\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to $0 \\leq x < \\arcsin(\\frac{1}{2}) = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$. The measure of this part is $\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\times 1 = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\nNow consider the condition that $\\cos(x+y)$ is irrational. The set where $\\cos(x+y)$ is rational is a countable union of lines. Since the set of irrational numbers is dense and has full measure in $\\mathbb{R}$, the set where $\\cos(x+y)$ is irrational has full Lebesgue measure in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the Lebesgue measure of $E$ is the measure of the region where $0 \\leq x < \\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$, assuming the set of irrational values for $\\cos(x+y)$ doesn't reduce the measure within this strip. Since the set of points where $\\cos(x+y)$ is rational has measure zero, the set of points where it is irrational has full measure. Thus, the measure of $E$ is the measure of the region where $\\sin(x) < \\frac{1}{2}$ within $I$. This region is $[0, \\frac{\\pi}{6}) \\times [0, 1]$. The Lebesgue measure of this region is $(\\frac{\\pi}{6} - 0) \\times (1 - 0) = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\nCalculating the numerical value: $\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\approx \\frac{3.141592653589793}{6} \\approx 0.5235987755982988}$.\n\nComparing this value with the given options:\nA. 0.7853981634\nB. 1.0471975512\nC. 1.8849555922\nD. 0.6283185307\nE. 0.2617993833\nF. 2.0943951024\nG. 1.5707963268\nH. 0.5235987667\nI. 0.3926990817\nJ. 1.3089969390\n\nThe closest option to our calculated value is H. 0.5235987667.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
