    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given series is $\\sum_{{n=0}}^{{\\infty}} \\frac{{n!}}{{n^2 cos(n)}}$.\nFor this series to be defined, we must have $n^2 \\cos(n) \\neq 0$.\nHowever, the term for $n=0$ is $\\frac{0!}{0^2 \\cos(0)} = \\frac{1}{0}$, which is undefined.\nEven if we consider the series starting from $n=1$, the denominator can be zero when $\\cos(n) = 0$, which occurs when $n = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi$ for some integer $k$. Since $n$ is an integer and $\\pi$ is irrational, $n$ will never be equal to $\\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi$.\nHowever, the presence of $n!$ in the numerator grows much faster than $n^2$ in the denominator.\nWe can consider the limit of the absolute value of the terms:\n$|\\frac{n!}{n^2 \\cos(n)}| = \\frac{n!}{n^2 |\\cos(n)|}$.\nSince $|\\cos(n)| \\leq 1$, we have $|\\frac{n!}{n^2 \\cos(n)}| \\ge \\frac{n!}{n^2}$.\nAs $n \\to \\infty$, $\\frac{n!}{n^2} \\to \\infty$.\nBy the divergence test, if $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n \\neq 0$, the series diverges. In this case, the terms themselves grow infinitely large.\nTherefore, the series diverges.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
