    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6773",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For which p is the norm ||x||_p expressable as an inner product?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0\nB. p = 3\nC. p <= 2\nD. 2\nE. -1\nF. -2\nG. p >= 1\nH. 1\nI. infinity\nJ. p < 1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The norm of a vector can be expressed as an inner product if and only if the norm is derived from an inner product. For a vector space with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$, the induced norm is given by $\\|x\\| = \\sqrt{\\langle x, x \\rangle}$. A known theorem states that a norm $\\|x\\|$ is induced by an inner product if and only if it satisfies the parallelogram law: $\\|x+y\\|^2 + \\|x-y\\|^2 = 2(\\|x\\|^2 + \\|y\\|^2)$.\n\nLet's consider the $p$-norms. For $0 < p$, the $p$-norm in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is defined as $\\|x\\|_p = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n |x_i|^p\\right)^{1/p}$. This norm satisfies the parallelogram law if and only if $p=2$. The $p$-norm for $p=2$ corresponds to the Euclidean norm, which is indeed induced by the standard dot product. The $L_p$ spaces have a norm that is derived from an inner product only for $p=2$. For $p=\\infty$, the norm is $\\|x\\|_\\infty = \\max_i |x_i|$, which is also not induced by an inner product in general. For $p < 1$, the $p$-norm does not even satisfy the triangle inequality and is not a norm. The question asks for which $p$ the norm ||x||_p is expressable as an inner product. This refers to the case where the norm itself is induced by an inner product.\n\nThe $p$-norms are generally not derived from an inner product unless $p=2$. Therefore, the norm $||\\cdot||_p$ can be expressed as an inner product only when $p=2$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
