      "generated_answer": "The domain of a function is the set of all possible input values (x-values) for which the function is defined. For the given function $f(x)=\\frac{{x+6}}{{\\sqrt{{x^2-3x-4}}}}$, we have two conditions for the function to be defined. First, the expression under the square root must be non-negative, and second, the denominator cannot be zero. Since the square root is in the denominator, the expression under the square root must be strictly positive, $x^2-3x-4 > 0$.\n\nWe find the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-3x-4=0$ by factoring: $(x-4)(x+1)=0$. The roots are $x=4$ and $x=-1$. The quadratic expression $x^2-3x-4$ is a parabola opening upwards, so it is positive when $x$ is outside the roots. Therefore, $x^2-3x-4 > 0$ when $x < -1$ or $x > 4$.\n\nThus, the domain of the function is $(-\\infty, -1) \\cup (4, \\infty)$.\n\nComparing this with the given options, option H matches our result.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
