      "generated_answer": "Let $S_n = X_2 + X_3 + \\dots + X_n$. We are asked about the convergence in probability and almost surely of $S_n/n$.\n\nFirst, let's consider the expected value of $X_n$.\n$E[X_n] = n \\cdot P(X_n=n) + (-n) \\cdot P(X_n=-n) + 0 \\cdot P(X_n=0)$\n$E[X_n] = n \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n\\log(n)} - n \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n\\log(n)} + 0 = \\frac{1}{2\\log(n)} - \\frac{1}{2\\log(n)} = 0$.\nSince $E[X_n] = 0$ for all $n \\ge 2$, we have $E[S_n/n] = E[\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=2}^n X_i] = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=2}^n E[X_i] = 0$.\n\nNow let's consider the variance of $X_n$.\n$E[X_n^2] = n^2 \\cdot P(X_n=n) + (-n)^2 \\cdot P(X_n=-n) + 0^2 \\cdot P(X_n=0)$\n$E[X_n^2] = n^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n\\log(n)} + n^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n\\log(n)} + 0 = \\frac{n}{2\\log(n)} + \\frac{n}{2\\log(n)} = \\frac{n}{\\log(n)}$.\n$Var(X_n) = E[X_n^2] - (E[X_n])^2 = \\frac{n}{\\log(n)} - 0^2 = \\frac{n}{\\log(n)}$.\n\nFor convergence in probability, we can use Chebyshev's inequality.\n$P(|S_n/n - 0| > \\epsilon) = P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) = P(|S_n| > n\\epsilon)$\n$P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) = \\frac{Var(S_n/n)}{\\epsilon^2} = \\frac{Var(\\sum_{i=2}^n X_i / n)}{\\epsilon^2} = \\frac{(\\sum_{i=2}^n Var(X_i))/n^2}{\\epsilon^2} = \\frac{1}{n^2\\epsilon^2} \\sum_{i=2}^n Var(X_i)$.\n$\\sum_{i=2}^n Var(X_i) = \\sum_{i=2}^n \\frac{i}{\\log(i)}$.\nWe know that $\\sum_{i=2}^n \\frac{1}{\\log(i)}$ is approximated by $\\frac{n}{\\log(n)}$ (using integration $\\int_2^n \\frac{1}{\\log x} dx \\approx \\frac{n}{\\log n}$).\nHowever, $\\sum_{i=2}^n \\frac{i}{\\log(i)}$ grows faster than $n$.\nConsider using the Law of Large Numbers. If $E[X_n] = 0$ and $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} < \\infty$, then $S_n/n \\to 0$ a.s.\n$\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{n/\\log(n)}{n^2} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n\\log(n)}$.\nThe series $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n\\log(n)}$ diverges by the integral test ($\\int_2^\\infty \\frac{1}{x\\log x} dx = [\\log(\\log x)]_2^