} dx$ grows faster than $n^2$. For example, by L'Hopital's rule, $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\int_2^n \\frac{x}{\\log x} dx}{n^2} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\frac{n}{\\log n}}{2n} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{1}{2\\log n} = 0$.\nThis implies convergence in probability to 0 is false.\n\nLet's check the definition of convergence in probability again.\nIf $E[X_n] = 0$ and $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} < \\infty$, then $\\frac{S_n}{n} \\to 0$ a.s.\nWe have $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n\\log n}$ which diverges.\nSo we cannot conclude convergence a.s. from this.\n\nLet's consider the probability of $X_n$ being large.\n$P(|X_n| \\ge n/2) = P(X_n=n) + P(X_n=-n) = \\frac{1}{2n\\log n} + \\frac{1}{2n\\log n} = \\frac{1}{n\\log n}$.\n$\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty P(|X_n| \\ge n/2) = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n\\log n}$ diverges.\nBy the first Borel-Cantelli lemma, $X_n$ is such that $|X_n| \\ge n/2$ infinitely often almost surely.\nThis means that $X_n/n$ does not converge to 0.\nIf $X_n/n$ does not converge to 0, then it is unlikely that $S_n/n$ converges to anything.\n\nLet's go back to $P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) = \\frac{1}{n^2\\epsilon^2} \\sum_{i=2}^n Var(X_i) = \\frac{1}{n^2\\epsilon^2} \\sum_{i=2}^n \\frac{i}{\\log i}$.\nFor large $n$, $\\sum_{i=2}^n \\frac{i}{\\log i}$ behaves approximately as $\\int_2^n \\frac{x}{\\log x} dx$.\nWe know that $\\int_2^n \\frac{x}{\\log x} dx \\sim \\frac{n^2}{2\\log n}$.\nSo, $P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) \\sim \\frac{1}{n^2\\epsilon^2} \\frac{n^2}{2\\log n} = \\frac{1}{2\\epsilon^2 \\log n}$.\nAs $n \\to \\infty$, $\\frac{1}{2\\epsilon^2 \\log n} \\to 0$.\nThus, $n^{-1}\\sum_{i=2}^n X_i$ converges in probability to 0.\n\nFor almost sure convergence, we need the second Borel-Cantelli lemma for which we require the events to be independent. The $X_i$ are independent.\nWe need to check if $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) < \\infty$ for all $\\epsilon > 0$.\n$P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) \\sim \\frac{1}{2\\epsilon^2 \\log n}$.\nThe series $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2\\epsilon^2 \\log n}$ diverges since $\\sum \\frac{1}{\\log n}$ diverges.\nSo, we cannot conclude almost sure convergence to 0.\n\nLet's check if there are any other possible limits.\nConsider $X_n$ again. $P(X_n=n) = P(X_n=-n) = \\frac{1}{2n \\log n}$, $P(X_n=0) = 1 - \\frac{1}{n \\log n}$.\nWe have $E[X_n] = 0$.\n$E[|X_n|] = n \\frac{1}{2n \\log n} + n \\frac{1}{2n \\log n} = \\frac{1}{\\log n}$.\n$\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty E[|X_n|]$ diverges.\nBy the first Borel-Cantelli lemma, $P(|X_n| > 0 \\text{ i.o.}) = 1$. This is already true since $P(X_n \\ne 0) = \\frac{1}{n \\log n}$ and $\\sum \\frac{1}{n \\log n} = \\infty$.\n\nLet's check if $n^{-1} \\max_{2 \\le k \\le n} |X_k|$ goes to 0 in probability.\n$P(\\max_{2 \\le k \\le n} |X_k| > \\epsilon n) = P(\\cup_{k=2}^n \\{|X_k| > \\epsilon n\\})$.\nSince $P(|X_k| > \\epsilon k) = \\frac{1}{k \\log k}$ for $k \\ge 2$.\nIf $\\epsilon k < k$, then $P(|X_k| > \\epsilon n) = 0$. So we need $\\epsilon n \\le k$.\nFor $k=n$, $P(|X_n| > \\epsilon n) = 0$ if $\\epsilon > 1$. If $\\epsilon < 1$, $P(|X_n| > \\epsilon n) = P(|X_n|=n) + P(|X_n|=-n) = \\frac{1}{n \\log n}$.\n\nLet's reconfirm convergence in probability. We have shown $P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) \\sim \\frac{1}{2\\epsilon^2 \\log n} \\to 0$.\nSo, $n^{-1}\\sum_{i=2}^n X_i$ converges in probability to 0.\n\nFor almost sure convergence, consider the criteria for the strong law of large numbers for Cesaro means.\nLet $Y_i = X_i/i$. $E[Y_i] = E[X_i/i] = E[X_i]/i = 0$.\n$Var(Y_i) = Var(X_i)/i^2 = \\frac{i/\\log i}{i^2} = \\frac{1}{i\\log i}$.\nWe want to know convergence of $\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=2}^n Y_i = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=2}^n \\frac{X_i}{i}$.\nThis is not what we are asked. We are asked about $n^{-1}\\sum_{i=2}^n X_i$.\n\nLet's use Kolomogorov's criterion for almost sure convergence.\nIf $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} < \\infty$, then $S_n/n \\to 0$ a.s.\n$\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{n/\\log n}{n^2} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n\\log n}$ diverges.\n\nConsider another form of CLT.\n$P(S_n/n > 0) = P(\\sum X_i > 0)$.\nLet's try to find an example where this happens.\n\nLet's check the options. If it converges in probability to 0, then the first element is 0.\nWe found convergence in probability to 0. So the first element is 0.\nThis narrows down the options to C, F, J.\n\nNow we need to determine almost sure convergence.\nLet's use a theorem by Stout: Let $X_n$ be a sequence of independent random variables with $E[X_n]=0$. If $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{E[X_n^2]}{n^2} < \\infty$, then $\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\to 0$ a.s.\nWe have $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{E[X_n^2]}{n^2} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{n/\\log n}{n^2} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n\\log n}$ which diverges.\nSo, this theorem does not apply directly.\n\nLet's check for a non-zero limit.\nConsider the case where $(S_n/n)$ converges in probability to $c$. Then $E[S_n/n] \\to c$.\nWe have $E[S_n/n] = 0$. So if it converges in probability, it must converge to 0.\n\nConsider the case where $n^{-1}\\sum_{i=2}^n X_i$ converges in probability to 0. This means the first answer is 0.\nNow, does it converge almost surely?\nGiven $P(X_n=n)=P(X_n=-n)=1/(2n\\log (n)), P(X_n=0)=1-1/(n*\\log(n))$.\nWe know $\\sum P(X_n \\neq 0) = \\sum \\frac{1}{n\\log n} = \\infty$.\nSo by Borel-Cantelli, $X_n \\neq 0$ i.o. almost surely.\nThis indicates that the terms $X_n$ do not vanish quickly enough for almost sure convergence of the sum scaled by $n$.\n\nLet's use the result from Feller, Vol. II, Chapter IX, Section 6, Theorem 2. If $X_n$ are independent with $E[X_n]=0$ and $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\frac{E[X_n^2]}{n^2} = \\infty$, and if $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty P(|X_n| > n) < \\infty$, then $\\frac{S_n}{n}$ tends to a limit distribution, which can be degenerate (i.e., a point mass).\n\nLet's check $P(|X_n| > n) = 0$. So this condition is met.\nWe have $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{E[X_n^2]}{n^2} = \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n\\log n} = \\infty$.\nThis suggests that $\\frac{S_n}{n}$ might not converge to 0 almost surely.\n\nConsider a simplified case: $P(X_n=n) = P(X_n=-n) = 1/(2n)$, $P(X_n=0) = 1-1/n$.\n$E[X_n] = 0$. $E[X_n^2] = n$. $Var(X_n) = n$.\n$\\sum \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} = \\sum \\frac{n}{n^2} = \\sum \\frac{1}{n}$ diverges.\n$P(|X_n|=n) = 1/n$. $\\sum P(|X_n|=n) = \\sum 1/n$ diverges.\nIn this case, $S_n/n$ does not converge in probability or almost surely.\n\nHowever, for our problem, the probabilities are $\\frac{1}{2n\\log n}$.\nWe showed convergence in probability to 0.\nFor almost sure convergence, since $\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} = \\infty$, it is likely that it does not converge almost surely.\n\nLet's assume the answer is [0, 0]. This corresponds to option J.\nConvergence in probability to 0 is shown.\nFor almost sure convergence, we refer to results that state if $\\sum Var(X_n)/n^2$ diverges, then $S_n/n$ does not necessarily converge a.s.\n\nFinal check for convergence in probability:\n$P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) = \\frac{1}{n^2\\epsilon^2} \\sum_{i=2}^n Var(X_i) = \\frac{1}{n^2\\epsilon^2} \\sum_{i=2}^n \\frac{i}{\\log i}$.\nUsing integral approximation: $\\sum_{i=2}^n \\frac{i}{\\log i} \\approx \\int_2^n \\frac{x}{\\log x} dx$.\nWe know $\\int_2^n \\frac{x}{\\log x} dx \\sim \\frac{n^2}{2\\log n}$.\nSo $P(|S_n/n| > \\epsilon) \\sim \\frac{1}{n^2\\epsilon^2} \\frac{n^2}{2\\log n} = \\frac{1}{2\\epsilon^2 \\log n} \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$.\nSo, convergence in probability to 0 is correct.\n\nFor almost sure convergence, if $\\sum \\frac{Var(X_n)}{n^2} = \\infty$, it is plausible that there is no almost sure convergence to a constant.\n\nThe answer should be [0, 0].\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
