    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6703",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: suppose the sequence a_n satisfies 0<a_n<1, and $(1-a_n)a_{{n+1}}>1/4$ for all n, what is the limit of a_n as n goes to infinity?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.33\nB. 0.75\nC. 0.5\nD. 0.25\nE. 2/3\nF. The sequence does not converge\nG. 1/n as n goes to infinity\nH. 0.6\nI. 0.8\nJ. 1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the given inequality be $(1-a_n)a_{n+1} > 1/4$.\nWe are also given that $0 < a_n < 1$ for all $n$.\nIf the sequence $a_n$ converges to a limit $L$, then as $n \\to \\infty$, we have $a_{n+1} \\to L$ and $a_n \\to L$.\nSubstituting $L$ into the inequality, we get $(1-L)L > 1/4$.\nThis inequality can be rewritten as $L - L^2 > 1/4$, or $0 > L^2 - L + 1/4$.\nMultiplying by -1, we get $L^2 - L + 1/4 < 0$.\nThis is equivalent to $(L - 1/2)^2 < 0$.\nHowever, the square of any real number is always non-negative, so $(L - 1/2)^2$ cannot be less than 0.\nThis implies that our assumption that the sequence converges to a limit $L$ must be incorrect, or that there is no such limit.\nTherefore, the sequence does not converge.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
