    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The domain of a function is the set of all possible input values (x-values) for which the function is defined. For the function $h(x) = \\sqrt{{25-x^2}}+\\sqrt{{-(x-2)}}$ to be defined, both square roots must yield a non-negative result. Therefore, we must have $25-x^2 \\ge 0$ and $-(x-2) \\ge 0$.\n\nThe inequality $25-x^2 \\ge 0$ implies $x^2 \\le 25$, which means $-5 \\le x \\le 5$.\nThe inequality $-(x-2) \\ge 0$ implies $x-2 \\le 0$, which means $x \\le 2$.\n\nFor both conditions to be met simultaneously, we need $x$ to be in the intersection of the intervals $[-5, 5]$ and $(-\\infty, 2]$. The intersection of these two intervals is $[-5, 2]$.\n\nThe domain of the function $h(x)$ is the interval $[-5, 2]$. The width of this interval is the difference between the upper and lower bounds: $2 - (-5) = 2 + 5 = 7$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{J}$.",
