    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6912",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the greatest common divisor of $2^{{1001}}-1$ and $2^{{1012}}-1$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1024\nB. 2049\nC. 2048\nD. 4096\nE. 2\nF. 4095\nG. 1\nH. 511\nI. 2047\nJ. 2050\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The greatest common divisor (GCD) of $2^a - 1$ and $2^b - 1$ is equal to $2^{\\text{gcd}(a, b)} - 1$.\nIn this case, we need to find the GCD of $2^{1001} - 1$ and $2^{1012} - 1$.\nFirst, we find the GCD of the exponents 1001 and 1012.\nWe can use the Euclidean algorithm to find gcd(1001, 1012):\n$1012 = 1 \\cdot 1001 + 11$\n$1001 = 91 \\cdot 11 + 0$\nSo, gcd(1001, 1012) = 11.\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of $2^{1001} - 1$ and $2^{1012} - 1$ is $2^{11} - 1$.\nNow, we calculate $2^{11} - 1$:\n$2^{10} = 1024$\n$2^{11} = 2 \\cdot 2^{10} = 2 \\cdot 1024 = 2048$\n$2^{11} - 1 = 2048 - 1 = 2047$.\n\nComparing this result with the given options:\nA. 1024\nB. 2049\nC. 2048\nD. 4096\nE. 2\nF. 4095\nG. 1\nH. 511\nI. 2047\nJ. 2050\n\nThe greatest common divisor is 2047, which corresponds to option I.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
