      "generated_answer": "Let $n$ be the number of sides of a convex polygon. The sum of the interior angles of a convex $n$-gon is given by the formula $(n-2) \\times 180^\\circ$. For a 10-gon, $n=10$, so the sum of the interior angles is $(10-2) \\times 180^\\circ = 8 \\times 180^\\circ = 1440^\\circ$.\n\nAn acute angle is an angle less than $90^\\circ$.\nA convex polygon has all interior angles less than $180^\\circ$.\n\nIf a convex $n$-gon has $k$ acute angles, then the remaining $n-k$ angles must be obtuse (greater than $90^\\circ$ and less than $180^\\circ$) or right (equal to $90^\\circ$).\nLet the $n$ interior angles be $\\alpha_1, \\alpha_2, \\dots, \\alpha_n$. We know that $0 < \\alpha_i < 180^\\circ$ for all $i=1, \\dots, n$.\nThe sum of the exterior angles of a convex polygon is always $360^\\circ$. The exterior angle at vertex $i$ is $180^\\circ - \\alpha_i$.\n\nIf an interior angle $\\alpha_i$ is acute, i.e., $\\alpha_i < 90^\\circ$, then the corresponding exterior angle $180^\\circ - \\alpha_i > 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 90^\\circ$.\nIf an interior angle $\\alpha_i$ is obtuse or right, i.e., $\\alpha_i \\ge 90^\\circ$, then the corresponding exterior angle $180^\\circ - \\alpha_i \\le 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 90^\\circ$.\n\nConsider the sum of the exterior angles: $\\sum_{i=1}^n (180^\\circ - \\alpha_i) = 360^\\circ$.\nIf we have $k$ acute interior angles, then these contribute more than $90^\\circ$ to the sum of exterior angles. Let these be $\\alpha_1, \\dots, \\alpha_k$. So $180^\\circ - \\alpha_i > 90^\\circ$ for $i=1, \\dots, k$.\nThe sum of these $k$ exterior angles is $\\sum_{i=1}^k (180^\\circ - \\alpha_i) > k \\times 90^\\circ$.\n\nThe remaining $n-k$ interior angles $\\alpha_{k+1}, \\dots, \\alpha_n$ must be $\\ge 90^\\circ$ (since they are not acute).\nThe corresponding exterior angles $180^\\circ - \\alpha_i \\le 90^\\circ$.\n\nIf we have $k$ acute interior angles, then the sum of these $k$ acute interior angles is $\\sum_{i=1}^k \\alpha_i