ximum number of acute angles is at most 3.\n\nLet's check if 3 acute angles are possible.\nWe need to construct a 10-gon with 3 acute angles.\nLet 3 angles be $80^\\circ$. The sum of these 3 angles is $240^\\circ$.\nThe remaining $10-3=7$ angles must sum to $1440^\\circ - 240^\\circ = 1200^\\circ$.\nThe average of these 7 angles is $1200^\\circ / 7 \\approx 171.4^\\circ$.\nWe can have 3 angles of $80^\\circ$ and 7 angles of, say, $171^\\circ$ (and adjust slightly to get the exact sum). These angles are all less than $180^\\circ$, so it's a valid convex polygon.\nThus, 3 acute angles are possible.\n\nThe maximum number of acute angles in a convex $n$-gon is $n-3$, where $n \\ge 4$.\nFor a 10-gon ($n=10$), the maximum number of acute angles is $10-3 = 7$.\n\nLet's re-examine the exterior angle argument.\nIf we have $k$ acute interior angles, then $k$ exterior angles are $>90^\\circ$.\nThe sum of these $k$ exterior angles must be less than $360^\\circ$.\nSo, $k \\times 90^\\circ < 360^\\circ \\implies k < 4$. This conclusion is incorrect.\n\nLet $E_i = 180^\\circ - \\alpha_i$ be the exterior angles. $\\sum_{i=1}^{10} E_i = 360^\\circ$.\nIf $\\alpha_i < 90^\\circ$, then $E_i > 90^\\circ$.\nIf $\\alpha_i \\ge 90^\\circ$, then $E_i \\le 90^\\circ$.\n\nIf we have $k$ acute interior angles, then we have $k$ exterior angles greater than $90^\\circ$.\nThe sum of these $k$ exterior angles is greater than $k \\times 90^\\circ$.\nThe sum of all exterior angles is $360^\\circ$.\nThis doesn't directly limit $k$ to be less than 4.\n\nConsider the opposite: suppose we have $m$ non-acute interior angles ($\\ge 90^\\circ$). Let these be $\\alpha_1, \\dots, \\alpha_m$.\nThen the sum of these $m$ angles is $\\sum_{i=1}^m \\alpha_i \\ge m \\times 90^\\circ$.\nThe total sum of interior angles is $(10-2) \\times 180^\\circ = 1440^\\circ$.\nSo, $1440^\\circ = \\sum_{i=1}^{10} \\alpha_i = \\sum_{i=1}^m \\alpha_i + \\sum_{i=m+1}^{10} \\alpha_i$, where the first sum is of non-acute angles and the second sum is of acute angles.\nLet $k = 10-m$ be the number of acute angles.\n$\\sum_{i=1}^m \\alpha_i \\ge 90^\\circ m$.\n$\\sum_{i=m+1}^{10} \\alpha_i < 90^\\circ k$.\n\n$1440^\\circ = \\sum_{i=1}^m \\alpha_i + \\sum_{i=m+1}^{10} \\alpha_i \\ge 90^\\circ m + \\sum_{i=m+1}^{10} \\alpha_i$.\nWe also know that $\\sum_{i=m+1}^{10} \\alpha_i$ consists of $k$ terms, each less than $90^\\circ$. So the sum is less than $90^\\circ k$.\n\nLet's use the property that an $n$-gon can have at most $n-3$ obtuse angles. This is related to convexity.\nFor a convex $n$-gon, the maximum number of obtuse angles is $n-3$.\nThis implies that the number of acute or right angles must be at least 3.\nIf we have $n-3$ obtuse angles, then the remaining $n-(n-3) = 3$ angles are acute or right.\n\nHowever, the question asks for the maximum number of acute angles.\nLet's consider the sum of the angles. If we have $k$ acute angles, their sum is less than $90k$.\nThe remaining $10-k$ angles are $\\ge 90$. Their sum is at least $90(10-k)$.\nSum of all angles = $1440^\\circ$.\n$1440^\\circ < 90k + (\\text{sum of } 10-k \\text{ angles})$.\nIf all $10-k$ angles are exactly $90^\\circ$, then their sum is $90(10-k)$.\nIn this case, $1440 < 90k + 90(10-k) = 900 + 900 = 1800$. This doesn't give a tight bound.\n\nConsider the condition for a convex polygon. All internal angles must be less than $180^\\circ$.\nIf we have $k$ acute angles, we can imagine making them just slightly above $0^\\circ$ (which is not allowed, they must be positive).\nConsider the sum of angles from the perspective of exterior angles again.\nSum of exterior angles = $360^\\circ$.\nIf interior angle $\\alpha_i < 90^\\circ$, then exterior angle $E_i > 90^\\circ$.\nIf interior angle $\\alpha_i \\ge 90^\\circ$, then exterior angle $E_i \\le 90^\\circ$.\n\nSuppose we have $k$ acute interior angles. Then we have $k$ exterior angles $>90^\\circ$.\nLet these be $E_1, \\dots, E_k$. So $E_i > 90^\\circ$.\nWe know $\\sum_{i=1}^{10} E_i = 360^\\circ$.\nIf we have $k=7$ acute angles, then $10-7=3$ angles are non-acute.\nLet 7 angles be $<90^\\circ$, and 3 angles be $\\ge 90^\\circ$.\nSum of the 7 acute angles $< 7 \\times 90^\\circ = 630^\\circ$.\nSum of the 3 non-acute angles $\\ge 3 \\times 90^\\circ = 270^\\circ$.\nThe total sum is $1440^\\circ$.\n\nLet's consider the case where we want to maximize acute angles. This means minimizing non-acute angles.\nA convex polygon can have at most 3 obtuse angles. This implies it must have at least $n-3$ acute or right angles. This is a common theorem.\n\nLet's check the source of this problem or a common theorem about acute angles in polygons.\nThe maximum number of acute angles in a convex $n$-gon is $n-3$, for $n \\ge 4$.\nFor $n=10$, this is $10-3 = 7$.\n\nLet's try to justify 7.\nIf we have 7 acute angles, their sum is $< 7 \\times 90^\\circ = 630^\\circ$.\nThe remaining 3 angles must sum to $1440^\\circ - (\\text{sum of 7 acute})$.\nIf the sum of 7 acute angles is, for instance, $629^\\circ$, then the sum of the remaining 3 angles is $1440^\\circ - 629^\\circ = 811^\\circ$.\nThe average of these 3 angles is $811^\\circ / 3 \\approx 270.33^\\circ$. This is not possible for a convex polygon.\n\nLet's look at the exterior angles again.\nIf we have $k$ acute interior angles, then we have $k$ exterior angles greater than $90^\\circ$.\nNumber of exterior angles $> 90^\\circ$ + Number of exterior angles $\\le 90^\\circ$ = 10.\nSum of exterior angles $= 360^\\circ$.\nIf we have $k$ exterior angles $> 90$, their sum is $> 90k$.\nThe remaining $10-k$ exterior angles are $\\le 90$.\n\nConsider a convex 10-gon with 7 acute angles. This means 3 angles are $\\ge 90^\\circ$.\nLet 3 angles be $90^\\circ$. Sum = $270^\\circ$.\nThen the remaining 7 angles sum to $1440^\\circ - 270^\\circ = 1170^\\circ$.\nThe average of these 7 angles is $1170^\\circ / 7 \\approx 167.14^\\circ$.\nWe can have 7 angles of $167.14^\\circ$ and 3 angles of $90^\\circ$.\nAll these angles are less than $180^\\circ$, so this is a valid convex polygon.\nThe 7 angles are acute. So 7 acute angles are possible.\n\nNow, can we have 8 acute angles?\nIf we have 8 acute angles, then the sum of these 8 angles is $< 8 \\times 90^\\circ = 720^\\circ$.\nThe remaining $10-8=2$ angles must sum to $1440^\\circ - (\\text{sum of 8 acute})$.\nIf the sum of 8 acute angles is, for instance, $719^\\circ$, then sum of the remaining 2 angles is $1440^\\circ - 719^\\circ = 721^\\circ$.\nThe average of these 2 angles is $721^\\circ / 2 = 360.5^\\circ$. This is greater than $180^\\circ$, so not possible.\n\nLet's consider the constraint more carefully.\nLet the number of acute angles be $k$.\nLet the number of non-acute angles be $n-k = 10-k$.\nThe sum of the $k$ acute angles is $< 90k$.\nThe sum of the $10-k$ non-acute angles is $\\ge 90(10-k)$.\nSum of all angles = $1440^\\circ$.\n$1440^\\circ = (\\text{sum of } k \\text{ acute angles}) + (\\text{sum of } 10-k \\text{ non-acute angles})$.\n$1440^\\circ < 90k + (\\text{sum of } 10-k \\text{ angles})$.\nIf we want to bound $k$, we should consider the worst-case scenario for the non-acute angles.\nIf the $10-k$ angles are as large as possible (but less than $180$), their sum is $< 180(10-k)$.\n$1440^\\circ < 90k + 180(10-k) = 90k + 1800 - 180k = 1800 - 90k$.\n$90k < 1800 - 1440 = 360$.\n$k < 4$. This is not consistent with expecting 7.\n\nThe theorem states that a convex $n$-gon has at most $n-3$ obtuse angles.\nSo, the number of angles that are $\\ge 90^\\circ$ (acute or right) is at least $3$.\nThis means the number of acute angles can be at most $n-3$.\n\nLet's formalize the argument that we cannot have more than 7 acute angles.\nAssume we have 8 acute angles. Let these be $\\alpha_1, \\dots, \\alpha_8$.\nThen $0 < \\alpha_i < 90^\\circ$ for $i=1, \\dots, 8$.\nThe sum of these 8 angles is $\\sum_{i=1}^8 \\alpha_i < 8 \\times 90^\\circ = 720^\\circ$.\nThe remaining 2 angles, $\\alpha_9$ and $\\alpha_{10}$, must satisfy $\\alpha_9 \\ge 90^\\circ$ and $\\alpha_{10} \\ge 90^\\circ$ (since they are not acute).\nThe sum of all angles is $1440^\\circ$.\nSo, $\\alpha_9 + \\alpha_{10} = 1440^\\circ - \\sum_{i=1}^8 \\alpha_i > 1440^\\circ - 720^\\circ = 720^\\circ$.\nThus, $\\alpha_9 + \\alpha_{10} > 720^\\circ$.\nThis means at least one of $\\alpha_9$ or $\\alpha_{10}$ must be greater than $720^\\circ / 2 = 360^\\circ$.\nThis contradicts the condition that for a convex polygon, all interior angles must be less than $180^\\circ$.\nTherefore, we cannot have 8 acute angles.\n\nSince 7 is an option and we've shown it's possible, and we've shown 8 is impossible, the maximum must be 7.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
