    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | Every free abelian group is torsion free. Statement 2 | Every finitely generated torsion-free abelian group is a free abelian group.\n\nOptions: \nA. Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is not provided\nB. Statement 1 is True, Statement 2 is both True and False\nC. False, False\nD. Statement 1 is not provided, Statement 2 is True\nE. True, False\nF. Both statements are not provided\nG. False, True\nH. Statement 1 is False, Statement 2 is not provided\nI. True, True\nJ. Statement 1 is not provided, Statement 2 is False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Statement 1: A free abelian group is a direct sum of copies of $\\mathbb{Z}$. The elements of $\\mathbb{Z}$ have no torsion, meaning $n \\cdot x = 0$ implies $x = 0$ for any non-zero integer $n$. Therefore, any free abelian group, being a direct sum of such groups, is also torsion-free. So, Statement 1 is True.\n\nStatement 2: The structure theorem for finitely generated abelian groups states that any finitely generated abelian group is isomorphic to a direct sum of cyclic groups of the form $\\mathbb{Z}$ and $\\mathbb{Z}_n$ (cyclic groups of order $n$). A torsion-free abelian group has no elements of finite order. If a finitely generated abelian group is torsion-free, then all the cyclic summands of the form $\\mathbb{Z}_n$ must be absent. This leaves only direct sums of copies of $\\mathbb{Z}$, which are precisely the free abelian groups. Thus, Statement 2 is True.\n\nSince both statements are True, option I is the correct choice.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
