      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the limit of $\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!}$ as $n \\to \\infty$, we can expand the double factorials.\n$(2n)!! = (2n)(2n-2)(2n-4) \\dots 2$\n$(2n+1)!! = (2n+1)(2n-1)(2n-3) \\dots 1$\n\nSo, $\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{(2n)(2n-2)(2n-4) \\dots 2}{(2n+1)(2n-1)(2n-3) \\dots 1}$.\n\nWe can rewrite this as:\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2n}{2n+1} \\cdot \\frac{2n-2}{2n-1} \\cdot \\frac{2n-4}{2n-3} \\dots \\frac{2}{1}$.\n\nLet's consider the limit of each term as $n \\to \\infty$:\n$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{2n}{2n+1} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{2}{2 + \\frac{1}{n}} = \\frac{2}{2+0} = 1$.\nSimilarly, for other terms, $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{2k}{2k+1} = 1$ for any fixed $k$.\n\nHowever, we have an infinite product of terms that approach 1. To evaluate this, we can use Wallis' product formula, which states that:\n$\\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\frac{2n}{2n-1} \\cdot \\frac{2n}{2n+1}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{6}{5} \\cdot \\frac{6}{7}\\right) \\dots$\n\nOur expression is $\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\dots \\frac{2n}{2n-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n+1}$. This is not exactly Wallis' product.\n\nLet's rewrite the expression in a different way by squaring it:\n$\\left( \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\dots (2n)}{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\dots (2n+1)} \\right)^2 = \\frac{2^2 \\cdot 4^2 \\cdot 6^2 \\dots (2n)^2}{1^2 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5^2 \\dots (2n+1)^2}$\n$= \\left( \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\dots (2n)}{1 \\cdot 3 \\dots (2n-1)} \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\dots (2n)}{1 \\cdot 3 \\dots (2n-1)} \\right)$\n$= \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} \\cdot \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n-1)!!}$ (this is incorrect manipulation)\n\nLet's analyze the terms more closely:\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\dots \\frac{2n}{2n-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n+1}$. This is incorrect.\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\dots \\frac{2n}{2n-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n+1}$. This is still incorrect.\n\nThe correct expansion is:\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\dots (2n)}{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\dots (2n+1)} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n)}{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n-1)} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n+1}$ (this is incorrect)\n\nLet's rewrite the expression as:\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2}{2n+1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{2n-1} \\cdot \\frac{6}{2n-3} \\dots \\frac{2n}{1}$. This is incorrect.\n\nLet's go back to:\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2 \\times 4 \\times \\cdots \\times (2n)}{1 \\times 3 \\times \\cdots \\times (2n+1)}$\nWe can pair terms:\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right) \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right) \\cdots \\left(\\frac{2n}{2n-1}\\right) \\frac{1}{2n+1}$ (still incorrect)\n\nLet's consider the ratio of consecutive terms in Wallis' product:\nWallis' product: $\\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\cdot \\frac{6}{7} \\cdots$\nConsider the sequence $a_n = \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!}$.\n$a_1 = \\frac{2!!}{3!!} = \\frac{2}{1 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{2}{3}$.\n$a_2 = \\frac{4!!}{5!!} = \\frac{4 \\cdot 2}{5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{8}{15}$.\n$a_3 = \\frac{6!!}{7!!} = \\frac{6 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2}{7 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{48}{105} = \\frac{16}{35}$.\n\nLet's consider $\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 2n}{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdots (2n-1) \\cdot (2n+1)}$.\nWe have $(2n)!! = 2^n n!$.\nAnd $(2n+1)!! = \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(2n)!!} = \\frac{(2n+1)!}{2^n n!}$.\nSo, $\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2^n n!}{\\frac{(2n+1)!}{2^n n!}} = \\frac{2^{2n} (n!)^2}{(2n+1)!}$.\n\nUsing Stirling's approximation, $n! \\approx \\sqrt{2\\pi n} (\\frac{n}{e})^n$.\nSo, $\\frac{