s \\cdot (2n+1)}$\n$= \\frac{2}{2n+1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{2n-1} \\cdot \\dots \\cdot \\frac{2n}{1}$. Incorrect.\n\nLet's consider the ratio of consecutive terms:\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\cdots \\frac{2n}{2n-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n+1}$. Incorrect.\n\nLet's analyze the terms:\n$\\frac{2n}{2n+1} < 1$.\n$\\frac{2n-2}{2n-1} < 1$.\nSo the product of these terms multiplied by $\\frac{2}{1}$ will be less than 1.\n\nConsider the Wallis product:\n$\\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{(2n)^2}{(2n-1)(2n+1)} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 2}{1 \\cdot 3} \\cdot \\frac{4 \\cdot 4}{3 \\cdot 5} \\cdot \\frac{6 \\cdot 6}{5 \\cdot 7} \\dots$\n\nOur expression is $\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n)}{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n+1)}$.\nSquare it:\n$\\left(\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n)}{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n+1)}\\right)^2 = \\frac{2^2 \\cdot 4^2 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n)^2}{1^2 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n+1)^2}$\n$= \\frac{(2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n))^2}{(1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot (2n-1))^2 (2n+1)^2} = \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n-1)!!} \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} \\frac{1}{(2n+1)^2}$. This is incorrect.\n\nConsider $\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\cdots \\frac{2n}{2n-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2n+1}$. This is still incorrect.\n\nLet $P_n = \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!}$.\nConsider the ratio $P_n / P_{n-1}$:\n$\\frac{P_n}{P_{n-1}} = \\frac{(2n)!!/(2n+1)!!}{(2n-2)!!/(2n-1)!!} = \\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n-2)!!} \\frac{(2n-1)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = (2n) \\frac{1}{2n+1} = \\frac{2n}{2n+1}$.\nSo, $P_n = P_{n-1} \\frac{2n}{2n+1}$.\n$P_0 = 1$. (empty product) or $P_1 = 2/3$. Let's start with $n=1$.\n$P_1 = \\frac{2}{3}$.\n$P_2 = P_1 \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{2}{3} \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{8}{15}$.\n$P_3 = P_2 \\frac{6}{7} = \\frac{8}{15} \\frac{6}{7} = \\frac{48}{105} = \\frac{16}{35}$.\n\nWe have $P_n =