al.\n\nConsider the reciprocal:\n$\\frac{(2n+1)!!}{(2n)!!} = \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\cdot \\dots \\cdot \\frac{2n+1}{2n}$. This goes to infinity.\n\nLet's use an alternative approach.\n$\\frac{(2n)!!}{(2n+1)!!} = \\frac{1}{\\binom{2n}{n}} \\frac{2^{2n}}{2n+1}$.\nUsing Stirling's approximation for $\\binom{2n}{n} \\approx \\frac{4^n}{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}$.\nSo, $\\frac{1}{\\binom{2n}{n}} \\frac{2^{2n}}{2n+1} \\approx \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}{4^n} \\frac{4^n}{2n+1} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}{2n+1}$.\nAs $n \\to \\infty$, $\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}{2n+1} \\approx \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}{2n} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2\\sqrt{n}} \\to 0$.\n\nThe limit of $\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}{2n+1}$ as $n \\to \\infty$ is found by dividing numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{n}$:\n$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2\\sqrt{n} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{\\infty} = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the limit is 0.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
