    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement.\n\nStatement 1: \"Every integral domain with characteristic 0 is infinite.\"\nAn integral domain with characteristic 0 contains a subring isomorphic to the integers, $\\mathbb{Z}$. Since $\\mathbb{Z}$ is infinite, any integral domain containing it must also be infinite. Thus, Statement 1 is true.\n\nStatement 2: \"Every integral domain with prime characteristic is finite.\"\nConsider the field of integers modulo a prime $p$, denoted by $\\mathbb{Z}_p$. This field is an integral domain with characteristic $p$ (a prime characteristic). However, $\\mathbb{Z}_p$ is a finite set with $p$ elements. So, this statement might be true. Let's consider another case. What about polynomial rings over finite fields? For example, $\\mathbb{Z}_p[x]$ is an integral domain, and its characteristic is $p$. But $\\mathbb{Z}_p[x]$ is infinite as it contains infinitely many polynomials. Thus, Statement 2 is false.\n\nTherefore, Statement 1 is true, and Statement 2 is false. This corresponds to option I.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
