    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6526",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | The unity of a subring must be the same as the unity of the ring. Statement 2 | Every field is an integral domain.\n\nOptions: \nA. True, True\nB. Not Enough Information, True\nC. False, True\nD. Not Enough Information, False\nE. False, False\nF. True, False\nG. True, True if the field is finite\nH. Not Enough Information, Not Enough Information\nI. True, Not Enough Information\nJ. False, Not Enough Information\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement.\n\nStatement 1: \"The unity of a subring must be the same as the unity of the ring.\"\nThis statement is false. A subring does not necessarily have the same unity element as the larger ring. For example, consider the ring of integers $\\mathbb{Z}$ and the subring $2\\mathbb{Z}$ (the even integers). The unity of $\\mathbb{Z}$ is 1, but $2\\mathbb{Z}$ does not have a multiplicative identity element (no integer $e$ such that $2k \\cdot e = 2k$ and $e \\cdot 2k = 2k$ for all even integers $2k$). A subring may not even have a unity element.\n\nStatement 2: \"Every field is an integral domain.\"\nThis statement is true. An integral domain is a commutative ring with unity and no zero divisors. A field has all these properties by definition: it is a commutative ring with unity where every non-zero element has a multiplicative inverse. The existence of multiplicative inverses for every non-zero element guarantees that there are no zero divisors.\n\nBased on this analysis, Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is C.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
