    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7663",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: suppose the sequence a_n satisfies $lim_{{n\\rightarrow\\infty}}a_n\\sum_{{i=1}}^n a_i^2=1$. What is the limit of  3n(a_n)^3?\n\nOptions: \nA. 3.0\nB. n^2\nC. -1.0\nD. Undefined\nE. 1.0\nF. 0.0\nG. -Infinity\nH. Infinity\nI. 2.718 (e, the base of natural logarithms)\nJ. 1/n\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let $S_n = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2$. We are given that $\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} a_n S_n = 1$.\nAs $n \\to \\infty$, $a_n S_n \\to 1$. This implies that $a_n \\to 0$ and $S_n \\to \\infty$.\nSince $S_n = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2$ is an increasing sequence of positive terms (since $a_i^2 \\ge 0$ and at least one term must be non-zero for $S_n$ to diverge), and $S_n \\to \\infty$, we can use Stolz-Cesaro theorem.\n\nLet $x_n = n$ and $y_n = 1/a_n^3$. We are interested in $\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} 3n(a_n)^3 = 3 \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} \\frac{n}{1/a_n^3}$.\nThis is not directly applicable. Instead, let's consider the sum.\n\nSince $a_n S_n \\to 1$, we have $a_n \\approx 1/S_n$ for large $n$.\nAlso, $S_n - S_{n-1} = a_n^2$ for $n \\ge 2$.\nIf $\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} n (a_n)^3 = L$, then $\\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty} (a_n)^3 = 0$, which means $a_n \\to 0$.\nIf $a_n \\to 0$, then for large $n$, $S_n = S_{n-1} + a_n^2$.\n\nConsider the behavior of $S_n$. Since $a_n S_n \\to 1$, $a_n \\approx 1/S_n$. Then $a_n^2 \\approx 1/S_n^2$.\nSo $S_n - S_{n-1} \\approx 1/S_n^2$. This suggests $S_n^2 (S_n - S_{n-1}) \\approx 1$.\nIf $S_n$ changes by $\\Delta S$, then $S_n^2 \\Delta S \\approx 1$. If we treat $S_n$ as a continuous variable, $2S dS \\approx dt$ or $S^2 dS \\approx dt$.\nIf $S^2 dS \\approx dt$, then $\\int S^2 dS \\approx \\int dt$, so $S^3/3 \\approx t$, which means $S_n \\approx (3n)^{1/3}$.\nIf $S_n \\approx (3n)^{1/3}$, then $a_n \\approx 1/S_n \\approx 1/(3n)^{1/3}$.\nThen $n (a_n)^3 \\approx n (1/(3n)^{1/3})^3 = n (1/(3n)) = n/(3n) = 1/3$.\nSo $3n(a_n)^3 \\approx 3(1/3) = 1$.\n\nLet's verify this more rigorously.\nWe have $a_n S_n \\to 1$.\n$S_n - S_{n-1} = a_n^2$.\nThen $a_n S_n \\to 1$ implies $a_n \\to 0$.\nConsider the limit of $n (a_n)^3$. Assume the limit is $L$.\nIf $S_n \\sim cn^p$, then $a_n \\sim 1/S_n \\sim c^{-1}n^{-p}$.\nThen $a_n S_n \\sim c^{-1}n^{-p} cn^p = 1$. This is consistent.\n$a_n^2 \\sim c^{-2}n^{-2p}$.\n$S_n - S_{n-1} \\approx S_n(1 - (1-1/n)^p) \\approx S_n (p/n) \\sim cn^p (p/n) = p c n^{p-1}$.\nSo $a_n^2 \\sim p c n^{p-1}$.\n$c^{-2}n^{-2p} \\sim p c n^{p-1}$.\nThis implies $-2p = p-1$, so $3p = 1$, $p = 1/3$.\nAnd $c^{-2} = pc = (1/3)c$, so $c^{-2} = c/3$, $3 = c^3$, $c = 3^{1/3}$.\nSo $S_n \\sim (3n)^{1/3}$.\nThen $a_n \\sim 1/S_n \\sim 1/(3n)^{1/3}$.\n$n (a_n)^3 \\sim n (1/(3n)^{1/3})^3 = n (1/(3n)) = 1/3$.\nSo $3n(a_n)^3 \\sim 3(1/3) = 1$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$.",
