    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6525",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | A homomorphism may have an empty kernel. Statement 2 | It is not possible to have a nontrivial homomorphism of some finite group into some infinite group.\n\nOptions: \nA. Statement 1 is true, Statement 2 is not given\nB. Both statements are true, but they contradict each other\nC. Both statements are not given\nD. False, True\nE. Statement 1 is not given, Statement 2 is true\nF. Statement 1 is false, Statement 2 is not given\nG. False, False\nH. True, True\nI. True, False\nJ. Statement 1 is not given, Statement 2 is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement.\n\nStatement 1: A homomorphism may have an empty kernel.\nA kernel of a homomorphism is the set of elements in the domain that map to the identity element in the codomain. For a homomorphism from group $G$ to group $H$, denoted by $f: G \\to H$, the kernel is defined as $\\ker(f) = \\{g \\in G \\mid f(g) = e_H\\}$, where $e_H$ is the identity element in $H$. The identity element of the domain, $e_G$, is always mapped to the identity element of the codomain, i.e., $f(e_G) = e_H$. Therefore, $e_G$ is always in the kernel. This means the kernel can never be empty; it always contains at least the identity element. So, Statement 1 is false.\n\nStatement 2: It is not possible to have a nontrivial homomorphism of some finite group into some infinite group.\nA nontrivial homomorphism is a homomorphism that is not the zero homomorphism (where every element maps to the identity). Consider a finite group $G$ and an infinite group $H$. We can define a homomorphism $f: G \\to H$ by mapping every element of $G$ to the identity element of $H$. This is the zero homomorphism.\nHowever, we can also construct nontrivial homomorphisms. For example, let $G = \\mathbb{Z}_n$ (the cyclic group of order $n$) and $H = \\mathbb{Z}$ (the integers under addition). The map $f(k) = nk$ for $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}_n$ is a homomorphism from $\\mathbb{Z}_n$ to $\\mathbb{Z}$. The kernel is $\\{k \\in \\mathbb{Z}_n \\mid nk \\equiv 0 \\pmod{0}\\}$, which requires $nk=0$. Since $k \\in \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$, this means $k=0$. So, $\\ker(f) = \\{0\\}$, which is trivial. The image of this homomorphism is the subgroup $n\\mathbb{Z}$ of $\\mathbb{Z}$, which is infinite. This is a nontrivial homomorphism from a finite group to an infinite group. So, Statement 2 is false.\n\nBased on this analysis:\nStatement 1 is false.\nStatement 2 is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is (G) False, False.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
