c_4$. Palindrome $c_1 c_2 c_3 c_4 c_4 c_3 c_2 c_1$. This requires 2 M's if $c_4=M$, which is not possible.\n  So, length 7 palindrome with M as the unique center means the first half has length 3. $c_1 c_2 c_3 M c_3 c_2 c_1$.\n  The letters for $c_1, c_2, c_3$ are chosen such that we have at least two of each.\n  Consider the set of letters for the first half: $\\{I, S, P\\}$.\n  We are forming strings of length 3, where repetition is allowed, and the corresponding pairs are available.\n  Possible strings of length 3 using I, S, P for $c_1 c_2 c_3$: $3^3 = 27$.\n  We need to check counts for each of these 27 strings.\n  Example: If $c_1=I, c_2=I, c_3=I$, we need IIIMIII. Requires 6 I's. Not possible.\n  Instead, consider the counts of letters {I, S, P} appearing in the first half (each counted twice for the palindrome).\n  Let the first half string be $s$. Palindrome is $s M \\text{reverse}(s)$.\n  Count of I in $s$ is $n_I'$, count of S is $n_S'$, count of P is $n_P'$.\n  Total I's needed = $2 n_I'$. Total S's needed = $2 n_S'$. Total P's needed = $2 n_P'$.\n  We have I(4), S(4), P(2). So $2 n_I' \\le 4 \\implies n_I' \\le 2$.\n  $2 n_S' \\le 4 \\implies n_S' \\le 2$.\n  $2 n_P' \\le 2 \\implies n_P' \\le 1$.\n  We are forming strings of length 3 ($c_1 c_2 c_3$) from {I, S, P} where the counts of I are at most 2, S at most 2, P at most 1.\n  Let's enumerate these strings:\n  String length 3.\n  Possible letters for $c_1, c_2, c_3$ are I, S, P.\n  Consider combinations of counts of (I, S, P) in the string of length 3, such that $n_I \\le 2, n_S \\le 2, n_P \\le 1$.\n  The sum of counts must be 3.\n  Possible count combinations (nI, nS, nP):\n  - (2, 1, 0): II S, I S I, S I I. (3 permutations of type II S).\n    II S: has 2 I's, 1 S. Palindrome: IISMIIS. Requires 4 I's, 2 S's. Possible.\n    I S I: has 2 I's, 1 S. Palindrome: ISI M ISI. Requires 4 I's, 2 S's. Possible.\n    S I I: has 2 I's, 1 S. Palindrome: SII M IIH. Requires 4 I's, 2 S's. Possible.\n    So, 3 palindromes of the form with two I's and one S.\n  - (2, 0, 1): II P, I P I, P I I. (3 permutations of type II P).\n    II P: has 2 I's, 1 P. Palindrome: IIP M PII. Requires 4 I's, 2 P's. Possible.\n    I P I: has 2 I's, 1 P. Palindrome: IPI M IPI. Requires 4 I's, 2 P's. Possible.\n    P I I: has 2 I's, 1 P. Palindrome: PII M IIP. Requires 4 I's, 2 P's. Possible.\n    So, 3 palindromes of the form with two I's and one P.\n  - (1, 2, 0): I S S, S I S, S S I. (3 permutations of type I SS).\n    I S S: has 1 I, 2 S's. Palindrome: ISSM SSI. Requires 2 I's, 4 S's. Possible.\n    S I S: has 1 I, 2 S's. Palindrome: SISM SIS. Requires 2 I's, 4 S's. Possible.\n    S S I: has 1 I, 2 S's. Palindrome: SSI M ISS. Requires 2 I's, 4 S's. Possible.\n    So, 3 palindromes of the form with one I and two S's.\n  - (0, 2, 1): S S P, S P S, P S S. (3 permutations of type SS P).\n    S S P: has 2 S's, 1 P. Palindrome: SSP M PSS. Requires 4 S's, 2 P's. Possible.\n    S P S: has 2 S's, 1 P. Palindrome: SPS M SPS. Requires 4 S's, 2 P's. Possible.\n    P S S: has 2 S's, 1 P. Palindrome: PSS M SSP. Requires 4 S's, 2 P's. Possible.\n    So, 3 palindromes of the form with two S's and one P.\n  - (1, 1, 1): I S P, any permutation. (3! = 6 permutations).\n    ISP: has 1 I, 1 S, 1 P. Palindrome: ISPM PSI. Requires 2 I's, 2 S's, 2 P's. Possible.\n    There are 6 such permutations. So 6 palindromes.\n  - (1, 0, ?) Not possible, sum is not 3.\n  - (0, 0, ?) Not possible.\n\n  Total palindromes of length 7 with M in the middle:\n  From (2,1,0): 3\n  From (2,0,1): 3\n  From (1,2,0): 3\n  From (0,2,1): 3\n  From (1,1,1): 6\n  Total = 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 6 = 18 palindromes of length 7.\n\nLength 9:\n  First half string length 4: $c_1 c_2 c_3 c_4$. Palindrome: $c_1 c_2 c_3 c_4 M c_4 c_3 c_2 c_1$.\n  Counts required: $2 \\times (\\text{# of } c_i)$.\n  $n_I' \\le 2$, $n_S' \\le 2$, $n_P' \\le 1$.\n  Sum of counts in the first half string is 4.\n  Possible count combinations (nI, nS, nP) summing to 4:\n  - (2, 2, 0