      "generated_answer": "Let the expression be $N = 3^{3^{3^{...}}}$, where there are 2012 3's. We want to find $N \\pmod{100}$.\nWe observe the powers of 3 modulo 100:\n$3^1 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{100}$\n$3^2 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{100}$\n$3^3 \\equiv 27 \\pmod{100}$\n$3^4 \\equiv 81 \\pmod{100}$\n$3^5 \\equiv 243 \\equiv 43 \\pmod{100}$\n$3^{10} \\equiv 43^2 \\equiv 1849 \\equiv 49 \\pmod{100}$\n$3^{20} \\equiv 49^2 \\equiv 2401 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}$\n\nThe order of 3 modulo 100 is 20. This means that $3^k \\pmod{100}$ depends on $k \\pmod{20}$.\nSo we need to calculate the exponent modulo 20. The exponent is $3^{3^{3^{... (2011 \\text{ 3's})}}}$. Let this exponent be $E$. We need to find $E \\pmod{20}$.\n\nWe look at powers of 3 modulo 20:\n$3^1 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{20}$\n$3^2 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{20}$\n$3^3 \\equiv 27 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{20}$\n$3^4 \\equiv 3 \\times 7 \\equiv 21 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{20}$\n\nThe order of 3 modulo 20 is 4. This means that $3^k \\pmod{20}$ depends on $k \\pmod{4}$.\nSo we need to calculate the exponent of the exponent modulo 4. The exponent is $3^{3^{3^{... (2010 \\text{ 3's})}}}$. Let this exponent be $E'$. We need to find $E' \\pmod{4}$.\n\nWe look at powers of 3 modulo 4:\n$3^1 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$\n$3^2 \\equiv 9 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}$\n\nThe order of 3 modulo 4 is 2. This means that $3^k \\pmod{4}$ depends on $k \\pmod{2}$.\nSo we need to calculate the exponent of the exponent of the exponent modulo 2. The exponent is $3^{3^{3^{... (2009 \\text{ 3's})}}}$. Let this exponent be $E''$. We need to find $E'' \\pmod{2}$.\n\nSince we have $3^{...}$, and 3 is odd, any power of 3 will be odd.\nSo $E'' = 3^{\\text{something}}$ is odd.\nTherefore, $E'' \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}$.\n\nNow we can work our way back up.\nSince $E'' \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}$, we have $E' = 3^{E''} \\equiv 3^1 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$.\nSince $E' \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$, we have $E = 3^{E'} \\equiv 3^3 \\equiv 27 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{20}$.\nSince $E \\equiv 7 \\pmod{20}$, we have $N = 3^E \\equiv 3^7 \\pmod{100}$.\n\nNow we calculate $3^7 \\pmod{100}$:\n$3^1 \\equiv 3$\n$3^2 \\equiv 9$\n$3^3 \\equiv 27$\n$3^4 \\equiv 81$\n$3^5 \\equiv 243 \\equiv 43$\n$3^6 \\equiv 3 \\times 43 \\equiv 129 \\equiv 29$\n$3^7 \\equiv 3 \\times 29 \\equiv 87 \\pmod{100}$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
