    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | Any set of two vectors in R^2 is linearly independent. Statement 2 | If V = span(v1, ... , vk) and {{v1, ... , vk}} are linearly independent, then dim(V) = k.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, False\nB. True, False\nC. False, True\nD. Cannot determine, False\nE. True, True\nF. Cannot determine, Cannot determine\nG. False, Cannot determine\nH. True, Cannot determine\nI. Cannot determine, True\nJ. False, False, but there are exceptions\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement.\n\nStatement 1: \"Any set of two vectors in R^2 is linearly independent.\"\nThis statement is false. For example, the set of vectors {(1, 0), (2, 0)} in R^2 is linearly dependent because (2, 0) = 2 * (1, 0). A set of two vectors in R^2 is linearly independent if and only if they are not scalar multiples of each other and neither is the zero vector.\n\nStatement 2: \"If V = span(v1, ... , vk) and {{v1, ... , vk}} are linearly independent, then dim(V) = k.\"\nThis statement is true. The definition of the dimension of a vector space is the number of vectors in a basis for that space. A set of vectors that spans a vector space and is linearly independent forms a basis for that space. Therefore, if V is spanned by k linearly independent vectors, then these k vectors form a basis for V, and hence the dimension of V is k.\n\nBased on this analysis, Statement 1 is False and Statement 2 is True. This corresponds to option C.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
