    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6429",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | There exists a free abelian group of every positive integer rank. Statement 2 | A finitely generated abelian group is free abelian if its Betti number equals the number of elements in some generating set.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, False\nB. True, Cannot be determined\nC. False, True\nD. Cannot be determined, True\nE. False, Cannot be determined\nF. Statement 1 is false for certain positive integers, Statement 2 is false\nG. True, False\nH. Statement 1 is true only for certain positive integers, Statement 2 is true\nI. Cannot be determined, False\nJ. True, True\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Statement 1 is True. By definition, for any positive integer $n$, there exists a free abelian group of rank $n$, which is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{Z}^n$.\nStatement 2 is False. A finitely generated abelian group is free abelian if and only if its torsion subgroup is trivial. The Betti number is the rank of the torsion-free part, and for a free abelian group, this equals the rank of the group itself. However, the condition that the Betti number equals the number of elements in *some* generating set is not sufficient. For example, consider the group $\\mathbb{Z} \\oplus \\mathbb{Z}_2$. This group has a generating set $\\{(1, 0), (0, 1)\\}$. The number of elements in this generating set is 2. The Betti number of this group is 1 (rank of the torsion-free part $\\mathbb{Z} \\oplus \\{0\\}$). Thus, the Betti number (1) does not equal the number of elements in the generating set (2). However, consider a different generating set for $\\mathbb{Z} \\oplus \\mathbb{Z}_2$: $\\{(1, 0), (0, 1)\\}$. The elements are $(0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (1,1)$. The Betti number is 1. The number of elements in this generating set is not the correct measure. The Betti number is the rank of the free part of the abelian group. A finitely generated abelian group is free if and only if its torsion subgroup is trivial. Therefore, Statement 2 is false as stated.\n\nGiven the options:\nA. False, False\nB. True, Cannot be determined\nC. False, True\nD. Cannot be determined, True\nE. False, Cannot be determined\nF. Statement 1 is false for certain positive integers, Statement 2 is false\nG. True, False\nH. Statement 1 is true only for certain positive integers, Statement 2 is true\nI. Cannot be determined, False\nJ. True, True\n\nBased on our analysis, Statement 1 is True and Statement 2 is False. This corresponds to option G.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
      "success": true,
