    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6541",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For a class project, a student wants to see if boys and girls at their large high school differ in the number of contacts they have stored in their phone. The student conducts a survey of 50 randomly sampled boys and 40 randomly selected girls, and asks them to report the number of contacts. Which of the following is true about this situation?\n\nOptions: \nA. Because the sample sizes are both less than 50, the student should not use a two-sample t-test.\nB. Because the population standard deviations are not known and conditions are met, the student shoulduse a two-sample t-test.\nC. Because the student is testing for a difference in means, they should use a paired t-test.\nD. Because the student is comparing two groups, they should use a Mann-Whitney U test.\nE. Because the sample sizes are both greater than 30, the student should notuse a two-sample t-test.\nF. Because the sample sizes are different, the student should notuse a two-sample t-test.\nG. Because the student is comparing two groups, they should use an ANOVA test.\nH. Because the student is comparing two groups, they should use a one-sample t-test.\nI. Because the sample sizes are both greater than 30, the student should use a Chi-square test.\nJ. Because the shape of the population distribution is not known, the student should notuse a two-sample t-test.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question describes a scenario where we are comparing the means of two independent groups (boys and girls) on a continuous variable (number of contacts). The population standard deviations are unknown. A two-sample t-test is appropriate in this situation if the conditions for its use are met. Option B correctly identifies that since the population standard deviations are unknown and the conditions are assumed to be met for the test to be applicable, a two-sample t-test is the appropriate choice.\n\nOption A is incorrect because the sample sizes are not necessarily too small, especially if the populations are approximately normally distributed or if the sample sizes are large enough for the Central Limit Theorem to apply.\nOption C is incorrect because a paired t-test is used when the data are dependent, such as measuring the same individuals before and after an intervention. Here, the boys and girls are independent groups.\nOption D is incorrect. While Mann-Whitney U is a non-parametric alternative to the two-sample t-test, the question implicitly suggests a parametric approach given the options, and the most direct parametric approach is the t-test.\nOption E is incorrect. Sample sizes greater than 30 are generally considered sufficient for a two-sample t-test, not a reason to avoid it.\nOption F is incorrect. Two-sample t-tests can accommodate unequal sample sizes.\nOption G is incorrect. ANOVA is used for comparing means of three or more groups.\nOption H is incorrect. A one-sample t-test is used to compare a sample mean to a known population mean or a hypothesized value.\nOption I is incorrect. A Chi-square test is used for analyzing categorical data, not continuous variables like the number of contacts.\nOption J is incorrect. While the population distribution shape is not known, the Central Limit Theorem suggests that for sample sizes of 30 or more, the sampling distribution of the mean will be approximately normal, making the t-test robust.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
