    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement.\n\nStatement 1: \"For any two groups G and G', there exists a homomorphism of G into G'.\"\nThis statement is false. Consider the trivial group {e} and any non-trivial group G'. The only possible homomorphism from {e} to G' is the trivial homomorphism, which maps e to the identity element of G'. Now consider a non-trivial group G and the trivial group {e'} as G'. The only possible homomorphism from G to {e'} is the trivial homomorphism, which maps every element of G to e'. However, if we consider the case where G is not the trivial group and G' is also not the trivial group, it is not guaranteed that a non-trivial homomorphism exists. For example, a group with 2 elements and a group with 3 elements. The only homomorphism is the trivial one. More formally, for group G and G', there always exists at least the trivial homomorphism (mapping every element of G to the identity of G'), but this counts as one homomorphism. Statement 1 says \"there exists *a* homomorphism\", which is true (the trivial one). However, if \"a homomorphism\" implies a non-trivial one in some interpretations, it could be false. In standard group theory, the trivial homomorphism always exists, making statement 1 true. Let's assume the most inclusive interpretation where the trivial homomorphism counts.\n\nStatement 2: \"Every homomorphism is a one-to-one map.\"\nThis statement is false. A homomorphism is not necessarily one-to-one. For example, consider the homomorphism from the group of integers under addition $(\\mathbb{Z}, +)$ to itself defined by $f(x) = 2x$. This is a homomorphism, but it is not one-to-one because $f(1) = 2$ and $f(-1) = -2$, but also $f(2)=4$ and $f(0)=0$. A clearer counterexample for non-one-to-one mapping is from $\\mathbb{Z}$ to $\\mathbb{Z}_2$ by $f(x) = x \\pmod 2$. Here, $f(0) = 0$ and $f(2) = 0$, but $0 \\neq 2$.\n\nBased on this analysis:\nStatement 1 is True (due to the existence of the trivial homomorphism).\nStatement 2 is False.\n\nThis corresponds to option D. True, False.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
