compact.\nThen for any continuous f: X -> Y, f(X) is compact, hence closed and bounded. So Statement 2 is False in this common setting.\n\nCould Statement 2 be True?\nWe need f(X) to be not closed OR not bounded.\nConsider X = (0, 1] in R. X is bounded, but not closed.\nIf we take X to be \"closed and bounded\" as a subset of some Y.\nLet Y be the set of rational numbers Q with the usual metric. X = [0, 1] as a subset of Q.\nThis X is closed in Q, but not bounded in Q.\n\nLet's go back to the Riemann Series Theorem confirmation. Statement 1 is TRUE.\n\nNow Statement 2.\nIf we interpret \"closed and bounded\" in the usual sense for normed vector spaces, then Statement 2 is False. A compact set is closed and bounded, and the continuous image of a compact set is compact.\n\nHowever, if we allow for more general metric spaces where \"closed and bounded\" does not imply \"compact\", then Statement 2 could be True.\nLet Y be a metric space. Let X be a closed and bounded subset of Y.\nSuppose X itself is closed and bounded but not compact.\nLet f be the identity map from X to Y. Then f(X) = X, which is closed and bounded but not compact.\nIn such a context, f(X) could be considered \"not closed and bounded\" if \"closed and bounded\" implicitly means compact.\nBut the question asks if f(X) is NOT \"closed and bounded\".\n\nLet X be the Hilbert cube, which is a closed and bounded subset of R^\\infty, but it is not compact.\nLet Y = R^\\infty. Let X be the Hilbert cube. X is closed and bounded.\nLet f be the identity map. f(X) = X.\nIs the Hilbert cube \"closed and bounded\"? Yes.\nIs the Hilbert cube \"compact\"? No.\nSo, f(X) is closed and bounded, but not necessarily \"closed and bounded\" in a way that implies compactness.\n\nThe phrasing \"f(X) is NOT 'closed and bounded'\" is crucial. If it means f(X) is either not closed OR not bounded.\n\nLet X be a closed and bounded subset of Y.\nIf X is closed and bounded, it is a subset of some B(p, r) in Y.\nIf Y is R^n, and X i