plicity, where closed and bounded implies compact. In this context, Statement 2 is False.\n\nStatement 1: True (Riemann Series Theorem)\nStatement 2: False (Continuous image of a compact set is compact, and in Euclidean spaces, compact implies closed and bounded. If X is closed and bounded, it's compact, thus f(X) is compact and hence closed and bounded.)\n\nSo, we have True, False. This corresponds to option F.\n\nLet's double check Statement 2 more rigorously from a textbook perspective.\nTheorem: If K is a compact metric space and f: K -> Y is a continuous map into a metric space Y, then f(K) is compact.\nIn Euclidean spaces, a set is compact if and only if it is closed and bounded.\nIf X is closed and bounded, it is compact (in Euclidean spaces).\nTherefore, f(X) is compact, and thus f(X) is closed and bounded.\nSo, Statement 2 is False.\n\nFinal check:\nStatement 1: True. Riemann Series Theorem states that for a conditionally convergent series, rearrangement can lead to any sum. Here ∑|a_i| diverges and ∑ a_i converges, so it's conditionally convergent.\nStatement 2: False. If X is closed and bounded within a Euclidean space, it's compact. The continuous image of a compact space is compact. In Euclidean spaces, compact implies closed and bounded. Thus, f(X) is closed and bounded.\n\nTherefore, the answer is True, False.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
