    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7003",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | Suppose f : [a, b] is a function and suppose f has a local maximum. f'(x) must exist and equal 0? Statement 2 | There exist non-constant continuous maps from R to Q.\n\nOptions: \nA. Neither True nor False, False\nB. Neither True nor False, True\nC. False, Neither True nor False\nD. False, True or False\nE. True, Neither True nor False\nF. False, False\nG. Neither True nor False, Neither True nor False\nH. False, True\nI. True, True\nJ. True, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement.\n\nStatement 1: \"Suppose f : [a, b] is a function and suppose f has a local maximum. f'(x) must exist and equal 0?\"\nThis statement is not necessarily true. While at a local maximum within an open interval (a, b), if the function is differentiable, its derivative must be zero (Fermat's Theorem). However, a function can have a local maximum at an endpoint (a or b) where the derivative may not exist or might not be zero. Also, a function can have a local maximum where it is not differentiable (e.g., a sharp peak like the absolute value function). Therefore, Statement 1 is False.\n\nStatement 2: \"There exist non-constant continuous maps from R to Q.\"\nThe set of real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$ has infinitely many elements, and the set of rational numbers $\\mathbb{Q}$ also has infinitely many elements. However, $\\mathbb{R}$ is a complete ordered field, while $\\mathbb{Q}$ is not complete. A continuous function maps intervals to intervals. If there were a non-constant continuous function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{Q}$, it would map an interval $[x, y]$ to an interval $[f(x), f(y)]$. Since $f(x)$ and $f(y)$ are rational, this interval $[f(x), f(y)]$ would be a subset of $\\mathbb{Q}$. However, any non-trivial interval of real numbers contains irrational numbers. Therefore, a continuous function mapping from $\\mathbb{R}$ to $\\mathbb{Q}$ must be constant. Thus, Statement 2 is False.\n\nBased on our analysis, both Statement 1 and Statement 2 are False.\n\nLet's look at the options:\nThe options are of the form \"[Statement 1 evaluation], [Statement 2 evaluation]\".\n\nA. Neither True nor False, False\nB. Neither True nor False, True\nC. False, Neither True nor False\nD. False, True or False\nE. True, Neither True nor False\nF. False, False\nG. Neither True nor False, Neither True nor False\nH. False, True\nI. True, True\nJ. True, False\n\nOur findings are False for Statement 1 and False for Statement 2. This corresponds to option F.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
