    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7064",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1 | If R is a ring and f(x) and g(x) are in R[x], then deg (f(x)+g(x)) = maximum (deg f(x), deg g(x)). Statement 2 | If F is a field then every ideal of F[x] is principal.\n\nOptions: \nA. False, Not Determined\nB. Not Determined, Not Determined\nC. Not Determined, True\nD. True, Not Determined\nE. True, True\nF. True, False\nG. False, True\nH. Not Determined, False\nI. True, Not Applicable\nJ. False, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement:\n\nStatement 1: If R is a ring and f(x) and g(x) are in R[x], then deg (f(x)+g(x)) = maximum (deg f(x), deg g(x)).\nThis statement is **not always true**. While it is true if the coefficients of the highest degree terms of f(x) and g(x) do not cancel each other out, this cancellation can occur. For example, if R = $\\mathbb{Z}_2$ (the integers modulo 2) and f(x) = x + 1, g(x) = x. Then deg f(x) = 1 and deg g(x) = 1. However, f(x) + g(x) = (x + 1) + x = 2x + 1 = 1 (in $\\mathbb{Z}_2[x]$), so deg(f(x) + g(x)) = 0. This contradicts the statement that the degree is the maximum of the individual degrees (which is 1). Therefore, Statement 1 is False.\n\nStatement 2: If F is a field then every ideal of F[x] is principal.\nThis statement is **True**. This is a fundamental result in abstract algebra known as the Principal Ideal Domain (PID) property of polynomial rings over fields. F[x] is indeed a Principal Ideal Domain.\n\nBased on our analysis, Statement 1 is False and Statement 2 is True. This corresponds to the option \"False, True\".\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
