    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7614",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is NOT true of the χ2 probability distribution function?\n\nOptions: \nA. For small degrees of freedom, the curve displays strong right-skewness.\nB. The area under the χ2 curve is 1.\nC. χ2 is defined only for nonnegative values of the variable.\nD. For the same α, as the number of degrees of freedom decreases, the critical value for the rejection region increases.\nE. For the same α, as the number of degrees of freedom increases, the critical value for the rejection region decreases.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **E**.\n\nLet's analyze each option:\nA. For small degrees of freedom, the $\\chi^2$ distribution is indeed strongly right-skewed.\nB. Like all probability distribution functions, the total area under the $\\chi^2$ curve is 1.\nC. The $\\chi^2$ variable is the sum of squared standard normal variables, so it can only take nonnegative values.\nD. As the degrees of freedom decrease, the $\\chi^2$ distribution becomes more spread out to the right, meaning the critical value for a given $\\alpha$ (tail area) increases.\nE. As the degrees of freedom increase, the $\\chi^2$ distribution becomes less skewed and more bell-shaped. For the same $\\alpha$, the critical value for the rejection region will increase, not decrease, because the probability mass shifts towards larger values.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$.",
