    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7699",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Statement 1| Every group of order p^2 where p is prime is Abelian. Statement 2 | For a fixed prime p a Sylow p-subgroup of a group G is a normal subgroup of G if and only if it is the only Sylow p-subgroup of G.\n\nOptions: \nA. Statement 1 is true for certain prime numbers, Statement 2 is true for certain prime numbers\nB. Both statements are true only for certain prime numbers\nC. Statement 1 is false, Statement 2 is true only for certain prime numbers\nD. Statement 1 is true, Statement 2 is false for certain prime numbers\nE. True, True\nF. False, True\nG. Statement 1 is true only for certain prime numbers, Statement 2 is false\nH. Statement 1 is false for certain prime numbers, Statement 2 is true\nI. False, False\nJ. True, False\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each statement.\n\nStatement 1: Every group of order $p^2$ where $p$ is prime is Abelian.\nLet G be a group of order $p^2$. The center of G, $Z(G)$, has order $p$ or $p^2$. If $|Z(G)| = p^2$, then G is Abelian. If $|Z(G)| = p$, then G/Z(G) has order $p^2/p = p$. Since any group of prime order is cyclic, G/Z(G) is cyclic. A group G is Abelian if and only if G/Z(G) is trivial (which is not the case here as it has order p). However, if G/Z(G) is cyclic, it implies that G is Abelian. Therefore, every group of order $p^2$ is Abelian for any prime $p$. Statement 1 is true.\n\nStatement 2: For a fixed prime $p$, a Sylow $p$-subgroup of a group G is a normal subgroup of G if and only if it is the only Sylow $p$-subgroup of G.\nLet $P$ be a Sylow $p$-subgroup of G.\nIf $P$ is the only Sylow $p$-subgroup, then by Sylow's Theorems, the number of Sylow $p$-subgroups, $n_p$, must be 1. Since $n_p \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$ and $n_p$ divides $|G|/|P|$, $n_p = 1$ implies that $P$ is normal in G.\nIf $P$ is normal in G, then any conjugate of $P$ must be equal to $P$. Since Sylow $p$-subgroups are conjugate to each other, there can be only one Sylow $p$-subgroup. Therefore, $P$ is the only Sylow $p$-subgroup. Statement 2 is true.\n\nSince both statements are true, the correct option is E.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 256,
        "output_tokens": 457,
