    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_8185",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An electric dipole consisting of charges of magnitude $1.50 \\mathrm{{nC}}$ separated by $6.20 \\mu \\mathrm{{m}}$ is in an electric field of strength 1100 $\\mathrm{{N}} / \\mathrm{{C}}$. What is the magnitude of the electric dipole moment?\n\nOptions: \nA. $8.50$$10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nB. $13.20 \\times 10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nC. $4.60 \\times 10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nD. $6.20 \\times 10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nE. $7.40$$10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nF. $5.50 \\times 10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nG. $12.00 \\times 10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nH. $11.10 \\times 10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nI.  $9.30$$10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$ \nJ. $10.20$$10^{{-15}} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The electric dipole moment, denoted by $p$, is the product of the magnitude of one of the charges $(q)$ and the distance $(d)$ separating the two charges. Mathematically, $p = qd$.\nGiven:\nCharge magnitude, $q = 1.50 \\mathrm{{nC}} = 1.50 \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{{C}}$\nSeparation distance, $d = 6.20 \\mu \\mathrm{{m}} = 6.20 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{{m}}$\n\nCalculate the electric dipole moment:\n$p = (1.50 \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{{C}}) \\times (6.20 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{{m}})$\n$p = (1.50 \\times 6.20) \\times 10^{-9-6} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\n$p = 9.30 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\n\nThe strength of the electric field is not needed to calculate the magnitude of the electric dipole moment.\n\nComparing this result with the given options:\nA. $8.50 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nB. $13.20 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nC. $4.60 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nD. $6.20 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nE. $7.40 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nF. $5.50 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nG. $12.00 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nH. $11.10 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nI. $9.30 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\nJ. $10.20 \\times 10^{-15} \\mathrm{{C}} \\cdot \\mathrm{{m}}$\n\nThe calculated value matches option I.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
