    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_8908",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose a hologram is made with light of wavelength 600 nm (orange) and then viewed with light of wavelength 500 nm (green) . How will the images differ from those that would be formed with the orange light?\n\nOptions: \nA. The reconstructed image distance = (3 / 2) the object distance\nB. the reconstructed image distance = (6 / 5) the object distance\nC. The reconstructed image distance = (4 / 5) the object distance\nD. the reconstructed image distance = 2 times the object distance\nE. the reconstructed image distance = the object distance\nF. the reconstructed image distance = (5 / 6) the object distance\nG. The reconstructed image distance = 3 times the object distance\nH. The reconstructed image distance is the same as when viewed with orange light\nI. The reconstructed image distance = (5 / 4) the object distance\nJ. The reconstructed image distance = (2 / 3) the object distance\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To understand how the reconstructed image distance changes, we need to consider the principles of hologram reconstruction. The reconstruction of a hologram is analogous to diffraction. The effective slit spacing in a hologram is related to the wavelength of the light used to create it, and the viewing conditions determine the diffraction angles and thus the image distance.\n\nThe relationship between the wavelength of light used for reconstruction ($\\lambda_{view}$) and the wavelength of light used for recording ($\\lambda_{record}$) impacts the magnification and thus the image distance. Specifically, the image distance ($z'$) is proportional to the ratio of the viewing wavelength to the recording wavelength, multiplied by the object distance ($z$).\n\nThe formula for the image distance in holographic reconstruction can be expressed as:\n$z' = z \\left( \\frac{\\lambda_{view}}{\\lambda_{record}} \\right)$\n\nIn this case, $\\lambda_{record} = 600$ nm and $\\lambda_{view} = 500$ nm.\nSo, the ratio of wavelengths is $\\frac{\\lambda_{view}}{\\lambda_{record}} = \\frac{500 \\text{ nm}}{600 \\text{ nm}} = \\frac{5}{6}$.\n\nTherefore, the reconstructed image distance is $\\frac{5}{6}$ times the object distance:\n$z' = z \\left( \\frac{5}{6} \\right)$\n\nLooking at the options provided:\nA. The reconstructed image distance = (3 / 2) the object distance\nB. the reconstructed image distance = (6 / 5) the object distance\nC. The reconstructed image distance = (4 / 5) the object distance\nD. The reconstructed image distance = 2 times the object distance\nE. The reconstructed image distance = the object distance\nF. The reconstructed image distance = (5 / 6) the object distance\nG. The reconstructed image distance = 3 times the object distance\nH. The reconstructed image distance is the same as when viewed with orange light\nI. The reconstructed image distance = (5 / 4) the object distance\nJ. The reconstructed image distance = (2 / 3) the object distance\n\nOption F matches our calculated relationship. The image will appear closer to the viewer when viewed with a shorter wavelength of light than that used for recording.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
