      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each nuclear reaction to identify X.\n\n(a) ${^1_1}\\mathrm{H} + {^9_4}\\mathrm{Be} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{X} + {^1_0}\\mathrm{n}$\nFor conservation of atomic number (number of protons), $1 + 4 = Z + 0$, so $Z = 5$.\nFor conservation of mass number (number of nucleons), $1 + 9 = A + 1$, so $A = 9$.\nThus, X is an element with atomic number 5 and mass number 9. The element with atomic number 5 is Boron (B). So X is ${^9_5}\\mathrm{B}$.\n\n(b) ${^{12}_6}\\mathrm{C} + {^1_1}\\mathrm{H} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{X}$\nFor conservation of atomic number, $6 + 1 = Z$, so $Z = 7$.\nFor conservation of mass number, $12 + 1 = A$, so $A = 13$.\nThus, X is an element with atomic number 7 and mass number 13. The element with atomic number 7 is Nitrogen (N). So X is ${^{13}_7}\\mathrm{N}$.\n\n(c) ${^{15}_7}\\mathrm{N} + {^1_1}\\mathrm{H} \\rightarrow {^4_2}\\mathrm{He} + \\mathrm{X}$\nFor conservation of atomic number, $7 + 1 = 2 + Z$, so $Z = 6$.\nFor conservation of mass number, $15 + 1 = 4 + A$, so $A = 12$.\nThus, X is an element with atomic number 6 and mass number 12. The element with atomic number 6 is Carbon (C). So X is ${^{12}_6}\\mathrm{C}$.\n\nNow let's re-examine the provided options and the question. The question asks to identify X in \"the following nuclear reactions\". This implies that X should be the same across all reactions, or there might be a misunderstanding in how the question is posed. However, if we are to find an option that matches any of the calculated X values, let's compare:\n\nCalculated X values:\n(a) ${^9_5}\\mathrm{B}$\n(b) ${^{13}_7}\\mathrm{N}$\n(c) ${^{12}_6}\\mathrm{C}$\n\nLet's check the options again to see if any of them directly matches any of these. It seems the question is asking to identify X as a single entity that might be the correct option from the list, and we need to check which of the options fits one of the reactions. The problem statement \"Identify X in the following nuclear reactions\" suggests that X is a common element, but the reactions are distinct. Let's assume the question is asking which of the options could be X in *any* of the provided reactions if the reactions were meant to yield one of the options.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the question assuming it's asking to find X in the first reaction and see if that matches any option.\nFrom (a), X is ${^9_5}\\mathrm{B}$. Let's look for Boron isotopes in the options. Option D is ${ }^{9}\\mathrm{B}$. This matches our result for reaction (a).\n\nLet's verify if any other reaction gives a result that is also in the options.\nFrom (b), X is ${^{13}_7}\\mathrm{N}$. Option A is ${ }^{14}\\mathrm{N}$. This is not ${^{13}_7}\\mathrm{N}$.\nFrom (c), X is ${^{12}_6}\\mathrm{C}$. There is no Carbon isotope listed in the options.\n\nGiven that option D, ${ }^{9}\\mathrm{B}$, perfectly matches the result of the first reaction (a), it is highly probable that the question is designed for us to find X in one of the reactions that corresponds to an option. Since reaction (a) yields ${^9}\\mathrm{B}$, and option D is ${ }^{9}\\mathrm{B}$, we choose D.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
