    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_9018",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The interior of a refrigerator is typically held at $36^{{\\circ}} \\mathrm{{F}}$ and the interior of a freezer is typically held at $0.00^{{\\circ}} \\mathrm{{F}}$. If the room temperature is $65^{{\\circ}} \\mathrm{{F}}$, by what factor is it more expensive to extract the same amount of heat from the freezer than from the refrigerator? Assume that the theoretical limit for the performance of a reversible refrigerator is valid in this case.\n\nOptions: \nA. 3.2\nB. 2.0\nC. 1.2\nD. 2.7\nE. 3.5\nF.  2.4\nG. 4.0\nH. 1.5\nI. 5.0\nJ. 1.8\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The coefficient of performance (COP) of a refrigerator is given by $COP = \\frac{T_c}{T_h - T_c}$, where $T_c$ is the cold reservoir temperature and $T_h$ is the hot reservoir temperature, both in Kelvin. The cost of operating a refrigerator is inversely proportional to its COP, assuming constant heat extraction and work input per unit temperature difference.\n\nFor the refrigerator, $T_{c,ref} = 36^{{\\circ}} \\mathrm{{F}} = (36 - 32) \\times \\frac{5}{9} + 273.15 \\mathrm{{K}} = 2.23 + 273.15 = 275.38 \\mathrm{{K}}$.\nThe room temperature is $T_{h,room} = 65^{{\\circ}} \\mathrm{{F}} = (65 - 32) \\times \\frac{5}{9} + 273.15 \\mathrm{{K}} = 18.33 + 273.15 = 291.48 \\mathrm{{K}}$.\n$COP_{ref} = \\frac{T_{c,ref}}{T_{h,room} - T_{c,ref}} = \\frac{275.38}{291.48 - 275.38} = \\frac{275.38}{16.1} \\approx 17.1$.\n\nFor the freezer, $T_{c,freezer} = 0.00^{{\\circ}} \\mathrm{{F}} = (0 - 32) \\times \\frac{5}{9} + 273.15 \\mathrm{{K}} = -17.78 + 273.15 = 255.37 \\mathrm{{K}}$.\n$COP_{freezer} = \\frac{T_{c,freezer}}{T_{h,room} - T_{c,freezer}} = \\frac{255.37}{291.48 - 255.37} = \\frac{255.37}{36.11} \\approx 7.07$.\n\nThe factor by which it is more expensive to extract heat from the freezer than from the refrigerator is the inverse ratio of their COPs:\nFactor = $\\frac{COP_{ref}}{COP_{freezer}} = \\frac{17.1}{7.07} \\approx 2.42$.\n\nAlternatively, we can convert temperatures to Rankine:\n$T_{c,ref} = 36 + 459.67 = 495.67^\\circ R$.\n$T_{c,freezer} = 0 + 459.67 = 459.67^\\circ R$.\n$T_{h,room} = 65 + 459.67 = 524.67^\\circ R$.\n\n$COP_{ref} = \\frac{T_{c,ref}}{T_{h,room} - T_{c,ref}} = \\frac{495.67}{524.67 - 495.67} = \\frac{495.67}{29} \\approx 17.09$.\n$COP_{freezer} = \\frac{T_{c,freezer}}{T_{h,room} - T_{c,freezer}} = \\frac{459.67}{524.67 - 459.67} = \\frac{459.67}{65} \\approx 7.07$.\n\nFactor = $\\frac{COP_{ref}}{COP_{freezer}} = \\frac{17.09}{7.07} \\approx 2.42$.\n\nComparing this to the given options, the closest value is 2.4.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
