  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_7895",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Why do Uranus and Neptune have blue methane clouds but Jupiter and Saturn do not?\n\nOptions: \nA. The magnetic fields of Uranus and Neptune affect the formation of methane clouds.\nB. The relatively slow rotation of Uranus and Neptune allows methane to migrate to higher levels in the atmosphere and condense into clouds.\nC. Jupiter and Saturn have a chemical reaction that neutralizes the blue color of methane.\nD. Jupiter and Saturn have a higher atmospheric pressure, preventing the formation of methane clouds.\nE. The blue color of methane clouds is due to the reflection of sunlight, which is less intense on Jupiter and Saturn.\nF. The gravitational pull on Uranus and Neptune is stronger, which affects the formation of methane clouds.\nG. Uranus and Neptune have a higher concentration of methane in their atmosphere.\nH. Methane did not exist in the solar nebula at the radii of Jupiter and Saturn when the planets formed.\nI. Methane does not condense into ice in the warmer atmospheric temperatures of Jupiter and Saturn.\nJ. Methane reacts with the abundant ammonia clouds in Jupiter and Saturn and is removed from the atmosphere.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is **I**.\n\nThe key difference lies in the temperature of the atmospheric layers where methane can condense and form clouds. Jupiter and Saturn are warmer and more massive, leading to higher atmospheric pressures and temperatures even at higher altitudes. This prevents methane from condensing into solid ice particles that would form visible blue clouds. Uranus and Neptune, being farther from the Sun and less massive, have colder upper atmospheres where methane can readily condense, creating the visible blue clouds we observe.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 285,
        "output_tokens": 107,
        "total_tokens": 392
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
