    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A circuit consists of a battery and a light bulb. At first, the circuit is disconnected. Then, the circuit is connected, and the light bulb lights. After the light bulb has been lit for a few moments, how has the net charge residing on the circuit elements changed?\n\nOptions: \nA. The net charge depends on the power of the battery.\nB. The net charge has become more positive.\nC. The net charge has halved.\nD. The net charge has doubled.\nE. The net charge has completely discharged.\nF. The net charge has become neutral.\nG. The net charge has become zero.\nH. The net charge has not changed.\nI. Whether the net charge becomes more positive or more negative depends on the initial net charge residing on the circuit elements before the bulb was lit.\nJ. The net charge has become more negative.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "When a circuit is connected, there is a flow of charge (current) through the wires and the light bulb. However, the wires and the battery are typically made of conductive materials which are electrically neutral to begin with. This means that the total positive charge equals the total negative charge. While charges move, the total net charge on each component, and thus the entire circuit, remains zero. Therefore, the net charge on the circuit elements does not change; it remains neutral or zero.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{H}.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
