  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_7893",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You’ve made a scientific theory that there is an attractive force between all objects. When will your theory be proven to be correct?\n\nOptions: \nA. When the theory can predict future observations.\nB. As soon as you propose the theory.\nC. When your theory becomes a law.\nD. You can never prove your theory to be correct only “yet to be proven wrong”.\nE. When the scientific community accepts it as a fact.\nF. The first time you drop a bowling ball and it falls to the ground proving your hypothesis.\nG. After you’ve repeated your experiment many times.\nH. When you and many others have tested the hypothesis.\nI. After a single successful experiment that supports your hypothesis.\nJ. When the theory is published in a scientific journal.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is **A**.\n\nScientific theories are proven correct through their ability to consistently predict future observations and be supported by evidence. While other options describe aspects of the scientific process (hypothesis testing, publication, community acceptance), option A most accurately captures the essence of how a scientific theory gains validity and is considered \"proven\" in a predictive and empirical sense. It's important to note that in science, theories are never definitively \"proven\" in a absolute, final sense, but rather are supported by overwhelming evidence and predictive power, making them the best current explanations for phenomena. Option D touches on this idea of falsifiability, which is also a key aspect of scientific theories, but option A describes how a theory *demonstrates* its correctness and usefulness.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
