    "global index": "MathQA_1958",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: miller street begins at baker street and runs directly east for 4.5 kilometers until it ends when it meets turner street . miller street is intersected every 250 meters by a perpendicular street , and each of those streets other than baker street and turner street is given a number beginning at 1 st street ( one block east of baker street ) and continuing consecutively ( 2 nd street , 3 rd street , etc . . . ) until the highest - numbered street one block west of turner street . what is the highest - numbered street that intersects miller street ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 15 th\nB. 16 th\nC. 17 th\nD. 18 th\nE. 19 th\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The total length of Miller Street is 4.5 kilometers, which is equal to 4500 meters.\nIntersections occur every 250 meters.\nTo find the number of intersections, we divide the total length by the distance between intersections: 4500 meters / 250 meters/intersection = 18 intersections.\nHowever, the question asks for the highest-numbered street that intersects Miller Street. Baker Street is the starting point (0 meters) and Turner Street is the ending point (4500 meters). The numbered streets are between Baker Street and Turner Street. The first numbered street is 1st Street, which is 250 meters east of Baker Street.\nThe intersections occur at distances of 250m, 500m, 750m, ..., 4250m.\nThe number of these intersecting streets can be found by (4250 - 250) / 250 + 1 = 4000 / 250 + 1 = 16 + 1 = 17.\nAlternatively, if we consider the number of blocks, the total length of 4500 meters divided by the block length of 250 meters gives 4500/250 = 18 blocks. This means there are 18 equal segments. The number of intersecting streets between the two endpoints will be one less than the number of segments. However, the problem states that each of those streets *other than Baker street and Turner street* is given a number. So, if there are 18 equal segments, there are 17 points of intersection between Baker Street and Turner Street.\nThe first numbered street is 1st street, one block east of Baker street, which is at the 250m mark.\nThe last numbered street is one block west of Turner street. Since Turner street is at the 4500m mark, the last numbered street will be at 4500m - 250m = 4250m.\nTo find which number street this is, we divide the distance from Baker Street by the block length: 4250 meters / 250 meters/street = 17.\nTherefore, the highest-numbered street is the 17th street.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
