Distance covered by Ram for first meeting = 18.5\nDistance covered by Shyam for first meeting = L - 18.5\nDistance covered by Ram for second meeting = 2L - 10.5\nDistance covered by Shyam for second meeting = L + 10.5\n\n(18.5) / (L - 18.5) = (2L - 10.5) / (L + 10.5)\n\nThis leads to L = 33.\n\nConsider the relationship: when two swimmers meet for the nth time, the total distance covered by them is (2n-1)L.\nFor the 1st meeting, total distance = 1L.\nFor the 2nd meeting, total distance = 3L.\n\nLet $d_R_1$ be distance covered by Ram for 1st meeting = 18.5 m.\nLet $d_S_1$ be distance covered by Shyam for 1st meeting = L - 18.5 m.\n\nLet $d_R_2$ be distance covered by Ram for 2nd meeting.\nLet $d_S_2$ be distance covered by Shyam for 2nd meeting.\nThe problem states \"they 1st pass at a point 18.5 m from the deep end\".\n\"after setting off on the return length , the swimmers pass for the 2nd time just 10.5 m from the shallow end\".\n\nIf Ram started from the deep end and is faster.\nFirst pass: Ram covered 18.5m. Shyam covered L-18.5m.\nSecond pass:\nRam has swum L (deep to shallow) + (L-10.5) (shallow towards deep). Total = 2L - 10.5.\nShyam has swum L (shallow to deep) + 10.5 (deep towards shallow). Total = L + 10.5.\n\nSo, in the time they meet for the second time, each has covered 3 times the distance they covered when they first met. (Since total distance covered is 3L vs L).\nSo, if Ram covered $d_R_1$ in first meeting, he would cover $3d_R_1$ in second meeting if they had identical starting positions and continuous swimming.\nHowever, in our case, Ram has a different starting position for the second part (from shallow end for the journey to the 2nd meeting).\n\nLet L be the length of the pool.\nFirst meeting: Ram (started from deep end) has covered 18.5 meters. Shyam (started from shallow end) has covered L - 18.5 meters.\nRatio of speeds = Ram's speed / Shyam's speed = 18.5 / (L - 18.5).\n\nSecond meeting:\nThey have each completed 1 length. Ram is at the shallow end, Shyam is at the deep end.\nThey start their 'return' stroke.\nThey meet 10.5 meters from the shallow end. This means Ram has covered L - 10.5 meters on his return stroke and Shyam has covered L - 10.5 meters on his return stroke until they meet, from their starting points for the return stroke.\nWait, \"just 10.5 m from the shallow end\" means the distance from the shallow end.\n\nIf Ram started from the deep end:\nAt the first meeting, Ram has covered 18.5 m. Shyam has covered L - 18.5 m.\nAt the second meeting, Ram has covered L (first length) + (L - 10.5) (on return length). Total = 2L - 10.5 m.\nShyam has covered L (first length) + 10.5 (on return length). Total = L + 10.5 m.\n\nFrom this approach, L=33.\n\nLet's try a different interpretation of the problem statement.\nLet's use the concept that the ratio of distances traveled by the two swimmers is constant until they meet.\nCase 1: They meet for the first time. Total distance covered by both is L.\nRam's distance = 18.5. Shyam's distance = L - 18.5.\nSo, $\\frac{V_R}{V_S} = \\frac{18.5}{L - 18.5}$.\n\nCase 2: They meet for the second time. Total distance covered by both is 3L.\nThe problem mentions a rest of 45 sec. But it states \"after setting off on the return length\", implying the rest period is over before the second encounter. This does not affect relative distances covered.\nWhen they meet for the second time, they have completed their first length and are on their return journey.\nRam has covered L (one length) + $x_2$ (part of second length).\nShyam has covered L (one length) + $y_2$ (part of second length).\n$x_2 + y_2 = L$.\nThey meet 10.5 m from the shallow end.\nIf Ram started from the deep end, he reached the shallow end and turned back. He traveled $x_2 = L - 10.5$ m from the shallow end.\nIf Shyam started from the shallow end, he reached the deep end and turned back. He traveled $y_2 = 10.5$ m from the deep end.\nSo, Ram's total distance = L + (L - 10.5) = 2L - 10.5.\nShyam's total distance = L + 10.5.\nSo, $\\frac{V_R}{V_S} = \\frac{2L - 10.5}{L + 10.5}$.\n\nEquating the ratios:\n$\\frac{18.5}{L - 18.5} = \\frac{2L - 10.5}{L + 10.5}$\n$18.5(L + 10.5) = (L - 18.5)(2L - 10.5)$\n$18.5L + 194.25 = 2L^2 - 10.5L - 37L + 194.25$\n$18.5L = 2L^2 - 47.5L$\n$0 = 2L^2 - 66L$\n$0 = 2L(L - 33)$\nSince L cannot be 0, L = 33.\n\nThis is consistently giving L=33. Let's reconsider the problem statement from a different angle or perhaps there's a misunderstanding of \"1st pass at a point 18.5 m from the deep end\".\nIf \"hardy is faster than andy\" and Ram is Hardy and Shyam is Andy.\nRam starts from deep end, Shyam from shallow end.\nAt 1st pass, Ram has traveled 18.5m. Shyam has traveled L-18.5m.\nSince Ram is faster, 18.5 > L-18.5 => 2L < 37. Which means L < 18.5. This contradicts the options.\nIf L=33, then Ram travels 18.5m and Shyam travels 33-18.5 = 14.5m. This means Ram is faster which is consistent with the condition.\n\nLet's re-read \"they 1 st pass at a point 18.5 m from the deep end\". This means the point where they meet is 18.5m away from the deep end.\nLet the deep end be at x=0 and shallow end be at x=L.\nRam starts at x=0, Shyam starts at x=L.\n1st meeting: at x = 18.5.\nRam's distance = 18.5. Shyam's distance = L-18.5.\n\n2nd meeting: \"just 10.5 m from the shallow end\". This means x = L - 10.5.\nAfter 1st length, Ram is at L, Shyam is at 0.\nRam turns around, travels towards x=0. Shyam turns around, travels towards x=L.\nThey meet at L - 10.5.\nRam's distance on the second leg = L - (L - 10.5) = 10.5 m.\nShyam's distance on the second leg = (L - 10.5) - 0 = L - 10.5 m.\n\nTotal distance for Ram = L + 10.5.\nTotal distance for Shyam = L + (L - 10.5) = 2L - 10.5.\n\nNow compare the ratios of distances for Ram and Shyam (or speeds).\nIf Ram is faster: $R/S > 1$.\nFrom 1st meeting: $18.5 / (L - 18.5)$.\nIf $R > S$, then $18.5 > L - 18.5 \\implies 2L < 37 \\implies L < 18.5$. This contradicts the options.\n\nTherefore, Ram must be the slower swimmer.\nSo, from 1st meeting, distance travelled by Ram = 18.5m means distance from deep end.\nIf Ram starts from deep end, he travels 18.5m.\nShyam starts from shallow end, he travels L-18.5m.\nThe problem states \"Hardy is faster than Andy\". Assuming Ram = Hardy and Shyam = Andy contradicts this.\nSo, let Ram be Andy (slower) and Shyam be Hardy (faster).\nShyam (faster) starts from the deep end, Ram (slower) starts from the shallow end.\n1st meeting: Shyam (faster) travels 18.5m. Ram (slower) travels L-18.5m.\nRatio of speeds (Shyam/Ram) = 18.5 / (L - 18.5).\nSince Shyam is faster, 18.5 > L - 18.5 => 2L < 37 => L < 18.5. This still yields L < 18.5.\n\nLet's consider the meeting point from the deep end definition.\nLet L be the length of the pool.\nLet A's speed be $V_A$ and B's speed be $V_B$. Assume A starts from the deep end (0) and B starts from the shallow end (L).\nFirst meeting: point P1 = 18.5m from the deep end.\nSo, A swam 18.5m. B swam L - 18.5m.\n$\\frac{V_A}{V_B} = \\frac{18.5}{L - 18.5}$.\n\nSecond meeting: point P2 = 10.5m from the shallow end. So P2 = L - 10.5m from the deep end.\nAfter the first length, A is at L, B is at 0.\nThey turn back. A swims from L towards 0. B swims from 0 towards L.\nThey meet at L - 10.5.\nA has traveled L (first length) + (L - (L - 10.5)) (on return journey) = L + 10.5 m.\nB has traveled L (first length) + (L - 10.5) (on return journey) = 2L - 10.5 m.\n$\\frac{V_A}{V_B} = \\frac{L + 10.5}{2L - 10.5}$.\n\nNow, equate the ratios:\n$\\frac{18.5}{L - 18.5} = \\frac{L + 10.5}{2L - 10.5}$\n$18.5(2L - 10.5) = (L - 18.5)(L + 10.5)$\n$37L - 194.25 = L^2 + 10.5L - 18.5L - 194.25$\n$37L = L^2 - 8L$\n$0 = L^2 - 45L$\n$0 = L(L - 45)$\nSince L cannot be 0, L = 45.\n\nThis solution (L=45) is one of the options.\nLet's check the condition \"hardy is faster than andy\".\nLet Ram be A and Shyam be B. The problem says \"Hardy is faster than Andy\".\nIf Ram is Hardy (faster) and Shyam is Andy (slower).\nSo A is faster than B. $V_A > V_B$.\nFrom the first ratio: $\\frac{18.5}{L - 18.5} > 1 \\implies 18.5 > L - 18.5 \\implies 2L < 37 \\implies L < 18.5$. This contradicts L=45.\n\nSo, Ram (A) is Andy (slower) and Shyam (B) is Hardy (faster).\nSo $V_A < V_B$.\nFrom the first ratio: $\\frac{18.5}{L - 18.5} < 1 \\implies 18.5 < L - 18.5 \\implies 2L > 37 \\implies L > 18.5$.\nThis is consistent with L=45. L=45 > 18.5.\n\nLet's assume Ram is slower than Shyam.\nRam starts from deep end, Shyam starts from shallow end.\n1st meeting:\nRam's distance = 18.5m. Shyam's distance = L-18.5m.\nSince Shyam is faster, he covers more distance. So $L - 18.5 > 18.5 \\implies L > 37$.\nThis is consistent with L=45.\n\nLet's check the second meeting with L=45.\nRam total distance = L + 10.5 = 45 + 10.5 = 55.5 m.\nShyam total distance = 2L - 10.5 = 2(45) - 10.5 = 90 - 10.5 = 79.5 m.\nRatio (Shyam/Ram) = 79.5 / 55.5 = 1.432.\nRatio from first meeting (Shyam/Ram) = (L - 18.5) / 18.5 = (45 - 18.5) / 18.5 = 26.5 / 18.5 = 1.432.\nThe ratios match.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{E}$",
