 faces disappear.\nAssume linear joining.\nThe two end triangular faces are exposed.\nThe joining faces disappear.\nConsider the faces of each prism.\nPrism 1: 2 triangles, R1, R2, R3.\nPrism 2: 2 triangles, S1, S2, S3.\nPrism 3: 2 triangles, T1, T2, T3.\nJoin R1 to S1, S2 to T1.\nExposed faces:\n2 triangles from P1.\nR2, R3 from P1.\nS3 from P2.\n2 triangles from P3.\nT2, T3 from P3.\nTotal individual faces: $2 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9$.\n\nNow, apply \"count each such large face as one\".\nThe two end triangular faces are large.\nThe rectangular faces. If arranged linearly, the rectangular faces on the same side are co-planar.\nSo, the three rectangular faces of P1 along one side would form one large face. The three of P2 on the same side form another large face. The three of P3 on the same side form a third large face.\nThis means we consider the three rectangular faces of each prism together on one side as one large face.\nIf three prisms are aligned linearly, the two end triangular faces are counted as 2 large faces.\nThe rectangular faces. Consider the three prisms placed end-on-end.\nThe side surface is formed by adjacent rectangular faces.\nIf the three prisms are aligned in a line, then the rectangular faces on the top form one large rectangular surface. The rectangular faces on the bottom form another large rectangular surface. The rectangular faces on the back form a third large rectangular surface.\nSo, 2 (triangles) + 3 (large rectangles) = 5. This is not an option.\n\nLet's assume the interpretation that leads to 10.\n2 triangular end faces.\nThe joining is along the rectangular faces.\nIf three prisms are placed end-to-end, the intermediate triangular faces are hidden.\nThe exposed faces are the two end triangular faces.\nAnd the rectangular faces.\nConsider prismatic structure.\nEach prism has 3 rectangular faces.\nWhen joined end-to-end, the two intermediate rectangular faces disappear.\nSo, each end prism contributes its two outer rectangular faces. The