    "global index": "MathQA_128",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: rani bought more apples than oranges . she sells apples at ₹ 23 apiece and makes 15 % profit . she sells oranges at ₹ 10 apiece and makes 25 % profit . if she gets ₹ 653 after selling all the apples and oranges , find her profit percentage z .\n\nOptions: \nA. 16.8 %\nB. 17.4 %\nC. 17.9 %\nD. 18.5 %\nE. 19.1 %\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the cost price of an apple be $C_a$ and the cost price of an orange be $C_o$.\nRani sells apples at ₹ 23 apiece and makes 15% profit.\nSo, selling price of an apple = $C_a + 0.15 C_a = 1.15 C_a$.\nGiven, selling price of an apple = ₹ 23.\n$1.15 C_a = 23$\n$C_a = 23 / 1.15 = 20$.\n\nRani sells oranges at ₹ 10 apiece and makes 25% profit.\nSo, selling price of an orange = $C_o + 0.25 C_o = 1.25 C_o$.\nGiven, selling price of an orange = ₹ 10.\n$1.25 C_o = 10$\n$C_o = 10 / 1.25 = 8$.\n\nLet the number of apples Rani bought be $n_a$ and the number of oranges Rani bought be $n_o$.\nWe are given that Rani bought more apples than oranges, so $n_a > n_o$.\n\nTotal selling price of all apples = $23 \\times n_a$.\nTotal selling price of all oranges = $10 \\times n_o$.\nTotal selling price = $23 n_a + 10 n_o = 653$.\n\nTotal cost price of all apples = $20 \\times n_a$.\nTotal cost price of all oranges = $8 \\times n_o$.\nTotal cost price = $20 n_a + 8 n_o$.\n\nTotal profit = Total selling price - Total cost price\nTotal profit = $653 - (20 n_a + 8 n_o)$.\n\nProfit percentage $z = \\frac{\\text{Total profit}}{\\text{Total cost price}} \\times 100$\n$z = \\frac{653 - (20 n_a + 8 n_o)}{20 n_a + 8 n_o} \\times 100$.\n\n We need to find integer values for $n_a$ and $n_o$ such that $n_a > n_o$ and $23 n_a + 10 n_o = 653$.\n\nLet's test some values for $n_a$. Since $n_a > n_o$, the maximum possible value for $n_o$ is $n_a - 1$.\nMinimum total selling price if $n_a = n_o + 1$ is $23(n_o+1) + 10 n_o = 33 n_o + 23$.\nSo $33 n_o + 23 \\le 653$, $33 n_o \\le 630$, $n_o \\le 19.09$.\nSo $n_o$ can be at most 19.\nIf $n_o = 19$, then $23 n_a + 10(19) = 653$, $23 n_a + 190 = 653$, $23 n_a = 463$, $n_a = 463/23 \\approx 20.13$. Not an integer.\n\nLet's rearrange the equation: $23 n_a = 653 - 10 n_o$.\nSince $653 - 10 n_o$ must be divisible by 23.\nWe can check values of $n_o$ from 0 up to a certain limit.\nIf $n_o = 3$: $23 n_a = 653 - 30 = 623$. $n_a = 623/23 = 27.08$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 4$: $23 n_a = 653 - 40 = 613$. $613/23 \\approx 26.65$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 5$: $23 n_a = 653 - 50 = 603$. $603/23 \\approx 26.21$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 6$: $23 n_a = 653 - 60 = 593$. $593/23 \\approx 25.78$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 7$: $23 n_a = 653 - 70 = 583$. $583/23 = 25.34$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 8$: $23 n_a = 653 - 80 = 573$. $573/23 \\approx 24.91$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 9$: $23 n_a = 653 - 90 = 563$. $563/23 \\approx 24.47$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 10$: $23 n_a = 653 - 100 = 553$. $553/23 \\approx 24.04$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 11$: $23 n_a = 653 - 110 = 543$. $543/23 \\approx 23.6$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 12$: $23 n_a = 653 - 120 = 533$. $533/23 = 23.17$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 13$: $23 n_a = 653 - 130 = 523$. $523/23 \\approx 22.73$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 14$: $23 n_a = 653 - 140 = 513$. $513/23 \\approx 22.3$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 15$: $23 n_a = 653 - 150 = 503$. $503/23 \\approx 21.87$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 16$: $23 n_a = 653 - 160 = 493$. $493/23 = 21.43$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 17$: $23 n_a = 653 - 170 = 483$. $483/23 \\approx 21$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 18$: $23 n_a = 653 - 180 = 473$. $473/23 \\approx 20.56$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 21$: $23 n_a = 653 - 210 = 443$. $443/23 \\approx 19.26$. Not integer.\nIf $n_o = 23$: $23 n_a = 653 - 230 = 423$. $423/23 \\approx 18.39$. Not integer.\nConsider remainders modulo 23: $653 \\equiv 653 - 2300 = 653 - 23 \\times 28 = 653 - 644 = 9 \\pmod{23}$.\n$23 n_a \\equiv 0 \\pmod{23}$.\n$10 n_o \\equiv 653 - 23 n_a \\equiv 9 \\pmod{23}$.\nWe need to solve $10 n_o \\equiv 9 \\pmod{23}$.\nMultiply by the inverse of 10 modulo 23.\n$10 \\times 7 = 70 = 3 \\times 23 + 1$. So, the inverse of 10 modulo 23 is 7.\n$7 \\times 10 n_o \\equiv 7 \\times 9 \\pmod{23}$.\n$n_o \\equiv 63 \\pmod{23}$.\n$63 = 2 \\times 23 + 17$.\n$n_o \\equiv 17 \\pmod{23}$.\nSo possible values for $n_o$ are 17, 40, 63, ...\n\nLet $n_o = 17$.\n$23 n_a + 10(17) = 653$.\n$23 n_a + 170 = 653$.\n$23 n_a = 653 - 170 = 483$.\n$n_a = 483 / 23 = 21$.\nSo, $n_a = 21$ and $n_o = 17$.\nThis satisfies the condition $n_a > n_o$ (21 > 17).\n\nNow calculate the total cost price:\nTotal cost price = $20 n_a + 8 n_o = 20(21) + 8(17) = 420 + 136 = 556$.\nTotal selling price = 653.\nTotal profit = Total selling price - Total cost price = $653 - 556 = 97$.\n\nProfit percentage $z = \\frac{\\text{Total profit}}{\\text{Total cost price}} \\times 100 = \\frac{97}{556} \\times 100$.\n$z \\approx 0.17446 \\times 100 \\approx 17.446 \\%$.\n\nLet's check if there are other integer solutions for $n_a > n_o$.\nIf $n_o = 17 + 23 = 40$.\n$23 n_a + 10(40) = 653$.\n$23 n_a + 400 = 653$.\n$23 n_a = 253$.\n$n_a = 253 / 23 \\approx 11$. This would mean $n_a < n_o$, which contradicts the condition.\nSo, $n_a = 21$ and $n_o = 17$ is the only valid integer solution.\n\nThe profit percentage is approximately 17.446%.\nComparing with the options:\nA. 16.8 %\nB. 17.4 %\nC. 17.9 %\nD. 18.5 %\nE. 19.1 %\n\nThe closest option is 17.4 %.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
