    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10447",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: At approximately what temperature will water boil ifthe elevationis 10,000 ft?\n\nOptions: \nA. 206°F\nB. 193°F\nC. 190°F\nD. 212°F\nE. 180°F\nF. 100°F\nG. 185°F\nH. 202°F\nI. 215°F\nJ. 170°F\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The boiling point of water decreases with increasing elevation due to lower atmospheric pressure. At sea level, water boils at 212°F (100°C). As you go higher, the pressure drops, and thus less energy is needed to convert water from liquid to gas.\n\nBased on general knowledge of how boiling point changes with altitude, 10,000 feet is a significant elevation.\n\nLet's look at the options:\nD. 212°F is the boiling point at sea level.\nF. 100°F is too low; water would be far from boiling at this temperature at 10,000 ft.\nI. 215°F is higher than sea level boiling point, which is incorrect for higher elevation.\nJ. 170°F is likely too low for 10,000 feet.\n\nThe decrease in boiling point is approximately 1°F for every 500 feet increase in elevation.\nSo for 10,000 feet, the decrease would be roughly 10,000 ft / 500 ft/°F = 20°F.\n212°F - 20°F = 192°F.\n\nLooking at the options, 193°F is the closest to this calculation.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$",
      "success": true,
