      "generated_answer": "The Laplace transform of $\\cos(kt)$ is given by the formula $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos(kt)\\} = \\frac{s}{s^2 + k^2}$. This transform is valid for $s > 0$. Therefore, we need to find the option that matches this result.\n\nComparing this to the given options:\nA. $k / (s^2 + k^2)$\nB. $k / (s^2 + k^2)$\nC. $1 / (s^2 + k^2)$\nD. $k^2 / (s^2 + k^2)$\nE. $s / (s^2 + k^2)$, for $s < 0$\nF. $s / (s^2 + k^2)$, for $s > 0$\nG. $k / (s^2 - k^2)$\nH. $s^2 / (s^2 + k^2)$\nI. $s / (s^2 - k^2)$\nJ. $s / (s^2 - k^2)$\n\nOption F matches the standard Laplace transform of $\\cos(kt)$ and the condition $s > 0$. This formula comes directly from the definition of the Laplace transform and can be derived using integration by parts.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
