    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10274",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Electrical analogous quantity for spring element (K) in force-voltage analogy is\n\nOptions: \nA. V\nB. 1/C\nC. C\nD. L\nE. LC\nF. 1/L\nG. 1/R\nH. RC\nI. I\nJ. R\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In the force-voltage analogy, we equate mechanical quantities with their electrical counterparts. The relationship for a spring is $F = Kx$, where $F$ is force, $K$ is the spring constant, and $x$ is displacement. In the electrical domain, the analogous relationship for a capacitor is $i = C \\frac{dV}{dt}$, where $i$ is current, $C$ is capacitance, and $V$ is voltage. If we consider force analogous to voltage ($F \\leftrightarrow V$) and velocity to current ($v \\leftrightarrow i$), then $\\int F dt \\leftrightarrow \\int V dt$ and $x = \\int v dt \\leftrightarrow \\int i dt$. Thus, $F = Kx$ becomes $V \\leftrightarrow K \\int i dt$. Comparing this with $i = C \\frac{dV}{dt}$, which can be rewritten as $\\int i dt = C V$, we see that $K$ is analogous to $\\frac{1}{C}$.\n\nThe correct answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 126,
        "output_tokens": 215,
        "total_tokens": 341
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
