$1000 / 32 = 31.25$. So 31 signals. Not an option.\n\nLet's consider if the wording \"allowable voice bandwidth for telephone line transmission is 3kHz\" means that we are transmitting a band-limited signal which, when sampled at Nyquist, gives 6000 samples/sec.\n\nLet's assume the problem is designed such that one of the options is the intended answer, and there might be a slight approximation or a standard value not explicitly mentioned.\n\nIf we assume option A (45 signals) is correct, then the bit rate per signal would be $1000 \\text{ kbps} / 45 \\approx 22.22 \\text{ kbps}$.\nIf we assume option D (50 signals) is correct, then the bit rate per signal would be $1000 \\text{ kbps} / 50 = 20 \\text{ kbps}$.\n\nOur calculated value is 24 kbps for a single voice signal. This is between the implied bit rates for 45 signals (22.22 kbps) and 50 signals (20 kbps).\n\nLet's consider if the sampling rate might be slightly adjusted for practical TDM.\nIf we pack signals into time slots, and the total time available per bit period $1 \\mu s$ is used for sending bits of all multiplexed signals.\n\nOne possibility is related to how bits are grouped within a frame.\nA frame consists of N samples from each of the N voice signals. Each sample is 4 bits.\nIf the sampling rate is $f_s$, then $f_s = 6$ kHz.\nThe duration of one sample is $1/f_s = 1/6000$ s.\nIn one frame, we have N samples, so the frame duration could be related to $N \\times (1/f_s)$.\n\nLet's revisit the total bit rate and bit rate per signal.\nTotal bit rate = 1 Mbps.\nBit rate per signal = 24 kbps.\nNumber of signals = 41.67.\n\nLet's speculate that the intended answer might be based on some round numbers or common multiplexing ratios.\n\nConsider the possibility of channelization. If we are using TDM, we are essentially dividing the total time into slots.\n\nLet's re-examine some common TDM systems. E1/T1 systems have their own defined frame structures.\n\nLet's assume there is a rounding or simplification ex