0 / 420.0 = 1.90. In option J, it's 720.6 / 390.8 = 1.84.\n\nThe ratio of life at 90% reliability to life at 96% reliability should be greater than 1. The standard formula for life adjustment based on reliability $R$ is often given as $L_R = L_{10} * (E_u / 0.9)^{1/b}$, where $E_u$ is the desired reliability in decimal form. For radial roller bearings, $b \\approx 10/3$.\nSo, $L_{90} = L_{10}$ and $L_{96} = L_{10} * (0.96)^{3/10} \\approx L_{10} * 1.072$. This is incorrect.\n\nLet's use the correct relationship: $L_R = L_{10} * (ln(1/0.9) / ln(1/R))^{1/b}$, where b is the Weibull slope. For roller bearings, b = 10/3.\nSo, $L_{90} = L_{10}$ (this is the definition of L10).\n$L_{96} = L_{10} * (ln(1/0.9) / ln(1/0.96))^{3/10} = L_{10} * (0.10536 / 0.04082)^{0.3} = L_{10} * (2.581)^{0.3} = L_{10} * 1.337$.\nThis ratio of $L_{10} / L_{96}$ should be around $0.748$.\nThis means $L_{90} / L_{96} = 1 / 1.337 \\approx 0.748$.\n\nThe question asks for \"bearing life for 90% reliability\" and \"bearing life for 96% reliability\". This means we are looking for a pair $(L_{90}, L_{96})$.\nOur calculation shows $L_{96} = 1.337 * L_{90}$.\nSo, $L_{90} / L_{96} = 1 / 1.337 \\approx 0.748$.\n\nThis implies that for a higher reliability (96%), the bearing life is *longer* than for lower reliability (90%) under the same load conditions, which contradicts common interpretation where L10 is the minimum life for 90% of bearings. However, the phrasing \"bearing life for X% reliability\" refers to the expected life at that reliability level.\n\nLet's consider another common way to express this, where the life is *decreased* for higher reliability when referring to the rating life. But typically, L10 is the life that 90% of bearings will meet or exceed. So, the life for 96% reliability would be expected to be higher, assuming sufficient load capacity.\n\nLet's re-read the question VERY carefully: \"(a)bearinglife for 90% reliability (b)bearinglife for 96% reliability\". This means we are ca