      "generated_answer": "To find the inverse z-transform using partial fraction expansion, we first perform the expansion of $F(z)/z$.\n$F(z)/z = (2z^2 - 2z) / [z(z - 3)(z - 5)^2] = (2z - 7) / [(z - 3)(z - 5)^2]$\nLet $F(z)/z = A/(z-3) + B/(z-5) + C/(z-5)^2$\nMultiplying by $(z-3)(z-5)^2$, we get:\n$2z - 7 = A(z-5)^2 + B(z-3)(z-5) + C(z-3)$\nSetting $z=3$, we get $2(3)-7 = A(3-5)^2$, so $-1 = 4A$, which means $A = -1/4$.\nSetting $z=5$, we get $2(5)-7 = C(5-3)$, so $3 = 2C$, which means $C = 3/2$.\nTo find B, we can compare the coefficients of $z^2$. The coefficient of $z^2$ on the left is 0. On the right, it is $A + B$. So, $0 = A + B$, which means $B = -A = 1/4$.\nThus, $F(z)/z = (-1/4)/(z-3) + (1/4)/(z-5) + (3/2)/(z-5)^2$.\nNow, multiply by z to get $F(z)$:\n$F(z) = (-1/4)z/(z-3) + (1/4)z/(z-5) + (3/2)z/(z-5)^2$.\nWe know the inverse z-transform of $z/(z-a)$ is $a^n u[n]$.\nFor the term $(3/2)z/(z-5)^2$, we use the property that the inverse z-transform of $z/(z-a)^2$ is $na^{n-1}u[n]$. So, the inverse z-transform of $(3/2)z/(z-5)^2$ is $(3/2) * 5^{n-1} * n * u[n] = (3/10) * n * 5^n * u[n] = [(3/10)n] * 5^n * u[n]$.\nTherefore, $f[n] = (-1/4) * 3^n * u[n] + (1/4) * 5^n * u[n] + (3/2) * (n-1) * 5^{n-1} * u[n]$.\nLet's recheck the partial fraction expansion.\n$F(z) = (2z^2 - 2z) / [(z - 3) (z - 5)^2]$.\nLet $F(z) = A/(z-3) + B/(z-5) + C/(z-5)^2$.\nMultiplying by $(z-3)(z-5)^2$:\n$2z^2 - 2z = A(z-5)^2 + B(z-3)(z-5) + C(z-3)$.\nSet $z=3$: $2(3)^2 - 2(3) = A(3-5)^2 \\implies 18 - 6 = A(-2)^2 \\implies 12 = 4A \\implies A = 3$.\nSet $z=5$: $2(5)^2 - 2(5) = C(5-3) \\implies 50 - 10 = C(2) \\implies 40 = 2C \\implies C = 20$.\nTo find B, compare coefficient of $z^2$: $2 = A + B$. So, $2 = 3 + B \\implies B = -1$.\nThus, $F(z) = 3/(z-3) - 1/(z-5) + 20/(z-5)^2$.\nWe know the inverse z-transform of $a^n u[n]$ is $z/(z-a)$.\nFor $1/(z-a)$, the inverse transform is $a^{n-1}u[n-1]$.\nFor $F(z)$, we want terms of the form $z/(z-a)$ or related.\nLet's consider $F(z) = 3 * z/(z-