    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10334",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A three input information channel with all three input symbols equally probable, is defined by the channel matrix, \\vert0.50.30.2\\vert \\vert0.20.30.5\\vert \\vert0.30.30.4\\vert Assuming that a maximum-likelihood decision rule is used, determine the error probability, P_E, of the channel.\n\nOptions: \nA. (13) / (30)\nB. (19) / (30)\nC. (15) / (30)\nD. (18) / (30)\nE. (14) / (30)\nF. (17) / (30)\nG. (21) / (30)\nH. (12) / (30)\nI. (16) / (30)\nJ. (20) / (30)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The channel matrix represents the conditional probabilities of receiving an output symbol given a transmitted input symbol. Since all input symbols are equally probable, the prior probabilities are $P(x_1) = P(x_2) = P(x_3) = 1/3$. For a maximum-likelihood decision rule, we choose the output symbol that maximizes the joint probability $P(y_j, x_i) = P(y_j|x_i)P(x_i)$. The error probability is the sum of probabilities of incorrect decisions. This problem requires calculating the total probability of error for each input symbol and then averaging them.\n\nThe error probability can be calculated by summing the probabilities of incorrect decoding for each input symbol and then averaging over the input symbols. Given the channel matrix $P(y_j|x_i)$ and equal input probabilities $P(x_i) = 1/3$, for each input $x_i$, we find the most likely output $y_j$. The error occurs when the actual output is not the most likely one for that input.\n\nLet $C$ be the channel matrix where $C_{ij} = P(y_j|x_i)$.\n$C = \\begin{pmatrix} 0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.3 & 0.4 \\end{pmatrix}$\nSince $P(x_1) = P(x_2) = P(x_3) = 1/3$, the joint probabilities are $P(y_j, x_i) = P(y_j|x_i)P(x_i) = \\frac{1}{3} C_{ij}$.\n\nFor input $x_1$: The probabilities of receiving outputs are $P(y_1|x_1)=0.5$, $P(y_2|x_1)=0.3$, $P(y_3|x_1)=0.2$. The maximum likelihood decision for $x_1$ is to choose $y_1$. The probability of error for $x_1$ is $P(E|x_1) = P(y_2|x_1) + P(y_3|x_1) = 0.3 + 0.2 = 0.5$.\n\nFor input $x_2$: The probabilities of receiving outputs are $P(y_1|x_2)=0.2$, $P(y_2|x_2)=0.3$, $P(y_3|x_2)=0.5$. The maximum likelihood decision for $x_2$ is to choose $y_3$. The probability of error for $x_2$ is $P(E|x_2) = P(y_1|x_2) + P(y_2|x_2) = 0.2 + 0.3 = 0.5$.\n\nFor input $x_3$: The probabilities of receiving outputs are $P(y_1|x_3)=0.3$, $P(y_2|x_3)=0.3$, $P(y_3|x_3)=0.4$. The maximum likelihood decision for $x_3$ is to choose $y_3$. The probability of error for $x_3$ is $P(E|x_3) = P(y_1|x_3) + P(y_2|x_3) = 0.3 + 0.3 = 0.6$.\n\nThe total error probability is $P_E = P(E|x_1)P(x_1) + P(E|x_2)P(x_2) + P(E|x_3)P(x_3)$.\n$P_E = 0.5 \\times \\frac{1}{3} + 0.5 \\times \\frac{1}{3} + 0.6 \\times \\frac{1}{3}$\n$P_E = \\frac{1}{3} (0.5 + 0.5 + 0.6) = \\frac{1}{3} (1.6) = \\frac{1.6}{3} = \\frac{16}{30}$.\n\nLet's recheck the maximum likelihood decision.\nFor input $x_1$: $P(y_1|x_1)=0.5$, $P(y_2|x_1)=0.3$, $P(y_3|x_1)=0.2$. Maximum is $y_1$. Error is $P(y_2|x_1) + P(y_3|x_1) = 0.3 + 0.2 = 0.5$.\nFor input $x_2$: $P(y_1|x_2)=0.2$, $P(y_2|x_2)=0.3$, $P(y_3|x_2)=0.5$. Maximum is $y_3$. Error is $P(y_1|x_2) + P(y_2|x_2) = 0.2 + 0.3 = 0.5$.\nFor input $x_3$: $P(y_1|x_3)=0.3$, $P(y_2|x_3)=0.3$, $P(y_3|x_3)=0.4$. Maximum is $y_3$. Error is $P(y_1|x_3) + P(y_2|x_3) = 0.3 + 0.3 = 0.6$.\n\n$P_E = \\frac{1}{3} (0.5 + 0.5 + 0.6) = \\frac{1.6}{3} = \\frac{16}{30}$.\n\nLet's check the options. Option I is $16/30$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
