      "generated_answer": "To find the Fourier sine series of $f(x) = x^2$ over the interval $(0, 1)$, we need to calculate the coefficients $b_n$ using the formula:\n$b_n = \\frac{2}{L} \\int_0^L f(x) \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right) dx$\nIn this case, $L=1$, so the formula becomes:\n$b_n = 2 \\int_0^1 x^2 \\sin(n\\pi x) dx$\n\nWe can use integration by parts twice.\nLet $u = x^2$, $dv = \\sin(n\\pi x) dx$. Then $du = 2x dx$, $v = -\\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi x)$.\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ \\left[ -\\frac{x^2}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi x) \\right]_0^1 - \\int_0^1 -\\frac{2x}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi x) dx \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi) - 0 + \\frac{2}{n\\pi} \\int_0^1 x \\cos(n\\pi x) dx \\right]$\nSince $\\cos(n\\pi) = (-1)^n$,\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{(-1)^n}{n\\pi} + \\frac{2}{n\\pi} \\int_0^1 x \\cos(n\\pi x) dx \\right]$\n\nNow, let's evaluate the integral $\\int_0^1 x \\cos(n\\pi x) dx$.\nLet $u = x$, $dv = \\cos(n\\pi x) dx$. Then $du = dx$, $v = \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x)$.\n$\\int_0^1 x \\cos(n\\pi x) dx = \\left[ \\frac{x}{n\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right]_0^1 - \\int_0^1 \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x) dx$\n$= \\left[ \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi) - 0 \\right] - \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\left[ -\\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi x) \\right]_0^1$\nSince $\\sin(n\\pi) = 0$, the first term is 0.\n$= - \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\left[ -\\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi) - (-\\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\cos(0)) \\right]$\n$= - \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\left[ -\\frac{1}{n\\pi} (-1)^n + \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\right]$\n$= \\frac{(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2} - \\frac{1}{n^2\\pi^2} = \\frac{(-1)^n - 1}{n^2\\pi^2}$\n\nSubstitute this back into the expression for $b_n$:\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{(-1)^n}{n\\pi} + \\frac{2}{n\\pi} \\left( \\frac{(-1)^n - 1}{n^2\\pi^2} \\right) \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{(-1)^n}{n\\pi} + \\frac{2(-1)^n - 2}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ \\frac{-(-1)^n n^2\\pi^2 + 2(-1)^n - 2}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right]$\nThis does not match any of the options directly. Let's recheck the calculation or try to simplify it differently.\n\nLet's use the standard formula for integration by parts for $\\int x^2 \\sin(ax) dx$:\n$\\int x^2 \\sin(ax) dx = -\\frac{x^2}{a}\\cos(ax) + \\frac{2x}{a^2}\\sin(ax) + \\frac{2}{a^3}\\cos(ax) + C$\nHere $a = n\\pi$.\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{x^2}{n\\pi}\\cos(n\\pi x) + \\frac{2x}{(n\\pi)^2}\\sin(n\\pi x) + \\frac{2}{(n\\pi)^3}\\cos(n\\pi x) \\right]_0^1$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ \\left(-\\frac{1^2}{n\\pi}\\cos(n\\pi) + \\frac{2(1)}{(n\\pi)^2}\\sin(n\\pi) + \\frac{2}{(n\\pi)^3}\\cos(n\\pi)\\right) - \\left(0 + 0 + \\frac{2}{(n\\pi)^3}\\cos(0)\\right) \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{1}{n\\pi}(-1)^n + \\frac{2}{(n\\pi)^2}(0) + \\frac{2}{(n\\pi)^3}(-1)^n - \\frac{2}{(n\\pi)^3}(1) \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{(-1)^n}{n\\pi} + \\frac{2(-1)^n}{(n\\pi)^3} - \\frac{2}{(n\\pi)^3} \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{(-1)^n}{n\\pi} + \\frac{2[(-1)^n - 1]}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi} + \\frac{2[(-1)^n - 1]}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right]$\nSince $(-1)^n - 1 = - (1 - (-1)^n)$,\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi} - \\frac{2[1 - (-1)^n]}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right]$\n\nLet's check the options with this result.\nOption F: $2 \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left[ \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi} + \\frac{2[1 - (-1)^n]}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right] \\sin(n\\pi x)$\nOur calculated $b_n$ is $2 \\left[ \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi} - \\frac{2[1 - (-1)^n]}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right]$.\nSo, let's recheck the third term in the indefinite integral.\n$\\int \\frac{2}{a^2} \\sin(ax) dx = -\\frac{2}{a^3} \\cos(ax)$.\nThe formula was $\\int x^2 \\sin(ax) dx = -\\frac{x^2}{a}\\cos(ax) + \\frac{2x}{a^2}\\sin(ax) - \\frac{2}{a^3}\\cos(ax) + C$.\nLet's use integration by parts again carefully.\n$b_n = 2 \\int_0^1 x^2 \\sin(n\\pi x) dx$\n$= 2 \\left[ -\\frac{x^2}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi x) \\right]_0^1 + 2 \\int_0^1 \\frac{2x}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi x) dx$\n$= 2 \\left[ -\\frac{(-1)^n}{n\\pi} \\right] + \\frac{4}{n\\pi} \\int_0^1 x \\cos(n\\pi x) dx$\nNow consider $\\int_0^1 x \\cos(n\\pi x) dx = \\left[ \\frac{x}{n\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right]_0^1 - \\int_0^1 \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x) dx$\n$= 0 - \\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\left[ -\\frac{1}{n\\pi} \\cos(n\\pi x) \\right]_0^1$\n$= \\frac{1}{n^2\\pi^2} [\\cos(n\\pi) - \\cos(0)]$\n$= \\frac{1}{n^2\\pi^2} [(-1)^n - 1]$\nSubstituting this back:\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{(-1)^n}{n\\pi} \\right] + \\frac{4}{n\\pi} \\left[ \\frac{(-1)^n - 1}{n^2\\pi^2} \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ -\\frac{(-1)^n}{n\\pi} + \\frac{2[(-1)^n - 1]}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right]$\n$b_n = 2 \\left[ \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi} - \\frac{2[1 - (-1)^n]}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right]$\nSo the Fourier sine series is:\n$x^2 \\sim_s 2 \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left[ \\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi} - \\frac{2[1 - (-1)^n]}{n^3\\pi^3} \\right] \\sin(n\\pi x)$\n\nThis matches option I.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$",
