      "generated_answer": "Let H denote heads and T denote tails. The sample space for tossing a coin 3 times is {HHH, HHT, HTH, THH, HTT, THT, TTH, TTT}. There are $2^3 = 8$ possible outcomes, and each outcome has a probability of $1/8$ assuming a fair coin.\n\n(a) If it is known that the first toss is heads, the reduced sample space is {HHH, HHT, HTH, HTT}. We want to find the probability that all 3 are heads, which is HHH. There is only 1 favorable outcome out of 4 possible outcomes. So, the probability is $1/4$.\n\nAlternatively, using conditional probability:\n$P(\\text{all 3 are heads} | \\text{first is heads}) = P(\\text{HHH}) / P(\\text{first is heads})$\n$P(\\text{first is heads}) = P(\\text{HHH}) + P(\\text{HHT}) + P(\\text{HTH}) + P(\\text{HTT}) = 1/8 + 1/8 + 1/8 + 1/8 = 4/8 = 1/2$.\n$P(\\text{all 3 are heads}) = P(\\text{HHH}) = 1/8$.\nSo, $P(\\text{all 3 are heads} | \\text{first is heads}) = (1/8) / (1/2) = 1/4$.\n\n(b) If it is known that the first 2 tosses are heads, the reduced sample space is {HHH, HHT}. We want to find the probability that all 3 are heads, which is HHH. There is only 1 favorable outcome out of 2 possible outcomes. So, the probability is $1/2$.\n\nAlternatively, using conditional probability:\n$P(\\text{all 3 are heads} | \\text{first 2 are heads}) = P(\\text{HHH}) / P(\\text{first 2 are heads})$\n$P(\\text{first 2 are heads}) = P(\\text{HHH}) + P(\\text{HHT}) = 1/8 + 1/8 = 2/8 = 1/4$.\n$P(\\text{all 3 are heads}) = P(\\text{HHH}) = 1/8$.\nSo, $P(\\text{all 3 are heads} | \\text{first 2 are heads}) = (1/8) / (1/4) = 1/2$.\n\n(c) If it is known that 2 of them are heads, the reduced sample space is {HHH, HHT, HTH, THH}. We want to find the probability that all 3 are heads, which is HHH. There is only 1 favorable outcome out of 4 possible outcomes. So, the probability is $1/4$.\n\nAlternatively, using conditional probability:\n$P(\\text{all 3 are heads} | \\text{2 are heads}) = P(\\text{all 3 are heads and 2 are heads}) / P(\\text{2 are heads})$\nThe event \"all 3 are heads\" implies \"2 are heads\", so $P(\\text{all 3 are heads and 2 are heads}) = P(\\text{all 3 are heads}) = P(\\text{HHH}) = 1/8$.\n$P(\\text{2 are heads})$ is the probability of getting exactly 2 heads in 3 tosses. The outcomes with exactly 2 heads are HHT, HTH, THH. So, $P(\\text{2 are heads}) = P(\\text{HHT}) + P(\\text{HTH}) + P(\\text{THH}) = 1/8 + 1/8 + 1/8 = 3/8$.\nSo, $P(\\text{all 3 are heads} | \\text{2 are heads}) = (1/8) / (3/8) = 1/3$.\n\nLet's recheck part (c). The phrasing \"if it is known that 2 of them are heads\" refers to the event that exactly 2 of the 3 tosses are heads. The outcomes with exactly 2 heads are {HHT, HTH, THH}. The total number of outcomes with exactly 2 heads is 3.\nThe event \"all 3 are heads\" is {HHH}.\nThe question asks for the probability of \"all 3 are heads\" given \"2 of them are heads\".\nThis means we are looking for the probability of HHH given that the outcome is one of {HHT, HTH, THH}.\nSince HHH is not in the conditioning event {HHT, HTH, THH}, the probability of HHH given that it is one of {HHT, HTH, THH} is 0.\n\nLet's re-read the question carefully. \"Find the probability that all 3 are heads, (c) if it is known that 2 of them are heads.\"\nThis means we are conditioning on the event that exactly 2 heads have occurred.\nThe event E = \"exactly 2 heads\" = {HHT, HTH, THH}. The probability of E is $P(E) = 3/8$.\nThe event F = \"all 3 are heads\" = {HHH}. The probability of F is $P(F) = 1/8$.\nWe are asked to find $P(F | E)$.\n$P(F | E) = P(F \\cap E) / P(E)$.\nThe intersection of F and E is $F \\cap E = \\{\\text{HHH}\\} \\cap \\{\\text{HHT, HTH, THH}\\} = \\emptyset$.\nSo, $P(F \\cap E) = 0$.\nTherefore, $P(F | E) = 0 / (3/8) = 0$.\n\nLet's consider the possibility that \"2 of them are heads\" could also include the case of 3 heads. If \"2 of them are heads\" means \"at least 2 heads\", then the conditioning event is {HHH, HHT, HTH, THH}. In this case, the probability of \"all 3 are heads\" (HHH) given \"at least 2 heads\" would be $P(\\text{HHH}) / P(\\text{at least 2 heads}) = (1/8) / (4/8) = 1/4$. The options do not reflect this interpretation.\n\nLet's stick to the interpretation that \"2 of them are heads\" means \"exactly 2 heads\".\n\nRevisiting the options with our calculations:\n(a) 1/4\n(b) 1/2\n(c) 0\n\nNone of the options match exactly. Let's recheck our work.\n\nLet's review the options and our calculated values once more.\n(a) 1/4\n(b) 1/2\n(c) 0\n\nLet's assume there might be a typo in the question or the options.\nIf the question in (a) was \"Find the probability that the first 2 are heads, if it is known that the first is heads\", then the answer would be 1/2.\n\nLet's re-examine the standard interpretation of these problems.\nThe sample space is $\\Omega = \\{HHH, HHT, HTH, THH, HTT, THT, TTH, TTT\\}$, with $| \\Omega | = 8$.\n\n(a) Event A = \"first is heads\". $A = \\{HHH, HHT, HTH, HTT\\}$, so $P(A) = 4/8 = 1/2$.\nEvent B = \"all 3 are heads\" = {HHH}, so $P(B) = 1/8$.\nWe want $P(B|A) = P(B \\cap A) / P(A)$.\n$B \\cap A = \\{HHH\\}$, so $P(B \\cap A) = 1/8$.\n$P(B|A) = (1/8) / (1/2) = 1/4$.\n\n(b) Event C = \"first 2 are heads\". $C = \\{HHH, HHT\\}$, so $P(C) = 2/8 = 1/4$.\nEvent B = \"all 3 are heads\" = {HHH}, so $P(B) = 1/8$.\nWe want $P(B|C) = P(B \\cap C) / P(C)$.\n$B \\cap C = \\{HHH\\}$, so $P(B \\cap C) = 1/8$.\n$P(B|C) = (1/8) / (1/4) = 1/2$.\n\n(c) Event D = \"2 of them are heads\" (exactly 2 heads). $D = \\{HHT, HTH, THH\\}$, so $P(D) = 3/8$.\nEvent B = \"all 3 are heads\" = {HHH}, so $P(B) = 1/8$.\nWe want $P(B|D) = P(B \\cap D) / P(D)$.\n$B \\cap D = \\{HHH\\} \\cap \\{HHT, HTH, THH\\} = \\emptyset$. So $P(B \\cap D) = 0$.\n$P(B|D) = 0 / (3/8) = 0$.\n\nOur calculations are: (a) 1/4, (b) 1/2, (c) 0.\nLet's check the options again.\n\nOption D: (a) 1/4, (b) 1/2, (c) 1/4. This matches our (a) and (b) but not (c).\nIf (c) was intended to be \"at least 2 of them are heads\", then the answer would be 1/4. However, the wording is \"2 of them are heads\", which typically means exactly 2.\n\nLet's re-examine the possibility of \"2 of them are heads\" meaning \"at least 2 heads\".\nEvent \"at least 2 heads\" = {HHH, HHT, HTH, THH}. Probability is 4/8 = 1/2.\nProbability of \"all 3 are heads\" (HHH) given \"at least 2 heads\" = $P(\\text{HHH}) / P(\\text{at least 2 heads}) = (1/8) / (1/2) = 1/4$.\nIf this interpretation is used for (c), then our answers are: (a) 1/4, (b) 1/2, (c) 1/4.\nThis matches option D.\n\nGiven the options provided, it is highly probable that \"2 of them are heads\" in part (c) was intended to mean \"at least 2 of them are heads\".\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
