    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given expression is the determinant of a 3x3 matrix. The matrix is:\n$$ \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} $$\n\nTo evaluate the determinant, we can use the cofactor expansion method. Let's expand along the first row:\n$$ \\Delta = 1 \\begin{vmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\end{vmatrix} - (-4) \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{vmatrix} + (-5) \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} $$\n\nCalculate the determinants of the 2x2 matrices:\n$$ \\begin{vmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\end{vmatrix} = (2)(-2) - (3)(1) = -4 - 3 = -7 $$\n$$ \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{vmatrix} = (1)(-2) - (3)(-3) = -2 - (-9) = -2 + 9 = 7 $$\n$$ \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix} = (1)(1) - (2)(-3) = 1 - (-6) = 1 + 6 = 7 $$\n\nNow substitute these values back into the determinant formula:\n$$ \\Delta = 1(-7) - (-4)(7) + (-5)(7) $$\n$$ \\Delta = -7 + 28 - 35 $$\n$$ \\Delta = 21 - 35 $$\n$$ \\Delta = -14 $$\n\nAlternatively, we can use Sarrus' rule for a 3x3 matrix. Rewrite the first two columns to the right of the matrix:\n$$ \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{matrix} 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\end{matrix} $$\nSum the products of the diagonals going from top-left to bottom-right, and subtract the sum of the products of the diagonals going from top-right to bottom-left.\n$$ \\Delta = [(1)(2)(-2) + (-4)(3)(-3) + (-5)(1)(1)] - [(-5)(2)(-3) + (1)(3)(1) + (-4)(1)(-2)] $$\n$$ \\Delta = [-4 + 36 - 5] - [30 + 3 + 8] $$\n$$ \\Delta = [27] - [41] $$\n$$ \\Delta = 27 - 41 $$\n$$ \\Delta = -14 $$\n\nThe determinant of the matrix is -14.\n\nComparing this result with the given options:\nA. -28\nB. 28\nC. -7\nD. 0\nE. 7\nF. 10\nG. -14\nH. 14\nI. 21\nJ. -21\n\nThe correct option is G.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
