    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10262",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the inverse Laplace transform L^-1[1 / {{s(s^2 + 1)}}], using the convolution.\n\nOptions: \nA. sin t - t\nB. 1 - cos t\nC. t sin t\nD. e^t - 1\nE. 1 - e^-t\nF. sin t\nG. e^t - cos t\nH. t - sin t\nI. 1 + cos t\nJ. - cos t\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the inverse Laplace transform of $1 / (s(s^2 + 1))$ using convolution, we can split the expression into two functions whose inverse Laplace transforms are known. Let $f(s) = 1/s$ and $g(s) = 1/(s^2 + 1)$. Their respective inverse Laplace transforms are $f(t) = 1$ and $g(t) = \\sin t$. The convolution of $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ is given by $(f * g)(t) = \\int_0^t f(\\tau) g(t - \\tau) d\\tau$.\n\nSo, we have $(1 * \\sin t)(t) = \\int_0^t 1 \\cdot \\sin(t - \\tau) d\\tau$.\nLet $u = t - \\tau$, then $du = -d\\tau$. When $\\tau = 0$, $u = t$. When $\\tau = t$, $u = 0$.\nThe integral becomes $\\int_t^0 \\sin(u) (-du) = \\int_0^t \\sin(u) du = [-\\cos(u)]_0^t = -\\cos(t) - (-\\cos(0)) = -\\cos(t) + 1 = 1 - \\cos(t)$.\n\nComparing this result with the given options:\nA. sin t - t\nB. 1 - cos t\nC. t sin t\nD. e^t - 1\nE. 1 - e^-t\nF. sin t\nG. e^t - cos t\nH. t - sin t\nI. 1 + cos t\nJ. - cos t\n\nThe result $1 - \\cos t$ matches option B.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
      "success": true,
