      "generated_answer": "The problem asks to find the inverse Laplace transform of the given function F(s). We will use partial fraction expansion to decompose F(s) and then find the inverse Laplace transform of each term.\n\nF(s) = [(s^2 + 3s + 1) / {{(s + 1)^3 (s + 2)^2}}]\n\nLet F(s) = A/(s+1) + B/(s+1)^2 + C/(s+1)^3 + D/(s+2) + E/(s+2)^2\n\nMultiply both sides by (s+1)^3 (s+2)^2:\ns^2 + 3s + 1 = A(s+1)^2 (s+2)^2 + B(s+1)(s+2)^2 + C(s+2)^2 + D(s+1)^3 (s+2) + E(s+1)^3\n\nLet s = -1:\n(-1)^2 + 3(-1) + 1 = C(-1+2)^2\n1 - 3 + 1 = C(1)^2\n-1 = C\n\nLet s = -2:\n(-2)^2 + 3(-2) + 1 = E(-2+1)^3\n4 - 6 + 1 = E(-1)^3\n-1 = -E\nE = 1\n\nNow we have:\ns^2 + 3s + 1 = A(s^2+2s+1)(s^2+4s+4) + B(s+1)(s^2+4s+4) + C(s^2+4s+4) + D(s^3+3s^2+3s+1)(s+2) + E(s^3+3s^2+3s+1)\n\nSubstitute C = -1 and E = 1:\ns^2 + 3s + 1 = A(s^4+6s^3+13s^2+12s+4) + B(s^3+5s^2+8s+4) - (s^2+4s+4) + D(s^4+5s^3+9s^2+7s+2) + (s^3+3s^2+3s+1)\n\nEquating coefficients of s^4:\n0 = A + D  => D = -A\n\nEquating coefficients of s^3:\n0 = 6A + B + 5D + 1\n0 = 6A + B + 5(-A) + 1\n0 = A + B + 1 => B = -A - 1\n\nEquating coefficients of s^2:\n1 = 13A + 5B - 1 + 9D + 3\n1 = 13A + 5(-A-1) - 1 + 9(-A) + 3\n1 = 13A - 5A - 5 - 1 - 9A + 3\n1 = (13-5-9)A - 3\n1 = -A - 3\nA = -4\n\nNow find B and D:\nB = -A - 1 = -(-4) - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3\nD = -A = -(-4) = 4\n\nSo, we have:\nA = -4, B = 3, C = -1, D = 4, E = 1\n\nF(s) = -4/(s+1) + 3/(s+1)^2 - 1/(s+1)^3 + 4/(s+2) + 1/(s+2)^2\n\nNow, we find the inverse Laplace transform of each term:\nL^-1{-4/(s+1)} = -4e^-t\nL^-1{3/(s+1)^2} = 3te^-t\nL^-1{-1/(s+1)^3} = - (1/2)t^2e^-t\nL^-1{4/(s+2)} = 4e^-2t\nL^-1{1/(s+2)^2} = te^-2t\n\nTherefore, F(t) = - (1/2) t^2e^-t + 3te^-t - 4e^-t + te^-2t + 4e^-2t\n\nComparing with the given options, we find that option D matches our result.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$",
