    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10051",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Givenf(t) = ^+\\infty\\int_-\\infty h(\\sigma)\\textphi(t - \\sigma)d\\sigma determine the power spectral density of f(t) in terms of the power spectral density of \\textphi(t) and the Fourier transform of h(t).\n\nOptions: \nA. G_f(\\omega) = \u000bertH(\\omega)\u000bert^2 G_\textphi(\\omega)\nB. \\( G_f(\\omega) = \\frac{{G_{{\\textphi}}(\\omega)}}{{|H(\\omega)|^2}} \\)\nC. \\( G_f(\\omega) = H(\\omega) G_{{\\textphi}}(\\omega)^2 \\)\nD. \\( G_f(\\omega) = |H(\\omega)|^2 / G_{{\\textphi}}(\\omega) \\)\nE. \\( G_f(\\omega) = \\frac{{1}}{{|H(\\omega)|^2}} G_{{\\textphi}}(\\omega) \\)\nF. \\( G_f(\\omega) = |H(\\omega)| G_{{\\textphi}}(\\omega)^2 \\)\nG. G_f(\\omega) = \u000bertH(\\omega)\u000bert^2 / G_\textphi(\\omega)\nH. G_f(\\omega) = H(\\omega)^2 G_\textphi(\\omega)\nI. \\( G_f(\\omega) = \\frac{{G_{{\\textphi}}(\\omega)}}{{H(\\omega)}} \\)\nJ. G_f(\\omega) = \u000bertH(\\omega)\u000bert G_\textphi(t)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given equation represents a convolution of $h(t)$ and $\\phi(t)$. The Fourier transform of a convolution is the product of the Fourier transforms of the individual functions. Therefore, $F\\{f(t)\\} = H(\\omega) \\Phi(\\omega)$, where $F\\{\\cdot\\}$ denotes the Fourier transform, $H(\\omega)$ is the Fourier transform of $h(t)$, and $\\Phi(\\omega)$ is the Fourier transform of $\\phi(t)$. The power spectral density of a signal is the magnitude squared of its Fourier transform. Thus, $G_f(\\omega) = |F\\{f(t)\\}|^2 = |H(\\omega) \\Phi(\\omega)|^2 = |H(\\omega)|^2 |\\Phi(\\omega)|^2 = |H(\\omega)|^2 G_\\phi(\\omega)$.\n\nThe most likely correct answer is A, assuming G_phi(omega) represents the power spectral density of phi(t).\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
