    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10269",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Two infinite parallel metal plates are charged with equal surface charge density of the same polarity. The electric field in the gap between the plates is\n\nOptions: \nA. inversely proportional to the distance between the plates.\nB. quadruple the field produced by one plate.\nC. zero.\nD. dependent on the thickness of the plates.\nE. dependent on coordinates of field points.\nF. proportional to the square of the distance between the plates.\nG. double the field produced by one plate.\nH. half the field produced by one plate.\nI. triple the field produced by one plate.\nJ. same as that produced by one plate.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The electric field due to an infinite plane of charge with surface charge density $\\sigma$ is given by $E = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\epsilon_0}$, and it is uniform and perpendicular to the plane. When we have two infinite parallel plates with equal surface charge densities of the same polarity, the electric fields from each plate add up in the region between the plates. Since the fields from both plates are in the same direction and have the same magnitude, the total electric field in the gap will be the sum of these two fields. If the field from one plate is $E_1$, then the field from the second plate is also $E_1$. Therefore, the total field in the gap is $E_{total} = E_1 + E_1 = 2E_1$. This means the electric field in the gap is double the field produced by one plate.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 196,
        "output_tokens": 191,
        "total_tokens": 387
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
