    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10622",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Evaluatedivuat the point (- 1, 1, 2) foru= x^2i+e^xyj+ xyzk(1)\n\nOptions: \nA. - 3 - e^1\nB. - 2 - e^-1\nC. 2 + e^-1\nD. - 3 - e^-1\nE. -3 + e^1\nF. -4 + e^-1\nG. -1 + e^1\nH. -1 - e^-1\nI. - 3 + e^-1\nJ. -2 + e^1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem asks us to evaluate the divergence of the vector field $u = x^2 i + e^{xy} j + xyz k$ at the point $(-1, 1, 2)$. The divergence of a vector field $u = u_1 i + u_2 j + u_3 k$ is given by $\\nabla \\cdot u = \\frac{\\partial u_1}{\\partial x} + \\frac{\\partial u_2}{\\partial y} + \\frac{\\partial u_3}{\\partial z}$.\n\nIn this case, $u_1 = x^2$, $u_2 = e^{xy}$, and $u_3 = xyz$. We need to find the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{\\partial u_1}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2) = 2x$\n$\\frac{\\partial u_2}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(e^{xy}) = xe^{xy}$\n$\\frac{\\partial u_3}{\\partial z} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}(xyz) = xy$\n\nSo, the divergence is $\\nabla \\cdot u = 2x + xe^{xy} + xy$.\n\nNow we need to evaluate this at the point $(-1, 1, 2)$. Substitute $x = -1$, $y = 1$, and $z = 2$ into the expression for the divergence:\n$\\nabla \\cdot u \\Big|_{(-1, 1, 2)} = 2(-1) + (-1)e^{(-1)(1)} + (-1)(1)$\n$= -2 - e^{-1} - 1$\n$= -3 - e^{-1}$\n\nComparing this result with the given options, we find that option D matches our calculated value.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 190,
        "output_tokens": 395,
        "total_tokens": 585
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
