    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10377",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the steady-state solution of the equation y [n] + [1 / \\surd(3)]y [n - 1] =cos(\\pi/6) n.\n\nOptions: \nA. y_ss[n] = \\surd(3/7)cos[(\\pi/6)n tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{2\\surd(3)}}]\nB. y_ss[n] = \\surd(2/7)sin[(\\pi/6)n + tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{3\\surd(3)}}]\nC. y_ss[n] = \\surd(2/7)cos[(\\pi/6)n tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{3\\surd(3)}}]\nD. y_ss[n] = \\surd(3/7)cos[(\\pi/3)n tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{3\\surd(3)}}]\nE. y_ss[n] = \\surd(3/7)sin[(\\pi/6)n tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{3\\surd(3)}}]\nF. y_ss[n] = \\surd(3/8)sin[(\\pi/6)n - tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{3\\surd(3)}}]\nG. y_ss[n] = \\surd(3/7)cos[(\\pi/6)n tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{3\\surd(3)}}]\nH. y_ss[n] = \\surd(3/7)sin[(\\pi/6)n - tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{2\\surd(3)}}]\nI. y_ss[n] = \\surd(3/7)cos[(\\pi/6)n + tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{3\\surd(3)}}]\nJ. y_ss[n] = \\surd(1/7)cos[(\\pi/6)n tan^-1 [{{1}} / {{3\\surd(3)}}]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given equation is a linear constant-coefficient difference equation:\n$y[n] + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}y[n-1] = \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{6}n)$.\n\nWe are looking for the steady-state solution, which will have the same frequency as the input.\nLet the input be $x[n] = \\cos(\\omega_0 n)$ where $\\omega_0 = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.\nWe can write $x[n]$ as the real part of a complex exponential: $x[n] = \\text{Re}(e^{j\\omega_0 n})$.\n\nAssume the steady-state solution is of the form $y_{ss}[n] = A \\cos(\\omega_0 n + \\phi)$.\nAlternatively, we can use the frequency response method.\nThe Z-transform of the equation is:\n$Y(z) + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}z^{-1}Y(z) = X(z)$\n$Y(z)(1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}z^{-1}) = X(z)$\nThe system function is $H(z) = \\frac{Y(z)}{X(z)} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}z^{-1}}$.\n\nFor an input $x[n] = \\cos(\\omega_0 n)$, the steady-state output is given by:\n$y_{ss}[n] = |H(e^{j\\omega_0})| \\cos(\\omega_0 n + \\angle H(e^{j\\omega_0}))$.\nHere, $\\omega_0 = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.\n$H(e^{j\\omega_0}) = H(e^{j\\pi/6}) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}e^{-j\\pi/6}}$.\n\nLet's calculate $e^{-j\\pi/6}$:\n$e^{-j\\pi/6} = \\cos(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}) + j\\sin(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}) = \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{6}) - j\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6}) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - j\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nSubstitute this into $H(e^{j\\pi/6})$:\n$H(e^{j\\pi/6}) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - j\\frac{1}{2})} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\frac{1}{2} - j\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2} - j\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}}$.\n\nNow, we need to find the magnitude and phase of $H(e^{j\\pi/6})$.\n$|H(e^{j\\pi/6})| = \\frac{1}{|\\frac{3}{2} - j\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}|} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{2})^2 + (-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}})^2}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4} + \\frac{1}{12}}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{27+1}{12}}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{12}}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}$.\n\n$\\angle H(e^{j\\pi/6}) = -\\angle (\\frac{3}{2} - j\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}) = -\\arctan\\left(\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}}{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right) = -\\arctan\\left(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\right) = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$.\n\nSo, the steady-state solution is:\n$y_{ss}[n] = \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}} \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}n + \\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\right)$.\n\nComparing this with the given options, option I matches our result.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 535,
        "output_tokens": 978,
        "total_tokens": 1513
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
