    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10157",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use partied fraction expansion to find the inverse z-transform of F (z) = [(30z^2 - 12 z) / (6z^2 - 5z + 1)].\n\nOptions: \nA. f(n) = [3 (1/2)^n + 1 (1/3)^n + 1] u[n]\nB. f(n) = [4 (1/2)^n - 1 (1/3)^n] u[n]\nC. f(n) = [3 (1/2)^n - 2 (1/3)^n] u[n-1]\nD. f (n) = [3 (1/2)^n + 2 (1/3)^n] n[n]\nE. f (n) = [2 (1/2)^n + 3 (1/3)^n] u[n]\nF. f(n) = [5 (1/2)^n + 3 (1/3)^n] u[n]\nG. f(n) = [3 (1/2)^n + 2 (1/3)^n] u[n-1]\nH. f (n) = [3 (1/2)^n + 2 (1/3)^n] u[n]\nI. f (n) = [3 (1/2)^n - 2 (1/3)^n] u[n]\nJ. f(n) = [2 (1/2)^n + 3 (1/3)^n] n[n-1]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the inverse z-transform of $F(z) = \\frac{30z^2 - 12z}{6z^2 - 5z + 1}$, we first perform partial fraction expansion. It's usually easier to work with $F(z)/z$.\nFirst, we find the roots of the denominator: $6z^2 - 5z + 1 = 0$, which factors into $(3z - 1)(2z - 1) = 0$. So the roots are $z = 1/3$ and $z = 1/2$.\n\nNow we compute $F(z)/z$:\n$\\frac{F(z)}{z} = \\frac{30z^2 - 12z}{z(6z^2 - 5z + 1)} = \\frac{30z - 12}{(3z - 1)(2z - 1)}$\n\nWe can set up the partial fraction expansion as:\n$\\frac{30z - 12}{(3z - 1)(2z - 1)} = \\frac{A}{3z - 1} + \\frac{B}{2z - 1}$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(3z - 1)(2z - 1)$:\n$30z - 12 = A(2z - 1) + B(3z - 1)$\n\nTo find A, let $z = 1/3$:\n$30(1/3) - 12 = A(2(1/3) - 1) + B(0)$\n$10 - 12 = A(2/3 - 1)$\n$-2 = A(-1/3)$\n$A = 6$\n\nTo find B, let $z = 1/2$:\n$30(1/2) - 12 = A(0) + B(3(1/2) - 1)$\n$15 - 12 = B(3/2 - 1)$\n$3 = B(1/2)$\n$B = 6$\n\nSo, $\\frac{F(z)}{z} = \\frac{6}{3z - 1} + \\frac{6}{2z - 1}$.\nWe can rewrite this as:\n$\\frac{F(z)}{z} = \\frac{6}{3(z - 1/3)} + \\frac{6}{2(z - 1/2)} = \\frac{2}{z - 1/3} + \\frac{3}{z - 1/2}$\n\nNow, multiply by z to get $F(z)$:\n$F(z) = \\frac{2z}{z - 1/3} + \\frac{3z}{z - 1/2}$\n\nFor the inverse z-transform, we use the property that $Z^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{z}{z-a}\\right\\} = a^n u[n]$.\nSo, the inverse z-transform of $F(z)$ is:\n$f[n] = 2 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^n u[n] + 3 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n u[n]$\n$f[n] = 3 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n u[n] + 2 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^n u[n]$\n\nLet's recheck the calculation for A and B.\n$30z - 12 = A(2z - 1) + B(3z - 1)$\nIf $z = 1/3$: $30(1/3) - 12 = 10 - 12 = -2$. $A(2/3 - 1) = A(-1/3)$. So $-2 = A(-1/3)$, which gives $A = 6$. This is correct.\nIf $z = 1/2$: $30(1/2) - 12 = 15 - 12 = 3$. $B(3/2 - 1) = B(1/2)$. So $3 = B(1/2)$, which gives $B = 6$. This is correct.\n\nSo, $\\frac{F(z)}{z} = \\frac{6}{3z - 1} + \\frac{6}{2z - 1} = \\frac{6/3}{z - 1/3} + \\frac{6/2}{z - 1/2} = \\frac{2}{z - 1/3} + \\frac{3}{z - 1/2}$.\nThen, $F(z) = \\frac{2z}{z - 1/3} + \\frac{3z}{z - 1/2}$.\nThe inverse z-transform is $f[n] = 2 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^n u[n] + 3 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n u[n]$.\n\nLet's review the options. The terms involve $(1/2)^n$ and $(1/3)^n$. This matches our results.\nOption H: $f (n) = [3 (1/2)^n + 2 (1/3)^n] u[n]$. This matches our calculated inverse z-transform.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 415,
        "output_tokens": 1215,
        "total_tokens": 1630
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
