    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The voltage across an inductor is given by $v(t) = \\frac{d}{dt} [L(t)i(t)]$.\nGiven $L(t) = L_0(t + \\tanh t)$ and $i(t) = \\cos(\\omega t)$.\nWe need to calculate the derivative of the product $L(t)i(t)$:\n$\\frac{d}{dt} [L(t)i(t)] = L'(t)i(t) + L(t)i'(t)$\n\nFirst, find $L'(t)$:\n$L'(t) = \\frac{d}{dt} [L_0(t + \\tanh t)] = L_0 (1 + \\text{sech}^2 t)$\n\nNext, find $i'(t)$:\n$i'(t) = \\frac{d}{dt} [\\cos(\\omega t)] = -\\omega \\sin(\\omega t)$\n\nNow, substitute these into the voltage equation:\n$v(t) = L'(t)i(t) + L(t)i'(t)$\n$v(t) = L_0 (1 + \\text{sech}^2 t) \\cos(\\omega t) + L_0 (t + \\tanh t) (-\\omega \\sin(\\omega t))$\n$v(t) = L_0 (1 + \\text{sech}^2 t) \\cos(\\omega t) - \\omega L_0 (t + \\tanh t) \\sin(\\omega t)$\n\nComparing this result with the given options:\nOption B: $U(t) = L_0 (1 + sech^2t)cos\\omegat+ \\omegaL_0 (t +tanht)sin\\omegat$ (Incorrect sign for the second term)\nOption C: $U(t) = L_0 (1 + sech^2t)cos\\omegat- \\omegaL_0 (t +tanht)sin\\omegat$ (Correct)\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
