      "generated_answer": "Let the state variables be defined as follows:\n$q_1 = v$\n$q_2 = \\dot{v}$\n$q_3 = \\ddot{v}$\n\nHowever, the given equation involves an integral of $v$. So we need to consider the integral term as a state variable.\nLet:\n$q_1 = \\int_0^t v(t') dt'$\n$q_2 = v(t)$\n$q_3 = \\dot{v}(t)$\n\nThen, the derivatives of these state variables are:\n$\\dot{q}_1 = \\frac{d}{dt} \\left( \\int_0^t v(t') dt' \\right) = v(t) = q_2$\n$\\dot{q}_2 = \\frac{d}{dt} v(t) = \\dot{v}(t) = q_3$\n$\\dot{q}_3 = \\frac{d}{dt} \\dot{v}(t) = \\ddot{v}(t)$\n\nNow, let's rewrite the given integrodifferential equation in terms of the state variables:\n$\\ddot{v} + a\\dot{v} + bv + C \\int_0^t v(t') dt' = \\sin t$\n\nSubstituting the state variables:\n$\\dot{q}_3 + aq_3 + bq_2 + Cq_1 = \\sin t$\n\nRearranging to express $\\dot{q}_3$:\n$\\dot{q}_3 = -Cq_1 - bq_2 - aq_3 + \\sin t$\n\nNow, let's define the state vector $q = \\begin{bmatrix} q_1 \\\\ q_2 \\\\ q_3 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nThe state equations are:\n$\\dot{q}_1 = q_2$\n$\\dot{q}_2 = q_3$\n$\\dot{q}_3 = -Cq_1 - bq_2 - aq_3 + \\sin t$\n\nThis is a standard way to convert an integrodifferential equation into state-space form. We need to look for an option that matches the form of $\\dot{q}_3$.\n\nLet's examine the options provided. The options talk about $q_1, q_2, q_3$ and their derivatives. We need to match the form of $\\dot{q}_3$ with the given options.\n\nLet's re-evaluate the common convention for defining state variables for integrodifferential equations that include an integral term.\nTypically, for an equation of the form:\n$\\frac{d^n y}{dt^n} + a_{n-1} \\frac{d^{n-1} y}{dt^{n-1}} + \\dots + a_0 y + C \\int_0^t y(t') dt' = f(t)$\n\nThe state variables are often defined as:\n$q_1 = \\int_0^t y(t') dt'$\n$q_2 = y(t)$\n$q_3 = \\dot{y}(t)$\n...\n$q_n = y^{(n-1)}(t)$\n\nIn our case, the highest derivative is the second derivative ($\\ddot{v}$), so $n=2$. The equation is:\n$\\ddot{v} + a\\dot{v} + bv + C \\int_0^t v(