    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10782",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the general solution of the differential equation [(d^2x) / (dt^2)] - 2 (dx/dt) - 3x= 10asin t +b(2t + 1)(1) whereaandbare constant vectors.\n\nOptions: \nA. x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^2t +a(cost - 2 sin t) +b[- (2/3) t + (1/9)]\nB. x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^3t -a(cost - 2 sin t) -b[(2/3) t + (1/9)]\nC. x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^t +a(3 cost + sin t) -b[(1/2) t + (2/9)]\nD. x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^3t +a(sin t - 2 cost) +b[(2/3) t + (1/9)]\nE. x=Y+A=c_1e^t +c_2e^3t +a(cost - 2 sin t) +b[(1/3) t + (1/9)]\nF. x=Y+A=c_3e^-t +c_4e^3t +a(2 sin t - cost) +b[(2/5) t + (1/7)]\nG. x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^3t +a(cost + 2 sin t) +b[(2/3) t - (1/9)]\nH. x=Y+A=c_1e^-2t +c_2e^3t +a(sin t + cost) +b[(3/2) t - (1/9)]\nI. x=Y+A=c_1e^t +c_2e^-3t +a(sin t + 2 cost) -b[(2/3) t + (1/9)]\nJ. x=Y+A=c_1e^2t +c_2e^-t -a(2 cost + sin t) +b[(2/4) t + (1/8)]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the general solution of the given differential equation, we first find the complementary function (Y) by solving the homogeneous equation:\n(d^2x)/(dt^2) - 2(dx/dt) - 3x = 0\nThe characteristic equation is r^2 - 2r - 3 = 0, which factors as (r-3)(r+1) = 0. The roots are r1 = 3 and r2 = -1.\nThus, the complementary function is Y = c1e^(-t) + c2e^(3t), where c1 and c2 are arbitrary constants.\n\nNext, we find a particular integral (A) for the non-homogeneous equation:\n(d^2x)/(dt^2) - 2(dx/dt) - 3x = 10asint + b(2t + 1)\nWe split the non-homogeneous term into two parts: 10asint and b(2t + 1).\n\nFor the term 10asint, we assume a particular solution of the form x_p1 = A1cost + B1sint.\nDifferentiating twice:\ndx_p1/dt = -A1sint + B1cost\nd^2x_p1/dt^2 = -A1cost - B1sint\nSubstituting into the differential equation:\n(-A1cost - B1sint) - 2(-A1sint + B1cost) - 3(A1cost + B1sint) = 10asint\n(-A1 - 2B1 - 3A1)cost + (-B1 + 2A1 - 3B1)sint = 10asint\n(-4A1 - 2B1)cost + (2A1 - 4B1)sint = 10asint\nComparing coefficients:\n-4A1 - 2B1 = 0  => 2A1 + B1 = 0 => B1 = -2A1\n2A1 - 4B1 = 10a\nSubstitute B1 = -2A1 into the second equation:\n2A1 - 4(-2A1) = 10a\n2A1 + 8A1 = 10a\n10A1 = 10a => A1 = a\nThen B1 = -2a.\nSo, x_p1 = acost - 2asint.\n\nFor the term b(2t + 1), we assume a particular solution of the form x_p2 = Ct + D.\nDifferentiating twice:\ndx_p2/dt = C\nd^2x_p2/dt^2 = 0\nSubstituting into the differential equation:\n0 - 2(C) - 3(Ct + D) = b(2t + 1)\n-2C - 3Ct - 3D = 2bt + b\n-3Ct + (-2C - 3D) = 2bt + b\nComparing coefficients:\n-3C = 2b => C = -2b/3\n-2C - 3D = b\nSubstitute C = -2b/3:\n-2(-2b/3) - 3D = b\n4b/3 - 3D = b\n3D = 4b/3 - b = b/3\nD = b/9\nSo, x_p2 = -(2b/3)t + b/9.\n\nThe particular integral is A = x_p1 + x_p2 = acost - 2asint - (2b/3)t + b/9.\nThe general solution is x = Y + A = c1e^(-t) + c2e^(3t) + acost - 2asint - (2b/3)t + b/9.\n\nNow let's examine the given options. We are looking for a solution of the form c1e^(-t) + c2e^(3t) plus terms involving 'a' and 'b'.\n\nOption A: x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^2t +a(cost - 2 sin t) +b[- (2/3) t + (1/9)]\nThe exponential terms c2e^2t and complementary function are incorrect. The particular integral part matches.\n\nOption B: x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^3t -a(cost - 2 sin t) -b[(2/3) t + (1/9)]\nThe complementary function is correct (c1e^-t + c2e^3t). The particular integral part is incorrect in sign for 'a' and 'b'.\n\nOption C: x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^t +a(3 cost + sin t) -b[(1/2) t + (2/9)]\nThe exponential term c2e^t is incorrect.\n\nOption D: x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^3t +a(sin t - 2 cost) +b[(2/3) t + (1/9)]\nThe complementary function is correct. The particular integral part for 'a' is a(sint - 2cost), which is the negative of what we found (a(cost - 2sint)). The particular integral part for 'b' is also incorrect.\n\nOption E: x=Y+A=c_1e^t +c_2e^3t +a(cost - 2 sin t) +b[(1/3) t + (1/9)]\nThe exponential term c1e^t is incorrect.\n\nOption F: x=Y+A=c_3e^-t +c_4e^3t +a(2 sin t - cost) +b[(2/5) t + (1/7)]\nThe complementary function has different constants, but the form is correct. The particular integral part for 'a' is a(2sint - cost), which is-(acost - 2asint). The particular integral part for 'b' is incorrect.\n\nOption G: x=Y+A=c_1e^-t +c_2e^3t +a(cost + 2 sin t) +b[(2/3) t - (1/9)]\nThe complementary function is correct. The particular integral part for 'a' is incorrect.\n\nOption H: x=Y+A=c_1e^-2t +c_2e^3t +a(sin t + cost) +b[(3/2) t - (1/9)]\nThe exponential term c1e^-2t is incorrect.\n\nOption I: x=Y+A=c_1e^t +c_2e^-3t +a(sin t + 2 cost) -b[(2/3) t + (1/9)]\nThe exponential terms are incorrect.\n\nOption J: x=Y+A=c_1e^2t +c_2e^-t -a(2 cost + sin t) +b[(2/4) t + (1/8)]\nThe exponential terms are incorrect.\n\nLet's re-examine our particular integral for the sine term. We assumed x_p1 = A1cost + B1sint, leading to:\n(-4A1 - 2B1)cost + (2A1 - 4B1)sint = 10asint\nWe obtained A1 = a and B1 = -2a. So, x_p1 = acost - 2asint.\n\nLet's re-examine our particular integral 