    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The electrostatic energy of a charged conducting shell can be calculated by considering the energy stored in the electric field. For a point charge q at the center and a conducting shell, charges will be induced on the inner and outer surfaces of the shell. We can calculate the energy by integrating the energy density of the electric field over the volume. For a conducting shell, the electric field exists between the inner and outer radii. The energy is therefore the integral of (1/2)ε₀E² over the volume between r₁ and r₂. This leads to the formula derived from the potential energy of the induced charges.\n\nThe correct answer is **D**.",
