    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10642",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The total normalized power of a periodic signal is given by S = ^n=+\\infty\\sum_n=-\\inftyV_nV_n\\textasteriskcentered where V_n= [1 / (\\vertn\\vert)]e-jarctan(n/2)forn = \\pm 1, \\pm 2,... Calculate the ratio: [{{normalized power(second harmonic)}} / {{normalized power (first harmonic)}}].\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.125\nB. 1.5\nC. 0.75\nD. 0.5\nE. 2\nF. 4\nG. 0.6\nH. 1\nI. 0.25\nJ. 3\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The normalized power of a periodic signal is given by $S = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} V_n V_n^*$, where $V_n = \\frac{1}{|n|} e^{-j \\arctan(n/2)}$ for $n = \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\dots$.\nThe normalized power of the second harmonic corresponds to $n=2$. Thus, $V_2 = \\frac{1}{|2|} e^{-j \\arctan(2/2)} = \\frac{1}{2} e^{-j \\arctan(1)} = \\frac{1}{2} e^{-j\\pi/4}$.\nThe normalized power of the second harmonic is $|V_2|^2 = (\\frac{1}{2} e^{-j\\pi/4})(\\frac{1}{2} e^{j\\pi/4}) = (\\frac{1}{2})^2 = \\frac{1}{4} = 0.25$.\n\nThe normalized power of the first harmonic corresponds to $n=1$. Thus, $V_1 = \\frac{1}{|1|} e^{-j \\arctan(1/2)} = e^{-j \\arctan(1/2)}$.\nThe normalized power of the first harmonic is $|V_1|^2 = (e^{-j \\arctan(1/2)})(e^{j \\arctan(1/2)}) = 1$.\n\nThe ratio of the normalized power of the second harmonic to the normalized power of the first harmonic is $\\frac{|V_2|^2}{|V_1|^2} = \\frac{0.25}{1} = 0.25$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
      "success": true,
