    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10872",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An input signal v(t) =I\\delta(t) (impulse function) is passed through a filter having function (1 - e-j\\omega\\tau)/j\\omegawhere \\tau is the stretchedumpulsewidth. Determine the output of the filter.\n\nOptions: \nA. v_o(t) = I [δ(t) * e^(-t/τ)]\nB. v_o(t) = I [e^(-jωt) - u(t - τ)]\nC. v_o(t) = I [1 - e^(-jωτ)]\nD. v_o(t) = I [u(t) / (u(t) + u(t - τ))]\nE. v_o(t) = I [u(t) / u(t - \tau)]\nF. v_o(t) = I [u(t) + u(t - \tau)]\nG. v_o(t) = I [u(t) * u(t - \tau)]\nH. v_o(t) = I [u(t) - u(t - \tau)]\nI. v_o(t) = I [δ(t) - e^(-t/τ)]\nJ. v_o(t) = I [sin(ωt) * u(t - τ)]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the output of a filter, we can use the convolution property in the time domain or multiplication in the frequency domain. The input signal is v(t) = Iδ(t). The transfer function of the filter is H(jω) = (1 - e^(-jωτ)) / (jω).\n\nIn the frequency domain, the output is V_o(jω) = V(jω) * H(jω), where V(jω) is the Fourier Transform of v(t), and '*' denotes multiplication.\nThe Fourier Transform of the impulse function δ(t) is a constant, which can be represented in a frequency-dependent manner. If we consider the input as a scaled impulse with amplitude I, its Fourier Transform can be considered as I.\n\nThus, V_o(jω) = I * [(1 - e^(-jωτ)) / (jω)].\nWe know that the Fourier Transform of the unit step function u(t) is 1/(jω) + πδ(ω).\nAlso, the Fourier Transform of u(t) - u(t-τ) is (1/(jω) - e^(-jωτ)/(jω)) = (1 - e^(-jωτ))/(jω).\n\nTherefore, H(jω) is the Fourier Transform of the function u(t) - u(t-τ).\nIn the time domain, the output v_o(t) is the convolution of the input signal v(t) with the impulse response of the filter h(t).\nh(t) = inverse Fourier Transform of H(jω) = u(t) - u(t-τ).\n\nThe output v_o(t) = v(t) * h(t) = Iδ(t) * [u(t) - u(t-τ)].\nThe convolution of an impulse function δ(t) with any function f(t) is f(t).\nSo, v_o(t) = I * [u(t) - u(t-τ)].\n\nNow let's check the given options:\nOptions A, B, C, I, and J involve impulse functions in the time domain or exponential terms in the frequency domain which do not directly match our derivation.\nOptions D, E, F, and G involve ratios or sums of step functions in a way that doesn't align with the convolution result.\n\nOption H is v_o(t) = I [u(t) - u(t - τ)]. This directly matches our derived output.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{H}$.",
