    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10608",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If the speed of an airplane is measured by means of a stagnation tube, what correction must be applied to the constant-density indication when the plane is cruising at 300 mph?\n\nOptions: \nA. - 10 mph\nB. 25 mph\nC. -30 mph\nD. -15 mph\nE. - 5 mph\nF. 10 mph\nG. 5 mph\nH. 0 mph\nI. 15 mph\nJ. -20 mph\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks about a correction to the indicated airspeed of an airplane when cruising at 300 mph. A stagnation tube measures total pressure. The indicated airspeed from a stagnation tube is actually dynamic pressure converted to a speed. At higher speeds, air compressibility becomes significant, leading to a difference between indicated airspeed and true airspeed. This difference requires a compressibility correction. While the exact value of the correction isn't directly calculable without more information (like altitude and air temperature), 300 mph is a speed where such a correction is noticeable and typically negative, meaning the indicated airspeed will be slightly higher than the true airspeed. Among the given options, a correction of -15 mph is a plausible magnitude for a compressibility correction at this speed in standard atmospheric conditions.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{D}$.",
