  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10542",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A communication link between two (\\lambda/2) dipole antennas is establishedwith a free space environment. If the transmitter delivers1 (kW) of power to the transmitting antenna, how muchpower will be received by a receiver connected to the receivingdipole 500(km) from the transmitter if the frequency is200(MHz) ? Assume that the path between dipoles is normalto each dipole; i.e., \\texttheta = 90°, and the dipoles are aligned.\n\nOptions: \nA. 7.85 × 10^-10 W\nB. 3.21 × 10^-11 W\nC. 1.53 × 10^-10 W\nD. 1.03 × 10^-10 W\nE. 5.67 × 10^-11 W\nF. 1.64 × 10^-10 W\nG. 2.05 × 10^-9 W\nH. 4.28 × 10^-11 W\nI. 9.42 × 10^-10 W\nJ. 2.53 × 10^-10 W\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The received power can be calculated using the Friis transmission equation, adjusted for the antennas involved. For two identical dipole antennas, the effective aperture of each antenna needs to be considered. The calculation involves the transmitted power, the gain of the transmitting antenna, the wavelength, the distance between antennas, and the effective aperture of the receiving antenna.\n\nThe gain of a $\\lambda/2$ dipole antenna in its maximum radiation direction is approximately 1.64 (or 2.15 dBi).\nThe effective aperture ($A_e$) of a $\\lambda/2$ dipole is given by $A_e = \\frac{3\\lambda^2}{8\\pi}$.\nThe wavelength $(\\lambda)$ at 200 MHz is $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{f} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m/s}}{200 \\times 10^6 \\text{ Hz}} = 1.5 \\text{ m}$.\nThe effective aperture is then $A_e = \\frac{3 \\times (1.5 \\text{ m})^2}{8\\pi} \\approx 0.0803 \\text{ m}^2$.\n\nThe maximum gain of a $\\lambda/2$ dipole is $G_{max} = 1.64$.\nThe Friis transmission equation for received power ($P_r$) is:\n$P_r = P_t \\times G_t \\times G_r \\times \\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\pi R}\\right)^2$\nHowever, when considering effective aperture, a more general form of the Friis equation is:\n$P_r = \\frac{P_t A_{et} A_{er}}{(\\lambda R)^2}$\nwhere $A_{et}$ and $A_{er}$ are the effective apertures of the transmitting and receiving antennas, respectively. For identical antennas, $A_{et} = A_{er} = A_e$.\nSo, $P_r = P_t \\frac{A_e^2}{(\\lambda R)^2}$.\n\nGiven:\n$P_t = 1 \\text{ kW} = 1000 \\text{ W}$\n$R = 500 \\text{ km} = 500 \\times 10^3 \\text{ m}$\n$\\lambda = 1.5 \\text{ m}$\n$A_e = 0.0803 \\text{ m}^2$\n\n$P_r = 1000 \\text{ W} \\times \\frac{(0.0803 \\text{ m}^2)^2}{(1.5 \\text{ m} \\times 500 \\times 10^3 \\text{ m})^2}$\n$P_r = 1000 \\times \\frac{0.006448}{ (750 \\times 10^3)^2 }$\n$P_r = 1000 \\times \\frac{0.006448}{ 5.625 \\times 10^{11} }$\n$P_r = 1000 \\times 1.146 \\times 10^{-14}$\n$P_r = 1.146 \\times 10^{-11} \\text{ W}$\n\nLet's re-evaluate using the gain of the dipole directly, as the $\\theta=90^\\circ$ confirms maximum radiation.\nThe Friis transmission equation is:\n$P_r = P_t G_t G_r \\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\pi R}\\right)^2$\nHere, $G_t = G_r = 1.64$ for a $\\lambda/2$ dipole at $\\theta=90^\\circ$.\n$\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\pi R} = \\frac{1.5 \\text{ m}}{4\\pi \\times 500 \\times 10^3 \\text{ m}} = \\frac{1.5}{6.283 \\times 10^6} \\approx 0.2387 \\times 10^{-6}$\n$\\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\pi R}\\right)^2 \\approx (0.2387 \\times 10^{-6})^2 \\approx 0.057 \\times 10^{-12} = 5.7 \\times 10^{-14}$\n\n$P_r = 1000 \\text{ W} \\times 1.64 \\times 1.64 \\times (5.7 \\times 10^{-14})$\n$P_r = 1000 \\times 2.6896 \\times 5.7 \\times 10^{-14}$\n$P_r = 1000 \\times 15.33 \\times 10^{-14}$\n$P_r = 1.533 \\times 10^{-10} \\text{ W}$\n\nComparing this with the options:\nA. 7.85 × 10^-10 W\nB. 3.21 × 10^-11 W\nC. 1.53 × 10^-10 W\nD. 1.03 × 10^-10 W\nE. 5.67 × 10^-11 W\nF. 1.64 × 10^-10 W\nG. 2.05 × 10^-9 W\nH. 4.28 × 10^-11 W\nI. 9.42 × 10^-10 W\nJ. 2.53 × 10^-10 W\n\nThe calculated value of $1.533 \\times 10^{-10}$ W matches option C.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 324,
        "output_tokens": 1321,
        "total_tokens": 1645
