    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10943",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For a single tone-modulated signal m(t) =cos\\omega_mt Find the demodulated single sideband outputg(t).\n\nOptions: \nA. y(t) = (Ar)cos(2ω_mt±Δφ)\nB. y(t) = (Ar/ 3)cos(ω_mt±3Δφ)\nC. y(t) = (Ar/ 4)cos(ω_mt±Δφ)\nD. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)cos(ω_mt±Δφ/2)\nE. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)sin(ω_mt)\nF. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)sin(2ω_mt±Δφ/2)\nG. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)cos(ω_mt±Δφ)\nH. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)sin(ω_mt±Δφ)\nI. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)cos(ω_mt)\nJ. y(t) = (Ar)cos(ω_mt±Δφ/3)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To demodulate a single sideband (SSB) signal, you typically multiply the received signal by a locally generated carrier and then low-pass filter the result. The original message signal $m(t) = \\cos(\\omega_m t)$ is assumed to be modulated into an SSB format. The demodulated output of an SSB signal, when properly synchronized, should ideally recover the original message signal. Therefore, the demodulated output $g(t)$ should be proportional to the original message signal $m(t)$.\n\nGiven that $m(t) = \\cos(\\omega_m t)$, a correctly demodulated output that recovers the original message would be of the form $K \\cos(\\omega_m t)$, where K is some proportionality constant. Looking at the options, option I, $y(t) = (Ar/2)\\cos(\\omega_m t)$, fits this form. The other options involve frequencies other than $\\omega_m$, phases that are not directly related to the original message, or trigonometric functions like sine which would imply a phase shift not present in the original cosine signal.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
