    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The problem provides the maximum and minimum frequencies of an FM signal and the modulating frequency. We need to calculate the carrier frequency ($f_c$), carrier swing, frequency deviation ($\\Delta f$), and modulation index ($\\beta$).\n\nThe carrier frequency is the average of the maximum and minimum frequencies:\n$f_c = (f_{max} + f_{min}) / 2 = (100.02 \\text{ MHz} + 99.98 \\text{ MHz}) / 2 = 200 \\text{ MHz} / 2 = 100 \\text{ MHz}$.\n\nThe carrier swing is the difference between the maximum and minimum frequencies:\nCarrier Swing $= f_{max} - f_{min} = 100.02 \\text{ MHz} - 99.98 \\text{ MHz} = 0.04 \\text{ MHz} = 40 \\text{ kHz}$.\n\nThe frequency deviation ($\\Delta f$) is half of the carrier swing:\n$\\Delta f = (\\text{Carrier Swing}) / 2 = 40 \\text{ kHz} / 2 = 20 \\text{ kHz}$.\n\nThe modulation index ($\\beta$) is the ratio of the frequency deviation to the modulating frequency ($f_m$):\n$\\beta = \\Delta f / f_m$.\nThe modulating frequency is given as 3 kHz.\n$\\beta = 20 \\text{ kHz} / 3 \\text{ kHz} = 20 / 3 \\approx 6.667$.\n\nNow let's compare these calculated values with the given options:\n- Carrier frequency ($f_c$): 100 MHz\n- Carrier swing: 40 kHz\n- Frequency deviation ($\\Delta f$): 20 kHz\n- Modulation index ($\\beta$): 6.667\n\nLooking at the options:\nOption F matches all these values: $f_c=100\\text{MHz}$, carrier swing$=40\\text{ kHz}$, frequency deviation$=20\\text{ kHz}$, modulation index$=6.667$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
