    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10475",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If E = 5 volts/meter is the amplitude of an incident plane wave which is reflected at normal incidence from a boundary surface and E_1 is the amplitude of the reflected wave, find: (a) Under what conditions is there a pure standing wave? (b) The standing wave ratio when E_1 = 1/10 volt/meter. (c) The value ofE_maxandE_minwhen E_1 = 3/5 volt/meter.\n\nOptions: \nA. (a) E_1 = - 3 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 1.5, (c) E_max = 6.5 volts, E_min = 3.5 volts\nB. (a) E_1 = - 2 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 1.25, (c) E_max = 6 volts, E_min = 4 volts\nC. (a) E_1 = - 5 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 1.04, (c) E_max= 5.6 volts, E_min = 4.4 volts\nD. (a) E_1 = - 6 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 0.8, (c) E_max= 5.5 volts, E_min = 4.5 volts\nE. (a) E_1 = 5 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 1.1, (c) E_max = 8 volts, E_min = 2 volts\nF. (a) E_1 = - 4 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 1.2, (c) E_max= 5.8 volts, E_min = 4.2 volts\nG. (a) E_1 = 5 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 0.9, (c) E_max= 5.3 volts, E_min = 4.6 volts\nH. (a) E_1 = 0 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 1, (c) E_max = 5 volts, E_min = 5 volts\nI. (a) E_1 = - 1 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 2, (c) E_max = 7 volts, E_min = 3 volts\nJ. (a) E_1 = 4 volt/meter, (b) VSWR = 0.75, (c) E_max = 4.5 volts, E_min = 5.5 volts\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let E be the amplitude of the incident wave, E = 5 volts/meter.\nLet E_1 be the amplitude of the reflected wave.\n\n(a) Under what conditions is there a pure standing wave?\nA pure standing wave is formed when the incident wave is completely reflected, meaning the magnitude of the reflected wave amplitude is equal to the magnitude of the incident wave amplitude, and there is a phase shift of 180 degrees (or pi radians) upon reflection if the reflection occurs from a perfect conductor. So, for a pure standing wave, E_1 = -E.\nTherefore, E_1 = -5 volts/meter.\n\n(b) The standing wave ratio (VSWR) when E_1 = 1/10 volt/meter.\nThe standing wave ratio (VSWR) is given by the formula:\nVSWR = (E_max) / (E_min) = (E + |E_1|) / (E - |E_1|)\nGiven E = 5 volts/meter and E_1 = 1/10 = 0.1 volt/meter.\nVSWR = (5 + 0.1) / (5 - 0.1) = 5.1 / 4.9\nVSWR ≈ 1.0408 ≈ 1.04\n\n(c) The value of E_max and E_min when E_1 = 3/5 volt/meter.\nGiven E = 5 volts/meter and E_1 = 3/5 = 0.6 volt/meter.\nE_max = E + |E_1| = 5 + 0.6 = 5.6 volts\nE_min = E - |E_1| = 5 - 0.6 = 4.4 volts\n\nComparing our calculated values with the given options:\n(a) E_1 = -5 volt/meter\n(b) VSWR = 1.04\n(c) E_max = 5.6 volts, E_min = 4.4 volts\n\nThis matches option C.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$",
