    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10061",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Methane reacts with water vapor at 25°C by the following reaction : CH_4(g) + H_2O(g) \\rightleftharpoons CO(g) + 3H_2(g) Using the following data, calculate the standard Gibbs free-energychange for this reaction. Data \\DeltaH_298^0(kcal mole^-1) \\DeltaS_298^0(cal deg^-1 mole^-1) CH_4(g) - 17.89 44.5 CO(g) - 26.40 47.3 H_2(g) 0 31.2 H_2O(g) - 57.80 45.1\n\nOptions: \nA. 34 kcal/mole\nB. 49,290 kcal/mole\nC. -10 kcal/mole\nD. 24.2 kcal/mole\nE. 60 kcal/mole\nF. 10,000 kcal/mole\nG. 15,280 kcal/mole\nH. 75.5 kcal/mole\nI. 51.3 kcal/mole\nJ. -20 kcal/mole\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The standard Gibbs free-energy change ($\\Delta G^0$) can be calculated using the Gibbs-Helmholtz equation: $\\Delta G^0 = \\Delta H^0 - T\\Delta S^0$.\n\nFirst, we need to calculate the standard enthalpy change ($\\Delta H^0$) and the standard entropy change ($\\Delta S^0$) for the reaction.\nFor the reaction: $\\text{CH}_4\\text{(g)} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{CO(g)} + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{(g)}$\n\n$\\Delta H^0_{\\text{reaction}} = \\sum (\\text{moles} \\times \\Delta H^0_f(\\text{products})) - \\sum (\\text{moles} \\times \\Delta H^0_f(\\text{reactants}))$\n$\\Delta H^0_{\\text{reaction}} = (1 \\times \\Delta H^0_f(\\text{CO(g)}) + 3 \\times \\Delta H^0_f(\\text{H}_2\\text{(g)})) - (1 \\times \\Delta H^0_f(\\text{CH}_4\\text{(g)}) + 1 \\times \\Delta H^0_f(\\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)}))$\n$\\Delta H^0_{\\text{reaction}} = (1 \\times (-26.40 \\text{ kcal/mole}) + 3 \\times 0 \\text{ kcal/mole}) - (1 \\times (-17.89 \\text{ kcal/mole}) + 1 \\times (-57.80 \\text{ kcal/mole}))$\n$\\Delta H^0_{\\text{reaction}} = (-26.40) - (-17.89 - 57.80)$\n$\\Delta H^0_{\\text{reaction}} = -26.40 - (-75.69)$\n$\\Delta H^0_{\\text{reaction}} = -26.40 + 75.69$\n$\\Delta H^0_{\\text{reaction}} = 49.29 \\text{ kcal/mole}$\n\n$\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}} = \\sum (\\text{moles} \\times \\Delta S^0(\\text{products})) - \\sum (\\text{moles} \\times \\Delta S^0(\\text{reactants}))$\n$\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}} = (1 \\times \\Delta S^0(\\text{CO(g)}) + 3 \\times \\Delta S^0(\\text{H}_2\\text{(g)})) - (1 \\times \\Delta S^0(\\text{CH}_4\\text{(g)}) + 1 \\times \\Delta S^0(\\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)}))$\n$\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}} = (1 \\times 47.3 \\text{ cal/deg/mole} + 3 \\times 31.2 \\text{ cal/deg/mole}) - (1 \\times 44.5 \\text{ cal/deg/mole} + 1 \\times 45.1 \\text{ cal/deg/mole})$\n$\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}} = (47.3 + 93.6) - (44.5 + 45.1)$\n$\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}} = 140.9 - 89.6$\n$\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}} = 51.3 \\text{ cal/deg/mole}$\n\nNow, we need to convert $\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}}$ to kcal/deg/mole by dividing by 1000:\n$\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}} = 51.3 \\text{ cal/deg/mole} / 1000 \\text{ cal/kcal} = 0.0513 \\text{ kcal/deg/mole}$\n\nThe temperature is given as 25°C, which needs to be converted to Kelvin:\n$T = 25^\\circ\\text{C} + 273.15 = 298.15 \\text{ K}$\n\nNow, calculate $\\Delta G^0$:\n$\\Delta G^0 = \\Delta H^0_{\\text{reaction}} - T\\Delta S^0_{\\text{reaction}}$\n$\\Delta G^0 = 49.29 \\text{ kcal/mole} - (298.15 \\text{ K} \\times 0.0513 \\text{ kcal/deg/mole})$\n$\\Delta G^0 = 49.29 \\text{ kcal/mole} - 15.30 \\text{ kcal/mole}$\n$\\Delta G^0 = 33.99 \\text{ kcal/mole}$\n\nThis value is closest to 34 kcal/mole.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 335,
        "output_tokens": 1115,
        "total_tokens": 1450
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
