int.\n\nLet's search for information confirming the relative prominence of these aspects in descriptions of TEF. Many sources detail the macronutrient effect and the neural/hormonal contributions (including sympathetic activity).\n\nUpon reflection and considering the nature of multiple-choice questions in biology/physiology, option E highlights a physiological mechanism more than option B, which states a direct relationship to amount. While both are true, mechanisms are often the focus of detailed physiological questions.\n\nHowever, if we consider the *most direct* and undeniable aspect of TEF, it is its dependence on the *input*, which is the food consumed, both in type and amount.\n\nLet's step back and imagine a scenario where one is more fundamentally correct or comprehensive in some way.\n\nConsider the possibility of a trick in the wording.\n\"directly related to the amount of food consumed\" - this is a correlation.\n\"partly related to sympathetic activity stimulation in the postprandial phase\" - this describes a mechanism.\n\nLet's review the common understanding of TEF. The energy cost of processing food. This cost is proportional to the amount of energy in the food, and also how the body processes it.\n\nRe-evaluating: Many sources emphasize that TEF is significant for protein > carbs > fat. This implies the *type* matters. And the *amount* of food determines the *magnitude* of this effect.\n\nLet's reconsider all options and see if there's a clearer best fit.\n\nIf we consider the most fundamental aspect of what TEF *is*, it's the energy used to handle food. The amount of food is a direct determinant of how much handling is needed.\n\nLet's assume B is the intended answer due to its direct and fundamental relationship with energy expenditure and intake.\n\nLet's consider an argument for E. Sympathetic activity *causes* an increase in metabolic rate, which contributes to TEF. So it's a mechanism.\n\nHowever, if you eat zero food, TEF is zero. If you ea