    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The World Systems Theory categorizes countries into core, periphery, and semi-periphery based on their economic and political power. Semi-periphery countries are those that are in-between core and periphery, exhibiting characteristics of both. They are not fully industrialized like core nations but are more developed than periphery nations.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. Small percentage of very high class individuals: This can be true for any country, not specific to semi-periphery.\nB. Highly industrialized and technologically advanced nations: This describes core countries.\nC. Countries with a high rate of population growth: This is often characteristic of periphery countries.\nD. Strong central government: While important, this is not the defining characteristic of semi-periphery.\nE. High rates of urbanization: This can be found in both core and semi-periphery countries as they industrialize.\nF. Economies dependent on foreign aid: This is more characteristic of periphery countries.\nG. Low income, agrarian societies: This describes periphery countries.\nH. Countries with no natural resources: This is not a defining characteristic, and many countries have natural resources but still fall into different categories.\nI. A diversified and developed economy: This best describes semi-periphery countries. They have some industrialization and a more developed economy than periphery nations, though not as dominant as core nations. Semi-periphery countries often export both manufactured goods and raw materials, showing a degree of economic diversification.\nJ. An economy centralized around one natural resource: This is highly characteristic of periphery countries.\n\nTherefore, a diversified and developed economy best correlates with semi-periphery countries, as they are in a transitional stage between core and periphery. They often have a mix of industrial and agricultural sectors, and their economies are more complex than purely agrarian societies but not as dominant as core economies.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{I}$.",
