    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5046",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: One line of evidence against antagonistic pleiotropy is that\n\nOptions: \nA. Exercise increases rather than decreases longevity\nB. Senescence can also be observed in cells with short telomere strands\nC. Women who have more children tend to live longer\nD. Certain species have shown no signs of aging\nE. The genetic mutation rate increases with age\nF. Even tissue cells with longer telomere strands can show senescence\nG. Senescence is observed in all living organisms\nH. Caloric restriction extends lifespan in many species\nI. Some animals show little or no senescence\nJ. Organisms with fewer offspring have a longer lifespan\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks for evidence *against* antagonistic pleiotropy. Antagonistic pleiotropy suggests that genes that are beneficial early in life (promoting reproduction) can have detrimental effects later in life (leading to aging).\n\nLet's analyze the options:\nA. Exercise increases rather than decreases longevity: This would be evidence *for* genes that are beneficial late in life, which could be seen as contradicting antagonistic pleiotropy if those genes were previously thought to be detrimental.\nB. Senescence can also be observed in cells with short telomere strands: Telomere shortening is a known aging mechanism, so this observation is consistent with aging processes, not necessarily against antagonistic pleiotropy.\nC. Women who have more children tend to live longer: This contradicts the idea that genes favoring early reproduction (more children) lead to decreased longevity. If having more children is associated with longer life, it weakens the antagonistic pleiotropy hypothesis that genes promoting high reproduction at a cost to late-life survival.\nD. Certain species have shown no signs of aging: This challenges the universality of aging, but doesn't directly refute the mechanism of antagonistic pleiotropy where it does occur.\nE. The genetic mutation rate increases with age: This is an observation about aging, not necessarily evidence against antagonistic pleiotropy. Increased mutations could be a *cause* or *consequence* of aging mechanisms.\nF. Even tissue cells with longer telomere strands can show senescence: Similar to B, this relates to telomeres and senescence, not directly opposing antagonistic pleiotropy.\nG. Senescence is observed in all living organisms: This statement is generally false, as some organisms exhibit negligible senescence. Even if true, it doesn't directly refute antagonistic pleiotropy.\nH. Caloric restriction extends lifespan in many species: This is an intervention that affects lifespan, but doesn't directly speak against the genetic basis of lifespan as proposed by antagonistic pleiotropy.\nI. Some animals show little or no senescence: This is similar to D and challenges the universality of aging, but not the mechanism itself where aging occurs.\nJ. Organisms with fewer offspring have a longer lifespan: This observation is *consistent* with antagonistic pleiotropy, as it suggests that traits favoring early reproduction (many offspring) come at the cost of lifespan.\n\nOption C directly challenges the core prediction of antagonistic pleiotropy by showing a positive correlation between a trait associated with early reproduction (having more children) and longevity. This suggests that genes promoting higher reproductive output might not necessarily be detrimental to females later in life, or that other factors are at play that decouple reproductive effort from reduced lifespan.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$.",
