    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Achondroplasia is caused by mutations in the FGFR3 gene. These mutations lead to an overactive receptor, which in turn inhibits chondrocyte proliferation and differentiation, resulting in dwarfism. Therefore, the mutation confers a new, detrimental function to the protein.\n\nThe most likely correct answer is **F. gain-of-function**. This is because the mutations in achondroplasia don't simply inactivate the gene product; they cause it to be constitutively active, leading to an abnormal and harmful function. Options like \"loss-of-function\" are therefore incorrect. Achondroplasia is also a dominant condition, meaning having one copy of the mutated gene is sufficient to cause the disorder, ruling out purely \"recessive\" classifications. While some of the options are related to gene dosage (haploinsufficiency/sufficiency), the core issue in achondroplasia is the altered function of the protein itself, not an insufficient amount of functional protein.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
