    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5202",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is true about an abdominal mass?\n\nOptions: \nA. An indentible mass is caused by faecal loading of the colon\nB. A non-tender mass is always indicative of a benign tumor\nC. A hard mass is always a symptom of colorectal cancer\nD. An enlarged kidney is dull to percussion\nE. A movable mass is a definite sign of a hernia\nF. A large mass arising out of the pelvis which disappears following urethral catheterization is caused by an ovarian cyst\nG. A mass that changes size is always due to a bladder infection\nH. A mass that disappears after eating is caused by a stomach ulcer\nI. A pulsatile mass is always due to an aortic aneurysm\nJ. A painful mass is always a symptom of gallstones\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze each option:\n\nA. An indentible mass is indeed often caused by fecal impaction in the colon. This is a common finding, especially in the elderly or those with motility issues.\n\nB. A non-tender mass is not *always* benign; malignancy can present without tenderness.\n\nC. A hard mass is *not always* colorectal cancer; other hardened masses exist.\n\nD. An enlarged kidney can be tender or dull to percussion depending on the cause and size.\n\nE. Mobility is a characteristic of some hernias, but other movable masses exist and not all hernias are easily palpable or movable.\n\nF. While an ovarian cyst could be the cause, a mass disappearing after catheterization suggests a bladder origin.\n\nG. A mass that changes size can be due to many things, not just a bladder infection.\n\nH. A mass disappearing after eating is not indicative of a stomach ulcer.\n\nI. A pulsatile mass strongly suggests an aortic aneurysm, but other vascular abnormalities could also be pulsatile.\n\nJ. A painful mass is not *always* gallstones; many conditions can cause painful abdominal masses.\n\nBased on the typical presentations of abdominal masses, option A is the most consistently true.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{A}$.",
