    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5473",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The pancreas secretes a large amount of digestive enzymes after a meal. Which statement about this process is correct?\n\n\nOptions: \nA. Bile is needed to activate all pancreatic enzymes\nB. All pancreatic enzymes are produced in an active form and can start digestion immediately\nC. Enzymes are produced in the pancreas but released in the stomach for digestion\nD. Pepsinogen is the chief enzyme which digests protein and requires removal of a peptide sequence from the active cleft before it can work\nE. Pancreatic secretion of hydrochloric acid is required to provide the correct pH for maximal pancreatic enzyme in the duodenum\nF. Enteropeptidase converts trypsinogen to trypsin by cleavage of a peptide sequence that blocks the active site of trypsin.\nG. Pancreatic enzymes are needed to break down carbohydrates only.\nH. Pancreatic á-amylase will hydrolyse starch to free glucose\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The question asks about the secretion of digestive enzymes by the pancreas after a meal. Let's analyze each option:\n\nA. Bile is produced by the liver and stored in the gallbladder. It aids in fat digestion by emulsification but does not activate all pancreatic enzymes.\n\nB. Many pancreatic enzymes are synthesized in inactive precursor forms (zymogens) to prevent self-digestion within the pancreas.\n\nC. Pancreatic enzymes are secreted directly into the duodenum, not the stomach.\n\nD. Pepsinogen is a gastric enzyme produced in the stomach, not the pancreas.\n\nE. The pancreas secretes bicarbonate, not hydrochloric acid, to neutralize stomach acid and provide the optimal pH for pancreatic enzymes in the duodenum.\n\nF. Enteropeptidase, an enzyme found in the brush border of the duodenum, is responsible for activating trypsinogen (an inactive pancreatic zymogen) into trypsin. This activation involves cleaving a peptide sequence that blocks the active site. Trypsin then activates other pancreatic proteases. This statement is consistent with the known physiology of pancreatic enzyme activation.\n\nG. Pancreatic enzymes break down carbohydrates (amylase), proteins (proteases like trypsin and chymotrypsin), and fats (lipase). This statement is incorrect as it limits the function to carbohydrates only.\n\nH. Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes starch into smaller polysaccharides, disaccharides (like maltose), and trisaccharides, not directly into free glucose.\n\nTherefore, option F is the most accurate statement.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{F}$.",
