    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_4921",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A segment of DNA from a lab mouse is determined to be 5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’. Which of the following DNA segments would be the result of this original DNA sequence experiencing both a point mutation and a deletion?\n\nOptions: \nA. 5' - GGATCCTCA - 3'\nB. 5’ – TGATCCCAG – 3’\nC. 5’ – GGATCCATG – 3’\nD. 5' - GGATCCTCGT - 3'\nE. 5’ – GGTCCTCATC – 3’\nF. 5' - GGATCCGCATG - 3'\nG. 5' - GGATCTCATG - 3'\nH. 5' - GGATCCTCAT - 3'\nI. 5' - GGATCCTCAG - 3'\nJ. 5’ – GCATCCTCATG – 3’\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The original DNA sequence is 5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’.\nWe are looking for a sequence that has undergone both a point mutation and a deletion.\nA point mutation is a change in a single nucleotide.\nA deletion is the removal of one or more nucleotides.\n\nLet's analyze each option:\n\nA. 5' - GGATCCTCA - 3'\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has lost 'TG' at the end. This is a deletion, but no point mutation.\n\nB. 5’ – TGATCCCAG – 3’\nThis sequence has significant changes and a different length. More than just a point mutation and a deletion.\n\nC. 5’ – GGATCCATG – 3’\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has lost 'TC' in the middle. This is a deletion, but no point mutation.\n\nD. 5' - GGATCCTCGT - 3'\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has a point mutation (A -> G) and a deletion of 'G' at the end, but also a 'T' added. This is more complex than just a point mutation and a deletion.\n\nE. 5’ – GGTCCTCATC – 3’\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has a point mutation (A -> T) and a deletion of 'G' and a point mutation (A -> C) at the end. This has multiple point mutations and a deletion.\n\nF. 5' - GGATCCGCATG - 3'\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has an insertion of 'G' after 'CCT', and a point mutation (A -> G). This is an insertion and a point mutation, not a deletion.\n\nG. 5' - GGATCTCATG - 3'\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has lost a 'C' (deletion) and the 'C' after the 'T' has become a 'T' (point mutation).\nOriginal: 5’ – GGA TCC TCA TG – 3’\nOption G: 5’ – GGA T CTCA TG – 3’\nThe second C is deleted and the A is changed to T. This has a deletion and a point mutation.\nLet's check again:\nOriginal: 5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’\nDeletion of the second 'C': 5’ – GGATTCTCATG – 3’\nNow apply a point mutation. For example, changing the 'A' to a 'T' would give 5' - GGATTCTCATG - 3'.\nIf we consider the original 5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’ and option G: 5' - GGATCTCATG - 3'.\nWe can see that the sequence 'CC' has become 'TC'. This could be a deletion of one 'C' and a point mutation of the other 'C' to 'T'. Or, more simply, a deletion of 'C' and a point mutation of the adjacent 'C' to 'T'.\nAlternatively, a deletion of the 'C' at position 5 (GGAT**C**CTCATG) resulting in GGATTCTCATG.\nAnd then a point mutation 'C' to 'T' at position 6 (GGATC**C**TCATG) resulting in GGATCTCATG. This seems plausible.\n\nH. 5' - GGATCCTCAT - 3'\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has lost 'G' at the end. This is a deletion, but no point mutation.\n\nI. 5' - GGATCCTCAG - 3'\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has a point mutation (T -> G) and a deletion of 'G' at the end. However, it's losing a 'G' at the end of the original sequence. The change from 'TG' to 'G' means a deletion of 'T' and then the final 'G' remains. Let's re-examine if this is a point mutation and deletion.\nOriginal: 5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’\nOption I: 5' - GGATCCTCAG - 3'\nHere, the 'T' in 'TG' at the end of the original sequence has been deleted, and the 'G' has remained. Then, the 'A' before 'TG' has been mutated to 'G'. This could be a point mutation (A to G) and a deletion of T. Yes, this fits.\n\nJ. 5’ – GCATCCTCATG – 3’\nCompared to the original (5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’), this sequence has a point mutation (G -> C) at the second position. This is only a point mutation, no deletion.\n\nLet's re-evaluate option G and I.\n\nOption G: 5' - GGATCTCATG - 3'\nOriginal: 5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’\nIf we delete the second C: 5’ – GGATCTCATG – 3’. This is just a deletion, not a point mutation. So G is incorrect.\n\nOption I: 5' - GGATCCTCAG - 3'\nOriginal: 5’ – GGATCCTCATG – 3’\nLet's apply a point mutation first. Change the third 'T' to 'C': 5’ – GGATCCTCACG – 3’. Then delete the 'G' at the end: 5’ – GGATCCTCAC – 3’. This doesn't match.\n\nLet's