    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5064",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 77-year-old female presents to the office for evaluation of a syncopal event that occurred while she was walking. Cardiac examination reveals a grade 3/6 systolic murmur, heard best at the right upper sternal border. There is radiation of the murmur into the neck. The valvular abnormality that most likely caused this episode is\n\nOptions: \nA. bicuspid aortic valve\nB. aortic insufficiency\nC. mitral valve prolapse\nD. tricuspid stenosis\nE. mitral regurgitation\nF. pulmonary regurgitation\nG. aortic stenosis\nH. pulmonary stenosis\nI. tricuspid regurgitation\nJ. mitral stenosis\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The patient's age and syncopal event, combined with a grade 3/6 systolic murmur heard best at the right upper sternal border with radiation into the neck, are classic findings for aortic stenosis. This condition obstructs blood flow from the left ventricle to the aorta, leading to reduced cardiac output, decreased cerebral perfusion, and syncope. Bicuspid aortic valve can predispose to aortic stenosis, but it is the stenosis itself that causes the symptoms. Mitral valve prolapse, mitral regurgitation, and mitral stenosis typically cause murmurs at the apex. Tricuspid and pulmonary valve abnormalities are usually heard at the left sternal border.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{G}$.",
