    "global index": "MedMCQA_2029",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The concentration of a negative ion is 100 mMoUL extracellular and 10 mMo1/1 intracellular. What will be the Nernst potential for this ion?\n\nOptions: \nA. --10 mV\nB. +10 mV\nC. --61 mV\nD. +61 mV\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-2.5-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Nernst potential for a negative ion can be calculated using the Nernst equation:\n\nE = (RT/zF) * ln([ion_out]/[ion_in])\n\nAt body temperature (37°C), RT/F is approximately 61.5 mV. For a negative ion with a charge of -1 (z = -1), the equation becomes:\n\nE = -61.5 * log10([ion_out]/[ion_in])\n\nGiven:\n[ion_out] = 100 mM\n[ion_in] = 10 mM\n\nE = -61.5 * log10(100/10)\nE = -61.5 * log10(10)\nE = -61.5 * 1\nE = -61.5 mV\n\nRounding to the nearest whole number, the Nernst potential is approximately -61 mV.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{C}$",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 128,
        "output_tokens": 216,
        "total_tokens": 344
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": null,
    "accuracy": null
  },
